      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>Agricultural Society Has Money</head>
        <p>Agricultural Society Has Money</p>
        <p>In Hand.</p>
        <p>SHOW MAY BE HELD</p>
        <p>THIS YEAR.</p>
        <p>Congratulations circulated in the Masonic Hall on Wednesday afternoon, when the War- rington and District Agricultural Society held its annual meeting, and it was intimated that for the first time in the history of the show the income had beaten the expenses by nearly .2100. Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., presided over a good attendance. The report of the Council was as follows "The twenty-sixth annual show was held under quite different circumstanCes` to those of ordinary- times. Owing to the war, it was feared that no show would be possible, but the mem- bers, desiring to keep their association together, were anxious to hold a modified exhibition just for the local shires and dairy cattle, which are such a feature of the district, and which, in latter years, have made the Warrington and District Show one of the best in the counties of Lancashire and Cheshire in these sections. "Sir Gilbert Greenall, the president, with his usual liberality on behalf of • Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co., placed the field at Wilderspool at their service, and also promised, as usual, to do the fitting up free, and to provide the special</p>
        <p>cups he has given for so many years. A Great Skteoess. "The undertaking was entered into with great spirit by everybody and proved a great success. There were seven classes for shires and seven for dairy cattle, and they were nearly as well filled as usual with most representative stock. Many good animals were exhibited, including the splendid filly foal belonging to Mr, Dan Massey, which was afterwards champion at Ash- bourne and other important shows. "The dairy cow and heifer clases,s had a new feature introduced, as the animals were milked /in the ring before the prizes were awarded. The I result was most gratifying, eight cows giving no less than 254,1 b. of milk. The heifers also gave good proof of maturing into heavy 'pail-fillers.' By -elle desire of the exhibitors of the animals in milk, the milk was sent down by Sir Gilbert Greenall and presented to the Workhouse, where it was much appreciated by the inmates.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>Section Eliminated.</head>
        <p>A Section Eliminated.</p>
        <p>Air</p>
        <p>"The light horse section of the breeding classes and the hunters in saddle, horses in harness and the tradesmen's turnouts were all eliminated. Notwithstanding this, the attend- ance was very good, and everybody was loud in their praise of the Council for having the cour- age to hold the show, as it was one of the very few one-day shows held in the two counties last year. "With regard to the finances, for the first time in the history of the show the income has • beaten the expenses by £9l Bs. sd. We are en- abled to show this good balance mainly owing to the magnificent donation from our Vice-Presi- dent, Mr. Roger C. Parr, of £5O, and from '.,t40, the secretary's salaec, whose duties have been carried out by Mr. W. Bainbridge on behalf of his son—our secretary—and for which lie desires to take no remuneration. The saving has, of course, mainly been on account of only about one-third of the usual 'prize-money being given, and the less cost of fitting up the showground, printing f • "Whatever may be possible to be done for next year, we are unable to foreshadow at pres- ent, but we feel thoroughly justified- in what we have done this year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>Generous Act.</head>
        <p>A Generous Act.</p>
        <p>"Considerable extra work was done-qn the secretarial department, as the President sent out a circular letter for this sociky and the Warrington Shire Horse Society on behalf of the 'Relief of Allies' Fund for the reinstate- ment of distressed farmers in the countries</p>
        <p>devastated by our enemies, and a generous re- sponse was given, no less a sum than ..P.,329 15s. Bd. being received up to date, which sum has been paid over to the fund. "The Council desire to thank the following gentlemen who kindly gave aups or special prizes :—Colonel Alan J. Sykes, M.P., Mr. F. Stapleton Bretherton, Mr. John Coxon, Colonel H. R. Armitage, Mr. G. Norris Midwood, Mr. J. W. Kenworthy, Mr. James Gould, and the War- rington Shill, Horse Society."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>President's Gift to Members.</head>
        <p>President's Gift to Members.</p>
        <p>In proposing that the report be adopted. Mr. Joseph Ball said he hoped all of them would do what .they could''to,enrol:new .menabers, and try to increase the subseriptions if possible. (Hear,</p>
        <p>hear.) ' Mr. J. Backhouse seconded, remarking that they had now made a start, and -they hoped to make a success of the show in the future by making it pay its own way. (Applause.) Mr. W. Weir proposed a vote of thanks to Mr. William Bainbridge, who is acting as secretary while his son, Captain Jas. Bainbridge, is unable to do so. "Although the show has been on a less scale than usual," he said, "a great deal of secretarial work had been involved." Mr. Mainwaring seconded, and the proposal was carried whole-heartedly. On Sir Gilbert Greenall being re-elected presi- dent, he said that he had been more than grati- fied with the success of the show. He could not say definitely what they would do this year, but, if possible, they would try to hold a show. (gear; ,hear.) As the Royal Show was coming to Manchester he was prepared to give a day ticket to every member of the society. . (Ap- .plause.) Mr. W. Peter Rylands was elected vice-presi- dent for the year. The result of the elections for the Council was:— .</p>
        <p>North-Eastern District: H. H. Ferguson (Warrington) and S. Carter (War- rington). North-Western: John Backhouse (Penketh) and Robt. Fairclough (Burtonwood). South-Eastern: James Whitlow (Crowley) and Edwin Clarke (Warburton). South-Western: John Whitlow (Preston Brook) and J. S. Priestlier (Acton Grange).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>Win</head>
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>FARM AND GARDEN.</head>
        <p>FARM AND GARDEN.</p>
        <p>It is suggested that the slopes of railway lines might be profitably employed for increasing crop production. They are cultivated near towns, but there is a large area in rural districts that might be utilised.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>WASPS IN JANUARY.</head>
        <p>WASPS IN JANUARY.</p>
        <p>"While cutting up a stack of hay," writes Mr. H. T. Sharpe, Horbling, Folkingham, Lin- colnshire, "I came across a quantity of wasps, queens included, and they were flying about just as if it- was the hettest day in summer." In an old outhouse at Warwick Castle Gardens last week twenty-two queen wasps were de- stroyed. It is rare to find so many together.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>ALLIED FARMERS' TROUBLES.</head>
        <p>ALLIED FARMERS' TROUBLES.</p>
        <p>Farm work on the small holdings in the rear of the fighting line in France has of late been greatly interfered with by extremely severe weather conditions, according to recent reports from the French agricultural districts. But with their indomitable perseverance the poor peasants are carrying on bravely, and are look- ing wistfully for the return of good weather and the renewal of the English farmers' expert help. The Agricultural Relief of Allies Committee has promised a consignment of seed oats for the early spring, and English farmers are co-operat- ing readily with offers of seeds in order that the gift shall be on a scale worthy both of the cause and of the agricultural community of this country. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>FAVOURABLE CONDITIONS.</head>
        <p>FAVOURABLE CONDITIONS.</p>
        <p>If the present breezy weather continues tillage and seed-sowing will short if be possible, says the "Times." The land does not dry so quickly in January as in March, however, and this, together with the uncertainty of the weather, makes it improbable that work on the ploughed fields will be practicable every day. Grassland is less exacting as to the conditions in which it can he harrowed and manured, and a rainfall that would drive teams from the arable fields might make old pastures and meadows amenable to surface cultivation and suitable for the re- ception of fertilisers. An excessive rainfall, as well as snow and frost, may be an effective hindrance on even the highest' and driest grass areas, but the strong winds of the past week will have' put great expanses of grassland in fit condi- tion for seasonable treatment, which takes both mechanical and manurial form. As Good as the War Loan.</p>
        <p>The treatment of grassland is an interesting study at all times, but especially so in the present circumstances. It is not suggested that farmers have made during the past year the huge profits which selling prices might imply, .but it can be said that money has been passing, freely through their hands, and in average cases there are • means, as well as inducements, to warrant a more generous expenditure than usual. Grassland farmers now have an excel- lent chance of investing capital that will pro- duce good economic results, while they will have the additional pleasure of knowing 'that if in- creasing the output of their holdings they will be doing a distinct service to the nation. Money wisely spent in improving grassland .is likely to be as serviceable to the country and the in- dividual as if it were invested in the War Loan.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>SERIOUS DECREASES IN FEEDING SHEEP.</head>
        <p>SERIOUS DECREASES IN FEEDING SHEEP.</p>
        <p>Up to the present time the offerings of sheep in the British fat markets may be said to have about met the demand, Says the "Darlington Times." Feeding sheep in the country last June showed an increase over 1914 of 7 per cent. The supply from this source is now nearly ex- hausted, and serious inroads have already been made upon ,the lambs born in 1915. According to the returns of the exportation of sheep car- cases from the Argentine, there has been a con- tinuous decrease from year to year, the decrease for 1915 compared with 1014 being more than half.</p>
        <p>BIRD PESTS. Many crops of sprouting wheat suffer badly from the attention of larks and rooks. It would be hard to say which do the most damage. The larks, by their number and their subtle ways, may spoil whole fields, and the careless farmer may not know what is going on, for the little brown birds are not noticeable save When on the wing. But rooks are seen plainly enough, and, , with their strong bills, have liower to uproot whole breadths. and that power they use all too freely. Until these birds espy the young blades they can only make haphazard attacks on the grain. The tender blade does not tempt them. .In passing over either a lark or rook-attacked field you will see upraised blades Viing about the I surface—blades that have scarcely been broken. Birds are apt, like other enemies, to attack crops in weak parts. The plan of campaign goes far to destroy nearly the whole crop, while the tak ing of a few blades here and there over a wide area would do no material harm, and might ch.</p>
        <p>good. No forage crops appear to suffer much from birds, save young clovers, among N%:hich is tri- ' folium with occasionally young vetches. And here wood pigeons are the culprits. They go for the centre delicate chit of the young clover plants, and their appetites are never satisfied. They may be scared, and killed for that matter, by powder and shot, and they are worth shoot- ing. But not an hour should be lost in plyint, the powder where fields are begun on, lest it be too late. • A dressing of some offensive fertiliser may sometimes be applied to "kill two birds," by offending the pigeons and fertilising the seeds.—Warwickshire in "Farm and Home."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>BUSINESS COWS.</head>
        <p>BUSINESS COWS.</p>
        <p>The cow is always a boarder upon some per- son, but she should be made to pay for her board at such remunerative rates as will leave a profit for the boarding-house keen,o2,, says a writer in "Farm and Home." If she fails to do so her carcase. should be turned into beef and her hide into leather. A cow with the business habits of ,keeping her accounts with the world paid up through the man who owns and feeds her is a good business cow, and this is the.kind of cow we would recommend. She should have a large, long udder of elastic, fine quality; a mellow, movable Skin, covered with soft. silky hair; a long large barrel, hooped with flat ribs, broad and wide apart, a bad loin, spreading out into broad, long hind-quarters; a clean neck of symmetrical length, carrying a clean-cut, fine head, with prominent eyes. A cow with these points has ability to serve a man well, if she gets a fair chance. That her calves may have powers equal to, or rather better than, .her own, care should be exercised in their breeding. The best blood of the breed adapted to the farmer's pur- pose should be used to enlarge, and not to lessen. the working capacity to be transmitted to her calves. According to her nature as a milk. giver her feeding should be adapted to the work she has to do.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>SWEET PEAS.</p>
        <p>The foundation of success in the successfqil culture of sweet peas is laid in the preparation of the soil. This should now receive attentioh by digging the ground deeply, adding plenty of rotten manue to the bottom spit, which shouhi always be well broken up, so as to allow the roots to reach easily the food that has been in- corporated with the soil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>ROSES ON GALVANISED WIRES</head>
        <p>ROSES ON GALVANISED WIRES</p>
        <p>Many readers may be thinking of planting some climbing rose-S" on the galvanised wire trellises and arches that can now be had so cheaply. To such, -a wo.rd of warning may well be given. One often hears of !the dying of the young wood of roses that have been trained on these wire supports, the thought never striking the grower that the fault lies with hie acid that</p>
        <p>, • I. , ... . • . . . . ^. • . . . . . .9,7,!•-' ' . . . , . :: ''..,. ,4'' • ' 1,.. .. , . . , .-.4. . . . . -10 • --.,, ~ , , -. r e 1.. y. . .. . ~...r,, . '` • , .- Cf.,',. • ..,..?:;ili ~'.'s':: -, ..NS'''..t' .. ' .-- ".. ' : ' ' .., , .. . . . - -- '. .1, . ?':4.;j::' -,,,,,..41-ii;e4.,,,i,,,,:444,0 ~,,,f.,,,6,,,,,1,,., ......._ ~. ... ...vet , WIN •.:i';',`E,,,v\,..-..-:',.,-,---.'.- ...,-;_;:•J .. . ~. . ~, ,S 1 ,K:igiNA,!,,,;.., -.' ~W, , . ,I, ...,.: ,-..-';,....„. ''''A'..4,'?'-',:..4:- ;i• .-..t....";.WN,‘ :A. "' ' ,-, ;;;:Ni'.i....;-'4 . - in; ~... - , . ~,,,..- :_. ~.,,,,,y, ,w,-; ~ . -,t.-.4,,,,, _,,,, :I.- . ~, ,',..,,4',!, . Jo ~.,.!..0. ~ ..,,, ~ ..,` . r- --, :. • s -,, ,t, A. . ~ ~,,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>is used in the galvanising. This trouble is easily and cheaply surmounted by giving the wire two good coats of the best white lead paint.</head>
        <p>is used in the galvanising. This trouble is easily and cheaply surmounted by giving the wire two good coats of the best white lead paint.</p>
        <p>THE MORELLO CHERRY. , , Any reader with a north wall who is anxious to turn it to good account cannot do better than plant thereon a Morello cherry tree. No fruit. tree is so well adapted for a north wall as this-I cherry. Should the soil be very light and gravelly, it is well to take it away and replace it with some good holding loam with which has been mixed plenty of lime rubble. Should the soil be at all heavy, lime rubble should be added freely, while it is all important, seeing that the , soil under a north wall gets little benefit from the sun, that the drainage should be free. The Morello cherry always bears on the young wood of the previous year. The great thing in the culture of this cherry is to see that the bearing wood is spread regularly over the wall; one often sees trees of the Morello bearing fruiting wood at the ends of the branches, the centre composed of bare barren branches. When this happens the young wood can be tied •to these bare limbs, for as a rule there is always plenty of young fruiting wood. In time these bare branches can be entirely removed, their place being taken by the young wood which was tied to them. In this way it is possible to rejuvenate the tree if at the same time care is taken to assist the roots by rich top dressings and fre- quent supplies of liquid manure.—Wick in "Farm and Home."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>FERTILE LAND _ FOR FOOD</head>
        <p>FERTILE LAND _ FOR FOOD</p>
        <p>‘SUPPLY.</p>
        <p>Waste Of Railway Slopes. Is it sufficiently realised that there are very large tracts of good land in this country which are never cultivated? We ,refer to the slopes and verges -of railways, which amount in the aggregate to thousands of acres. Many in- stances could be quoted in which railways have purchased land with the object of providing sufficient area for future line widenings, and pending such developments the land so pur- chased falls out of cultivation. zast year the Board of Agriculture suggested to the railways that increased use might be matte of the waste land adjoining- the lines for the growth of agri- cultural produce, and questimis were also raised as to the destruction of hay by the method adopted on many lines of burning it in order to form a fire line, across which fires resulting from sparks from engines would not spread to the adjoining land. How far the Railway Fires Act of 1905 may have been responsible for this practice we do not know. The Act makes the railways liable for damage 'caused to agricul- tural crops by such fires, ittroviding notice of claim and particulaes of the damage are sent in writing to the railway company within 14 days of the occurrence of the damage. In present circumstances the important ques- tion is how best to utilise the available land in the interests of the national food supply. This is an old problem. In "Darkest England." General Booth expressed the opinion that all the fruit consumed in England could be grown on the railway embankments and cuttings, and the productiveness of gardens at country stations points to the national gain that would result from utilising these slopes fully. Planted with suitable crops, they could not only be made productive, but crops of green vegetables would also be calculated to prevent fires by consti- tuting fire lines. Great Western Company's Offer. We understand that the Great Western Rail- way are giving careful,consideration to the ques- tion how best to bring under cultivation the un- used land along its line of route. In the neigh- bourhood of towns land is largely cultivated by the company's employes, but in outlying 'dis- tricts considerable areas are available, although the absence of proper access to the land from public roads presents a difficulty. The Great Western Company are, however, prepared to consider sympathetically any applications for tenancies of unlet land at low rentals, and arrangements have been made for notices to be exhibited at stations inviting applications for suitable plots. In the national interests we would urge rail- way companies to encourage the cultivation of such lands,- even free of rent. it being of course understood that they would be indemnified against claims in the event of accidents occur- ring. The importance of the question of increas- ing the home production of food supplies cannot be overstated. The Departmental Committee on Food Production have already placed on record their opinion that "it is necessary and practic- able to produce within this country a very large proportion of the food stuffs and °fil'r agricul- tural products natural to its soil but now Pur- chased abroad at a cost of nearly 300 million pounds per annum, twAhirds of. which are de- rived from countries outside the British Empire."—" The Times."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>HAY IMPRESSMENTS.</head>
        <p>HAY IMPRESSMENTS.</p>
        <p>Farmers Safeguarded By The County Committees.</p>
        <p>A judgment of great interest to taxpayers in general, and to agriculturists in particular, has just been given by Judge Scully in .a number of Berkshire, Oxfordshire and Buckinghamshire cases which his Honour heard for the conveni- ence of all parties at the Royal -Cotirts of Justice, London, on , 24th and 25th November, 1915. The cases had reference to the impress- ment under the Army Act of hay by the Gov- ernment from a dealer, and Ms Honour, at the conclusion of the arguments, reserved judg- ment, which he has just handed down to the parties concerned. The proceedings .had reference to some 794' tons of hay, and the Judge's award of .23,665 is a less amount* than the Military Authorities offered and the applicant had refused. Under these circumstances the Judge ordered the applicant, to pay £5O towards thi3 costs of the Army Council. The short facts of the case were as follows At various dates between the early part of May, -1914, and the middle of August of the same year, the District Purchasing Officers of Berk- shire, Oxfordshire and Buckinghamshire im- pressed from Mr. Chilton, under the Army Act, nearly eight hundred tons of hay, which he had bought from farmers. The hay was required for baling for the Overseas Forces of the Crown, and it was admittedly of good quality and suitable for 'the purpoge, as the 'only ques- tion before the Judge was what price should .awarded to Mr. Chilton. . . Mr. Chilton admitted, in cross-examination,' that 'some of the hay he had bought for Ms., ' but he had to replace this at a much higher price. He also claimed that he was entitled to the profits he could have got on his hay by selling it in the London market. He was en- titled as a business man to any advantage that a rising market brought. A feature of the very long judgment is the opinion expressed by his Honour that farmers' interests are thoroughly safeguarded by the 'County Committees which the War Office have established as part of the organisation of the scheme for the direct purchase of forage from the grower. The fact that a dealer could obtain a big price for hay in London did not alttr its intrinsic value where it stood, or affect the current Price of hay in the local market. It was. argued that it was unfair to take a mer- chant's hay from him at the same pi ice as .the farmers thought sufficient, without allowing him anything for the 'profit he might expect. That argument, if it conflicted with the Statute, must be addressed to Parliament.. To ignore the profit which the merchant might have made was neither mc re nor less unfair tha happened, in other cases of compulsory pur- chase. He thought, however, that the appli- cant had probably exaggerated the price at '.which he could have sold the hay in question in London for the fact that the Army took all</p>
        <p>the hay, and the London prices quoted were for "best only," would, he thought, sensibly reduce the difference between .the two valua- tions. -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>4 :1111.D-LIFE IN ENGLAND AND</head>
        <p>4 :1111.D-LIFE IN ENGLAND AND</p>
        <p>GERMANY.</p>
        <p>Mr. Waiter Scoles, chairman of the Shaftes- bury Society and Ragged School Union, speak; ing on Saturday at a reception of workers at Sion College, said he had searched Germany through and through and had not found any orgiinisation that sought to uplift and save a single child. In Germany a gathering like that was unknown and impossible.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
