      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>THIRTY YEARS A TREASURER</head>
        <p>THIRTY YEARS A TREASURER</p>
        <p>Appreciation Of Bro. Wm. AA atson'</p>
        <p>Faithful Services.</p>
        <p>At the half-yearly meeting of the " Constitu- tion " Lodge of the Independent Order of Odd- fellows (Manchester Unity) Friendly Society, held at the Pelican Hotel, on Wednesday even- ing, Mr. William Watson, who has acted as treasurer of the lodge for over 30 years, 'tendered his resignation owing to failing health. P.P.G.M. Ti S. Steel, in voicing the apprecia- tion of the m9mbers of the lodge, said that P.G. Watson had served the lodge faithfully for more than 30 years. Durinc, the time that he had held office.. the lodge had several tijnes passed through financial difficulties; but 'Bro.- Watson had been spared to witness the survival of those difficulties and to see the lodge in a stronger financial position. There had been many changes in the lodge, and nearly all those with whom he had been associated when he under- took the office of treasurer had passed away. He (Bro. Steel) would very much have preferred that P.G. Watson should have retained the office of treasurer, but no.doubt Bro. Watson had care-</p>
        <p>fully considered the matter before resigning his position, and had come to this conclusion after very careful thought. They all regretted his resignation, and they woull desire to place on record their sincere appreciation of the services he had rendered to the lodge during his long connection with them as treasurer. rew men were privileged to hold office in Odelfellows' lodge for such a great length of time, and it must be very gratifying to Bro. Watson to see the progress the lodge had made in the Warring- ton District. He (Bro. Steel) hoped that the members would not be satisfied by merely re- ' cording their appreciation of Bro. Watson's services, but that some lasting memento would be fixed in the lodge room. (Applause.) Whilst regretting Bro. Watson's severaUce from . the office of treasurer, they hoped that he would continue to take an interest in the welfare of the lodge, and that he might be spared to re- , main with them for several y ears, and that during that time he might be blessed with pod health. P.P.G.M. T. Rigty, in seconding the proposal, said that he remembered P.O. Watson being appointed to the office of treasurer, and he con- gratulated him upon the fact of having been spared to see the lodge rise t 6 the proud pOsition it held among the lodges in the Warrington District.</p>
        <p>The resolution wiis supported by P.P.G.M. Lester, P.G. Burdett and c•thers, ald was carried .unanimously. f P.G. Watson, who was visibly affected, briefly replied. Tie thanked the Members for the record of appreciation and the resolution, and promised that so long as he wa spared he would continue to take an active interest in the lodge. There were two candidates for the position of treasurer of the lodge. P.P.O.M. Lester and P.G. A. E. Burdett. and eventually P.G. A. E. Burdett was elected.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>AVM-TIME 1:4 ft Mt PRICES.</head>
        <p>AVM-TIME 1:4 ft Mt PRICES.</p>
        <p>Up 82 Per Cenl. In Berlin Since 19H;. 111 Per Cent. In Vienna. The "Board of Trade Labour Gazette" gives a comparison of retail food prices in the United Kingdom, Berlin, Vienna, and Italy. In this country they were higher on January 1 than on December 1 by less than '1 per cent. Fish showed an advance of about ,6 per cent.; the prices of flour, cheese, and potatoes con- tinued to rise, to the extent of about 2 per cent., and bread also increased somewhat. Meat, bacon, tea, sugar, milk, and Margarine showed practically no change in price, and there was a slight fall in the average prices of butter and eggs. Compared with 'July, 1914, prices in De- cember had risen 44 per cent. The general level of retail food Atices in Berlin appears to have fallen in NoxemPer by 6.6 per cent. as compared with the previous month, but to have been 82 per cent. above that of July, 1914. The drop in November, based on the latest Prussian official statistics, does not necessarily imply a corresponding relaiation of the strain due to short supplies, brit is due to the operation of the recent orders of the Federal Council fixing maiimuni retail prices for im- portant foods. In Vienna considerable advances have taken place since October, the only article which fen in price being potatoes, and it is calculated that on the whole.the cost of food was between 10 and 11 per cent. higher in October than in " September. Compared with July, 1914, every article (with the exception of potatoes) has in- creased in cost, the rise varying from nearly • 9 per cent. for sugar to 343 per cent. for lard. The Oneral level of prices showed an advance of over 117 per cent. Food pricer in October were on the whole about 111 per cent. higher than in the corresponding month of 1914. ' In Italy, compared with the figures for July, • 1914, prices for November sliowed an.. advance I of 28.6 per cent.</p>
        <p>„</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
