      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>-111</head>
        <p>-111</p>
        <p>LACK OF DISCIPLINE.</p>
        <p>,• • • ,</p>
        <p>J. CROSFIELD AND SONS.</p>
        <p>Juveniles Take Advantage Of A Soldier-Parents And Teachers.</p>
        <p>HIGHWAY ROBBER AT</p>
        <p>SEVEN.</p>
        <p>Recently there has been an unusual amount of crime by children in Warrington, and after several juvenile defendants had been prose- cuted at the Police Court on Wednesday, the Director of Education (Mr! J. M. Murray) made an important statement to the magistrates. "It is difficult to account for this increase in crime," he said, "but one must draw the con- clusion that it must be due to fathers being away at the Front, and the schools being de- pleted of male teachers. There is not the slight- est doubt that discipline in the town is growing much worse, and, if the magistrates would make some remarks or suggestions, the Education Committee would be grateful. It was a very serious matter indeed, and to see young children brought to the Police Court on charges of theft is most terrible." The Chairman of the Bench (Mr. John Hay- ward) rieplied that the magistrates were wishful to do all they could to help the education authorities. He suggested doing with children what he should do .with his own, that was, give them a birching. The magistrates would like parents to understand that if their children came to the Court a second time they would be sent to a reformatory school.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>HIGHWAY ROBBER AT SEVEN.</head>
        <p>HIGHWAY ROBBER AT SEVEN.</p>
        <p>The Bench had just dealt with several young- sters, all charged with theft. A bright-eyed boy of seven years had stopped. a girl of the same age—a daughter of Mrs. Bolsworth, of Napier-street—in Church-street, and asked her whether she had been to the Co- operative Stores, and had got some change. Re- ceiving a reply in the affirmative, he asked her to buy him a pennyworth of sweets, but she declined, saying, ",It's mamma's money." Not to be denied, the boy asked her to let him look at the money, and, as soon as she opened her hand, he snatched the coins—amounting to 4s. 2d.—and ran away. Another boy .of similar years had noticed the incident, and proceeded to chivalrous action by following the highway robber. For a time the young champion of the girl was eluded, but eventually he caught the thief up, and the thief suggested that he should take a shilling and say nothing. Re refused, and the money was thrown under a cart. When the amount of 4s. 2d. was mentioned, the boy, who was charged with the theft, said, "It was, only 2W." The magistrates ordered him to receive three strokes with the birch rod.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>CHILDREN DIVIDE SPOILS.</head>
        <p>CHILDREN DIVIDE SPOILS.</p>
        <p>Through the activities of two more boys—one fifteen years old and the other ten—Frederick Adams, newsagent and tobacconist, of 166, Lovely-lane, lost a box of cigarettes. It was proved that the elder boy sent in the younger to buy a halfpennyworth of cigarettes, and, while in the shop, asked him to steal a boxful. He did so and afterwards they divided the spoils in Bewsey-roatl. The younger bqj was ordered to receive six strokes with the birch rod, and the elder W•AS fined 10s. Mr. Swallow (instructed by Mr. T. S. Stee:, made an appeal on behalf of one of the boys.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>GIRL'S DOWNFALL</head>
        <p>A GIRL'S DOWNFALL</p>
        <p>A respectably-dressed girl of fifteen years o age was charged with stealing two rainproo coats and a boy's overcoat, belonging to Mrs. Mabel Walker, of 19, Froghall-lane, and a rain- coat, the property of Mrs. Alice .Hughes, of Leicester-street. It was proved that the cloth- ing had been taken from the halls in the two houses while the doors had been left open. The girl took them home, saying that her aunt had given them to her, and, later, her mother sent her to a pawnshop with them. In the meantime the pawnbroker had been notified by the police that the goods had been stolen, and gave in- formation which led to the girl being appre- hended. The defendant said she did not know what made her steal the clothing. It was mentioned .that the girl was in em- ployment, and, in fining her 205., Mr. Hayward impressed on parents the necessity of watching for any signs of dishonesty in their children during their early years. He hoped that the father of the girl would stop the fine put of her pocket money, so that she would feel she was being punished for the offence she had com- mitted. The father explained that as his wife was an invalid it had been impossible to bestow on the girl the care,she needed.-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>BY-ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>BY-ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>MILE END.</p>
        <p>Mile End polls on Tuesday next, and Mr. Warwick Brookes, the Coalition candidate, is presenting himself to the electors in the guise of a business man, and his sane views on ques- tions connected with the war—the only ques- tions which count in the election—are commend- ing him very favourably to the somewhat criti- cal attention of the Mile End public. The Airman Candidate.</p>
        <p>Mr. Pemberton Billing, speaking on Saturday at the opening of his campaign as " air " candi- date at the Mile End by-election (caused by the succession of Colonel Harry Lawson to the peerage), said that, as a practical airman, 'he knew theiair raids or. London could be stopped. There was nothing wrong with the air service; all they wanted were orders and the machines to go up. There would then be no more air raids. on London, a.MI the lights Could be up again, with normal conditions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>NORTH-WEST STAFFORDSHIRE.</head>
        <p>NORTH-WEST STAFFORDSHIRE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Samuel Finney (Labour) was on Monday returned Unopposed for North-West Stafford- shire, in succession to the late Mr. Albert Stanley.</p>
        <p>PORTSMOUTH. Sir H. Meux Elected Unopposed.</p>
        <p>Admiral of the Fleet the 'Hon. Sir Hedworth Meux, the retiring Commander-in-Chief at Portsmouth, was on Saturday returned un- opposed .as Member of Parliament for that borough in succession to Admiral 'Lord Charles Beresford. who has been raised to the peerage. Sir H. Meux is a younger brother of Lord Durham, and took the name of Meux in place of Lambton under the will of the late Lady Meux, who left him .Theobalds Park and much other property. Born in 1856, he has seen active service in Egypt and in the South African War, where he commanded the Naval Brigade in Ladysmith. In 1900 he contested Newcastle as a Liberal. _ .</p>
        <p>There is, the " Times " believes, no precedent, at any rate in modern times, for an officer of Sir H. Meux's rank sitting in the Rouse of Commons.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>Confusion Worse Confounded.</head>
        <p>Confusion Worse Confounded.</p>
        <p>Sailor (who has been reprimanded.by young officer for not saluting): Beg pardon, sir.' but you Tonunies ' are all so much alike."— "Punch."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>Steady Increase Of Business Reported.</head>
        <p>Steady Increase Of Business Reported.</p>
        <p>MR. JOHN CROSFIELD AND MAJOR GEORGE CROSFIELD RETIRE.</p>
        <p>The report of the directors of Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons, soap manufacturers, Bank Quay, Warrington, in the balance sh3.t thsy present to the shareholders . 114 at November 30th last, shows i tinuance of the steady increc in the business of the company to which reference has been made in the reports of previous years. After providing for dividends on the prefer- ence and ordinary capital, the directors have placed the sum of .R 20,000 to reserve, £20,000 to depreciation, and carried forward £10,113 16s. 3d. to the next account.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>toss to the Company.</head>
        <p>A toss to the Company.</p>
        <p>During the year under review II r. S. J. Cros- field, the chairman, and Major G. R. Crosfield, the deputy-chairman, both retired from the directorate. The directors regard this as a great loss not only to the company, but to themselves personally, and they desire to record their heartiest appreciation of the many valu- able services rendered by these gentlemen to the company, and their thanks for the many kindnesses they and others associated with the companv have received at their hands. The directors have elected as members of the Board Mr. F. W. Brock (one of the managing directors of Brunner, Mond' and Co., Ltd.), Mr. • F. J. Burlton, for many years the company's chief engineer, and MT. E. F. Armstrong, D.Sc.</p>
        <p>Loyal Services.</p>
        <p>The directors wish to record their apprecia:</p>
        <p>tion of the loyal .services of the managers and staff throughout the year, ,which have been ren- dered more arduous by the enlistment of so large a number of the company's employes. The directors have been much gratified at the re- sponse the employes of the company have made to the military needs of the country. The allocation to reserve and depreciation are the same as before.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>ENGLISH EARTHQUAKE.</head>
        <p>ENGLISH EARTHQUAKE.</p>
        <p>Chimney Pots Brought Down In . Midlands.</p>
        <p>There was an earthquake shock in the Mid- lands and also in some of the Northern counties on Friday evening of last week about' 7.30. The tremors did not last more than ten minutes, but caused sonsiderable• alarm at several towns. . Residents of the southern suburbs of Man- chester felt the shock, which seemed to travel through Cheshire in a southerly direction. Chimneys fell at Stafford and Stoke, and at the latter place the theatre and town hall rocked slightly. Furniture rattled in several Midland towns, and houses were shaken severely at Foui Oaks, Sutton Coldfield. In some places there' was a rumbling noise like distant thunder, and at Market Drayton many people rushed into the streets, thinking something terrible was happening. Shocks were officially recorded at the Peak of Derbyshire, both Matlock and Wirksworth re- cording oscillations, but no damage was reported. • . A sharp earthquake shock was registered bv IMr. J. J. Shaw at his West Bromwich observatory at 7hr. 29min. 6sec. on Friday</p>
        <p>evening. This was said to have been the severest shock experienced since that on the morning of December 17th, 1896, when many houses were cracked and chimneys brought down. The latest shock more or less coincides with the area disturbed by the earthquakes of March 1903, and of July 3rd, 1904.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>DOCTORS SAID HIS ECZEMA WAS</head>
        <p>DOCTORS SAID HIS ECZEMA WAS</p>
        <p>PAST CURING.</p>
        <p>Zam-Buk Replaces Running Sores</p>
        <p>With New Healthy Skin.</p>
        <p>Nothing but Zam-Buk's agnazing healing power could rid Mr. Thomas Hunt of the fiery eczema and give him a new skin. Mr. Hunt, who has turned 70, live g at Radnage Common, Stokenchurch, Bucks., where he said to a reporter : "Itchy- pimples first broke -out, on my hands and body. These pimples burst and turned to running eczema, which spread practically all over me. I was simply a mass of sores. Even my feet were covered with the terrible disease. "For years ,I couildn't work, and finally I was unable even to walk. I was an in- patient at . ,a London hospital for 27 eeks, and afterwards attended as an out-patient for several years. I received the most skilful treatment there, but still the eczema refused to be cured, and when two private doctors gave me up as hopeless, I felt it was use- less 'trying anything more. "One day, however, a friend who knew what a deplorable state I was in with the running sores all over my body and limbs, suggested I should try Zam-Buk. I never for a moment thought that Zam-Buk would do me any good, but soon after I started using the rare balm I began to see an improvement in my skin. "The irritation was not so bad. So I deter- mined to persevere carefully with Zam-Buk. As a result the terrible disease was gradually rooted out of my skin and new healthy tissues took the place of the disfiguring and painful sores. • "Though I am over 70 years'old I can honestly say that Zam-Bul has completely cured me, and has left me with a perfectly healthy skin. As Zam-Buk has cured such a terrible case as mine, no sufferer from skin disease need ever despair. There is only one lam-Buk for human use, and that is sold in green packages sealed with a Government stamp. Zam-Buk is splendid for chapped hands, chilblains, ulcers, bad legs, .piles, ringworm, poisoned sores, eczema, pimples, &amp;c. Obtainable of air chemists and drug stores. There is a new Veterinary form of Zam-Buk —a red preparation sold in a Red package— which is unequalled for the treatment of dogs, horses, cattle, poultry and pets.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>DEATH OF MR. ARNOLD MORLEY.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. ARNOLD MORLEY.</p>
        <p>The death is announced of the Right Hon. Arnold Morley. Mr. Morley, the son/ of Mr. Samuel Morley, M.P. for Bristol, and grandson of Mr. Samuel Hope, of Liverpool, was born in 1849. He was a Liberal, and was elected to the House of Com- mons by Nottingham in 1880, and remained in Parliament until 1895. From 1886 to 1892 he was chief Liberal Whip. Then he Joined Mr. Gladstone's last Ministry as Postmaster- General, which office he held until the defeat of Lord Rosebery's Cabinet. After the election of 1895 he did not return to the House. While at Cambridge he was a fine oar, and to the last he was a 1-ert yachtsman. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <p>Make your OLD Grates Into NEW</p>
        <p>and economise fuel by trying one of our</p>
        <p>Inexpensive and Easily Fitted.</p>
        <p>51, 53 &amp; 55, BRIDGE STREET, WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>BARLESS FIRES.</p>
        <p>J. S. Garland,</p>
        <p>pool magistrates as bearing wirness to the bene- fit of the restrictions, but he does not quote the following passage from the report, which emphasises the grave increase of home-drinking directly traceable to these too drastic regula- tions :—" Adequate steps should be taken to pre- vent the trade in intoxicants being diverted from the public-houses, where it is under control and observation, to the homes of the people. If this is not done, serious results may arise." The Liverpool magistrates are mostly rabid teetotallers and strong supporters of Control Board (or any other) restriction; their warning is therefore doubly important " Citizen " wants to extend the regulations, that have proved so dangerous in Liverpool, to Warring- ton. I hope Warrington people will protest very strongly before they submit to this futile and exasperating tyranny.</p>
        <p>J. E. SIMPSON. Liverpool, January 15th, 1916.</p>
        <p>• • . •.•t* •?:: • :....„*.••••••••••;:',.,...,.:.,.:*‘• ' • . ' '• • - ••• z.,..:,•*4,";•;/.4‘,....•;'..-.:i.,415.e...p.441,;•/.... • _ :`</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>frtters to ti r °Mita</head>
        <p>frtters to ti r °Mita</p>
        <p>SINGLE MEN AND MANUFAC- TURERS' PROFITS.</p>
        <p>Sir,--just a\-few linesqn your paper regarding single men. A good many firms are keeping single men ,from enlisting just for their own material advantage. I know of a few firms in Warrington who have single men as foremen. Why cannot married men take single men's work -as foremen? It is not patriotic of these firms to do such a thing. We ought to have compulsion. Every single man ought to be mall° to enlist. I appeal to the young wonicrt who are court- ing these Ming men not to iold them back. We have a bungling Government, or compulsion would have been here long ago. It is not justice nor right to the young men in the `trenches for these single young men to remain it home while they are fighting for us. All the young women ought to boycott all the -single men who are not in khaki uniform. I trust this -letter will make the single men lact and not talk, and all those who are physi- cally able to fight for their country. Some women eoula be put on the tramcars in War- Tington, like other places.</p>
        <p>- PRIVATE JOHNATHAN CRIBBINS Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>PARENT SLACKER.</head>
        <p>THE PARENT SLACKER.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Much has been written about the un- patriotic conduct of the single shirker or slacker (and lightly, too), but why should the parent slacker, go untold? These people are doing their utmost (and succeeding in many cases) to pre- vent their sons doing their duty to their King and country. Manufacturers' sons, gentlemen farmers' sons, and shopkeepers' sons are getting exemptions galore—and it is nothing but a burn- ing shame that such as these are exempted, whilst the working man's son has no option in the matter. Instead of providing armlets for this class the authorities should have given them coward's badges. The Continental system of conscription is had 'enough, but this is worse. In the former rich 'and poor are treated alike; it is not so here. The Labour party have made a very big mis- ' take in not having a representative on the 'Tribunal, to see that every case is tested on its merits. I hold the opinion that no single man is indispensable to his business at a crisis like this (unless he is really working on munitions). -Why should clerks in munition works be starred men? Surely they are not indispensable. Older men could easily be obtained to fill their plaees. The sooner the Government deal firmly with this 'question the better it will be for the cause. PATRIOT.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>DRINK RESTRICTIONS.</head>
        <p>DRINK RESTRICTIONS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Your irrepressible correspondeibt, Citizen" (of the wide, wide world af.;cas.ts me of having broken my word; but I think lir now your readers will know him sulTwier_liv weal •to ignore these little exhibitions .1 111-ternrer .and bad manners. My promise to retire was .conditional on " Citizen's " good behaTiour. As 'he has not received my offer in the spirit in .Which it was tendered, it is, of course, null and 'void.</p>
        <p>Mr. "Citizen" reminds.one irresistibly of the 'German wireless; he is constantly boasting of 'victory, but. never gets any " forrader." When "Jae says " My case is vindicated up to the hilt," one can only smile indulgently, as at the prattle of a child playing King of the Castle. Ire has •put up a very weak case so far, and reference to an irrelevant by-eleclion does not strengthen it.</p>
        <p>"Citizen" mentions the report of the Liver-</p>
        <p>MADE TO MEASURE, Fit Guaranteed, Materials Guaranteed.</p>
        <p>LADIES'</p>
        <p>SUITS nom 14/11</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>ROLL CALL.</head>
        <p>THE ROLL CALL.</p>
        <p>Sir,—The tremendous war through which we are now passing has brought out grit and character in the nation, as well as in others engaged in it. To say that the war is an un- mitigated evil is a mistake. War, like many other things in this world, brings sorrow, desolation and death in its train, but it brings also patriotism, courage, endurance, unselfish- ness and faithfulness to death, and there are multitudes of instances of our turning to the heart of God when the nearness of Eternity calls out. Realities stand out and the world and all its unrealities fade away, but the great reality of all is the Lord Jesus Christ.</p>
        <p>When we look back over the past year, of how many we may say and of some near and dear to ourselves "He answered the roll call willingly?" His country needed him and he joined the Colours, either by land or Sea, and in these days we can also say by air, for our colours float there as well. This war is a terrible blow to many na.tio.ns, for there is not a man in the country but feels that it is with him both day and night. There is not a woman who does not sorrow constantly for the brave lads who are still falling for this country. The mother, whose husband is eerving at the Front with a son, while two other brave sons or even one in the service of this country have answered not only the roll call, but their last I roll call, meets you with a smile upon her lips and tears in her eyes, and the sweet grasp of her hand speaks volumes. Even the mothers and wives have answered the roll call as well as their husbands and sons. We take up our newspapers day by day and we eee the words in heavy letters, England's "Roll of Honour?' But we know what it means. We know that a shot under the water will send and has already sent, 'hundreds of our seamen to a grave in the ocean, that another shot fired at the Musitania sent multi- tudes of undefended people to the same watery grave, and that the pathetic words "Killed in action?' carries the death knell to many a heart. • There is only one hope that can comfort those whose dear ones have laid down their lives for their country, and hope is in the love of God, as shown in the Lord Jesus Christ. Who gave His life for our • salvation, and Who is, and was God's love gift to the world. In the olden days the soldier King David, whose life was always in his 'own hands, who fought more battles than one could count, and whose deeds of valour would have won the Victoria Cross many a time over, rested his heart in God, and encouraged himself in the Mord his -God. It reminded me of the small text cards on some- one's wall at home, "God is our refuge and strength."</p>
        <p>E. DAVIES.' [The above letter was written to his wife, Mrs. Davies, 31, Hopwood-street, 'Warrington, by Private E. Davies, of the Royal Marines, with the expression of the desire that it should be published in "The Examiner." We have pre- sented it, as above. in the form of a letter addressed to the Editor.]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>.0,„ 24/11</head>
        <p>.0,„ 24/11</p>
        <p>COSTUMES</p>
        <p>MADE TO MEASURE, Exquisitely Tailored in 1916 Designs. (This opportunity of obtaining a Costume Tailor-made should be taken advantage of by every lady.)</p>
      </div>
