      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>BANKS.</head>
        <p>THE BANKS.</p>
        <p>MANCHESTER AND LIVERPOOL</p>
        <p>DISTRICT BANK.</p>
        <p>Large Increases Compared With Last</p>
        <p>Year:</p>
        <p>A meeting of the shareholders of the Man- chester and Liverpool District Banking Com- pany, Limited, was held on Tuesday, at the head office of the bank in Spring Gardens, Manchester, Sir Edward Donner, Bart., chair- man of the bank, presiding. The report of the directors recommending a dividend for the half-year ended 31st December, 1915, of 21s. a share, subject to the deduction of income tax, was adopted. The Chairman, in moving the adoption of the report, referred to the recent absorption of the .Bank of Whitehaven. He mentioned that the latter bank was established 80 years ago, and during that time had gained and maintained a high and honourable reputation in the district that it served. The directors had the greatest satisfaction in concluding the alliance on terms they thought fair and satis- factory to the shareholders of both banks. The Chairman then briefly referred to the division of the shares in accordance with the proposal in the form of special resolutions which each shareholder had already received. The present shares are £6O each, £l2 paid, and are to be divided proportionately into four shares of £l5 each, £3 paid. He referred with gratification to the fact that the step had al- ready been welcomed as providing a more con- venient form of . investment. After briefly referring to the substitution of new articles of association, the Chairman said he felt sure the shareholders would be more than satisfied with the profit and loss and balance sheet for the half-year.' Over £lOO,OOO Carried Forward.</p>
        <p>The payment of the usual dividend of 2is. a share, subject to the deduction of income tax, amounted to £165,900. This left a balance of £114,676, the whole of which they proposed to carry forward. The directors believed that the shareholders would concur in the policy of carrying forward the whole of the surplus pro- fits rather than of making allocations to reserve fund, &amp;c. The balance carried forward showed an increase, of £49,000 on a year ago. The bank's holding in the War Loan had been taken at net cost price in conformity with the deci- sion of the principal banks of the country. The special reserve of £200,000 for the depreciation of investments, together with the balance- car- ried forward of £114,676, not only covered all the depreciation in the bank's investments at the current prices on December 31st, but pro- vided by far the greater portion of the depre- ciation in the War Loan. Current, deposit, and other accounts, ,228,666,098—this was once again at December a record amount. 115)r nine years in succession Ile had been able to say that the amount had shown an annual increase. The increase for the last twelve months amounted to one and a quarter millions, and approxi- mately an increase of seven and a quarter millions since December, 1907, when the figures of the Lancaster Bank were first incorporated with the District Bank. Cash in hand and in the Bank of England £4,100,476, or an increase of half a million as compared with a year ago. High Percentage of Cash on Deposit.</p>
        <p>The bank has never shown a higher percen- tage of cash on the deposit liability at Decem- ber, although the percentage was slightly less than that shown in June last year. In referring to money with London brokers and others, including Stock Exchange loans, half the reduction of one and a half millions was represented by repayments on account of Stock Exchange loans. With regard to, bills of exchange, £1,682,943, which showed a large re- duction during the year of over three million pounds, he explained that this was entirely due to the fact that the volume of bills current had been gradually reduced. British Government securities, £3,452,600, included the bank's sub- scriptions to the first War Loan, and Consols which 'since the publication of the balance sheet in June had been converted into Four and a Half Per Cent. War Loan, to which must be added the bank's subscription to the second War Loan of five millions. As mentioned in the report, the whole of the British Government securities were valued in the balance sheet at cost price. The other investments amounted to 0t2,386,661 ; the depreciation had been ,amply provided for in the way to which he had already referred.-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>PARR'S BAN{ LIMITED.</head>
        <p>PARR'S BAN{ LIMITED.</p>
        <p>At the fifty-first ordinary general meeting of Parr's Bank, Ltd., to be held at Cannon-street Hotel, London, on Thursday, January 27th, the directors will sub,nit their report and balance sheet for the year ended December 31st, 1915. This states that including £225,670, 7s. 4d.</p>
        <p>brought forward at 31st December, 1914, taking War Loan Stock at cost, writing down other in- vestments' to or under prices ruling on 31st December, 1915, providing for all bad and doubt- ful debts, contributing to the Staff Widows' Fund, and rebating current bills; the balance of profits is £671,970 4s. 2d. Out of this an interim dividend at the rate of 18 per cent. per annum for the half-year ended 30th June, 1915, was paid on sth August, amounting to £218,117 145.; less income tax, £22,720 us. 10d.-2195,397 2s. 2d.; leaving £476,573 2s. From this sum the directors recommend the payment of a half-year's divi- dend at the rate of 18 per cvnt. per annum, pay- fible on sth February, 1916 (making 18 per cent. for the year 1915) amounting to £218,117 145.; less income tax, £35,444 2s. 6d--2182,673 11s. 6d.; leaving balaece to be carried to next account £293,899 10s. Gd. The Bank subscribed for a substantial amount • of the Wet Loans- issued by the Government, and 'also participated in the</p>
        <p>measure adopted in connection with American Exchange.. Branches of the Bank have been opened at Luton and Sherborne, and a sub-branch at Blagdon, Somerset. During the year the Bank has acquired by purchase the business of Messrs. Thomas Barnard and, Co., of Bedford, which was established in 1799. The sole proprietor, Mr. Thomas Henry Barnard, continues, as local director, his connection with the business. The Right Hon. Lord Farquhar ha's,:ip consequence of his appointment as Lord Steward of His Majesty's Household, resigned his seat at the Board, and the directors much regret to lose his valuable services. Mr. R. W. Whalley, director and general manager, retired at the end of April from the general managership, after upwards of forty years in the service of the Bank. He continues to give the Bank the benefit of his great busi- ness experience, and has been elected Deputy Chairman of the Board of Directors in place of Mr. Arthur J. Fraser, who desired to relinquish that position, but retains his seat on the Board. Mr. John Stewart, formerly deputy general manager, and Mr. John Rae, formerly assistant general manager, have been appointed joint general managers. The directors desire to put on record their appreciation of the patriotism of those mem- bers of the Bank's staff who have joined His Majesty's Forces, and their sympathy with the relatives of thow who have lost their lives in the service of the country.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>UNION B 11K OF MANCHESTER.</head>
        <p>UNION B 11K OF MANCHESTER.</p>
        <p>The half-year's profit was £108,733 and £10,571 was brought forward. From the total of £119,305 is deducted £46,096 for expenses at the head office and branches (89 offices), includ- ing special expenses arising out of the war. The dividend recommended is 121 per cent. per annum, £3,000 is to be written off bank pre- mises, £2,000 applied to the pension fund, and £25,919 carried forward. The report states that, notwithstanding. the war, the growth •of the business has been greater than in any other year. The bank's holdings in Consols and the 31 per cent. War Loan have been converted into 41, per cent. War 'Loan. In conformity with'the course adopted by the principal banks, the result of the conversion, and also £1,200,000 taken up afresh, has been valued at net cost price. As all .Government securities previously held by the bank had been written down to or below the official or market prices, no further provision has been necessary. The current, deposit, and other accounts amount to £7,053,582, as against £6,594,015 jast year, and loans and advances to customeg to £4,204,289, as against £4,440,239. The year's net profit has been .420,821, as against £100,948 in 1914, £108.984 in 1913, and £95,895 in 1912.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>LONDON CITY AND MIDLIND</head>
        <p>LONDON CITY AND MIDLIND</p>
        <p>BANK.</p>
        <p>The balance sheet of the London City and Midland Bank to December 31st shows that the holding of gold coin, which a year ago was £8,000,000, has been reduced to Ammo, while the total cash in hand amounts to £30,881,200, compared with £33,196,458 a year previously. The bank is still the only one which gives particulars of its holding of gold coin, and the retention of such a large amount will, no doubt, be fully justified by Sir Edward Holden should any criticism be offered. The remarkable growth of the bank is shown in the continued increase of current and deposit accounts. At the end of 1913 the total was &lt;03,833,850, of 1914 &lt;Z25,732,736, while the figures now published show the huge total of A:147,750,702. The hank's investments have risen to &lt;08,829,433, of which ‘03,946,384 repre- sents War Loans at cost and other British Government securities. As already stated, the directors 'have placed &lt;042,860 to Investment Account out of the profits.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>GERMAN PREFERS ENGLAND.</head>
        <p>GERMAN PREFERS ENGLAND.</p>
        <p>"Let The Kaiser Fight For Himself."</p>
        <p>John Godfrey Edward Oscar Stegmafin, 31, a German, was charged at Newcastle-on-Tyne on Monday with having resided in the prohibited area of Newcastle without the perniission of the Aliens Registration Office, and further with having made a false statement to a boarding- house keeper. The accused at first said he was a Scantlin-</p>
        <p>_ avian, but at the detective office stated: I am a German reservist. I am German born, but not •German at heart. If I had been I would have been fighting. There is no liberty, there,' and I want to enjoy the liberty of this country as long as I can. Let the Kaiser fight for him- self."</p>
        <p>The accused was sent to prison for six months on each charge, the sentences to rim con- currently.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>MINER'S ASTHMA.</head>
        <p>A MINER'S ASTHMA.</p>
        <p>Had suffered for 20 years, yet cured by VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE.</p>
        <p>Mr. William BlatChford, 12 -Swinburn- tarrace, Vipton, Co. Durham, suffered 'for 20 years from miner's asthma. He says: "I cannot praise Veno's enough for what it has done for me I could hardly get my breath sometimes, and often I dared not go to bed for fear of suffocating, and even to walk out into the open air used to choke me up. But now I enjoy the best of health; Veno's cured me." Veno's Lightning Cough Cure was awarded the Grand Prix and Gold Medal for safety and efficacy at the 1910 Paris Health Exhibition, and is a perfect remedy, for all cliegt: lung and., throat troubles. Prices 111 d, Is. 3d., and 35.,, of all chemists.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>IN THE COURTS.</head>
        <p>IN THE COURTS.</p>
        <p>WANTED HIS REVENGE.</p>
        <p>Drunken- Longford Labourer Annoyed</p>
        <p>With Tram Conductor.</p>
        <p>Residents in the neighbourhood of Longford recently witnessed the novel spectacle of a traiaear being chased by a man who was under the influence of drink, and who +wanted to have his revenge out of the conductor. But at last Saturday's Police Court the individual in question adopted a far different attitude. His name is Michael Logan, a labourer, of 345, Winwick-road, and he was charged with being drunk and disorderly, and obstructing the pas- sage of a Corporation tramcar. George Ainsworth, a tramdriver, of 29, George- street, said that on the previous Friday night he was driving a car towards Winwick, when the conductor came to him and complained of the prisoner's conduct. The latter was making use of foul language. He was requested to leave the car, but refused, saying that he had paid his penny. Later, he attempted to step off, but, in doing so, fell to the ground. *hen the car was returning from Longford Logan rushed at the car, but the witness put speed on and escaped him. Nearly all the evening he was waiting about for the car. As a result of his conduct, one journey was lost. James Albert Ellis (19), of Grappenhall, gave similar evidence.</p>
        <p>There had been ten previous convictions against Logan, and he was fined 405., the alternative being a month's imprisonment. For Her Husband's Sake.</p>
        <p>Logan's wife appeared before ,the Bench on Tuesday, a charge of committing a breach of the peace being preferred against her. Acting-Sergeant Hayes said he had occasion to arrest the husband of the prisoner on Saturday night and, as he did so, she rushed at him and struck him two blows on the face. Someone in the crowd struck him several times in the back and he had a very hot time of it. Mrs. Logan was bound over for six months to keep the peace, one surety of ,E 5 being required.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>ARMY PROPERTY IN SOLDIER'S POSSESSION.</head>
        <p>ARMY PROPERTY IN SOLDIER'S POSSESSION.</p>
        <p>James Francis North, a private of the 12th South Lancs. Regiment, was before the Borough Bench on •Saturday on a charge of stealing from Orford Barracks, three pairs of Army boots, two combs, a razor and shaving brush, the property of the military authorities.</p>
        <p>P.C. -John- son, of the Cheshire Lines Police, said that on the previous day he asked the de- fendant to account for his possession of the goods', but he could not do so. The case was adjourned for a week in order that inquiries should be made.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>LOITERING IN THE MARKET.</head>
        <p>LOITERING IN THE MARKET.</p>
        <p>In asking for Patrick Byrne, a single man, hailing from Ireland, to be remanded for a week, the Chief Constable at Monday's sitting of the Borough Court, said that on Sunday:night at 115, P C. Fish saw • him crouching behind a stall in tli Market. When spoken to he said he had gone there for the purpose of lying down, but the constable declared that it was impos- sible for him to do that.</p>
        <p>The charge was one of being found loitering for the purpose of committing a felony, and a remand was ordered.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>LAZY OLD MAN.</head>
        <p>A LAZY OLD MAN.</p>
        <p>Neglects His Work And Threatens</p>
        <p>His Wife.</p>
        <p>Their brogue declaring them Scots, Mrs. McArthur, of Cross-street, summoned her hus- band, John • McArthur, of 120, Longford-street, to the Police Court on Tuesday for using threats towards her.</p>
        <p>Mrs. McArthur said her husband came home drunk and threatened to stick a knife into her. The Chief Constable averred that the defend- ant was a lazy old, man, who was of intemperate habits. He was employed at a" munitions works, but was continually neglecting his work. The defendant was bound over for six_ months, one surety of ,es.being required.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>OUTBURST IN COURT.</head>
        <p>OUTBURST IN COURT.</p>
        <p>ile Who Gave Her Husband Good Hidings.</p>
        <p>"There was a certain liveliness "—to use the well-known military phrase—in the Borough Police Court on Friday morning, w4on James Tomlinson, of Orford Green, Orford, was sum- moned by his wife, Mrs. Emma Tomlinson, of Wheatfield View, Orford, for an assault alleged to have been committed on Saturday last. Mr. Arthur Browne, who represented the complainant, said that the assault was entirely unprovoked. Tomlinson went home for his dinner last Saturday afternoon, and, after he had had it, went out for a shave lie had not returned at 9.30 the same evening, so Mrs. Tomlinson and her daughter went to find him. This they did at the Orford Hotel. Mrs. Tomlinson went into the vaults for her hus- band, and, after some conversation had taken place, a stranger held her arms and said to Tomlinscrn : "Now, Jimmie, go for her "—and Jimmie did go for her, added Mr. Browne. Outside the hotel a man said to Tomlinson: " Yorrare It coward to hit a woman like that," and Tomlinson replied: "I will do it again" —and he did it again. Mrs. Tomlinson gave- evidence in support of this statement, after whioh the defendant proceeded to question her.</p>
        <p>- He asked:, Have you not laid on me many a time? '</p>
        <p>Mrs. Tomlinson: I should think so—l should think so.</p>
        <p>Airs. Tomlinson then began to shout across</p>
        <p>the Court: "You brute ! You scamp! And I would have laid on you on Saturday night if I had been able to."</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable: Wait till you get him home before you talk like that. (Laughter.) _ _ _</p>
        <p>, Mrs. Tomlinson: I won't have him home. Evidence was also given by Miss Edith Moores, the daughter of Mrs. Tomlinson. Tomlinson admitted that, when in the vaults, he hit his wife once, but said that she first pulled him on to the floor and a man ha-d to pull her off him. Tomlinson was fined 20s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>PUT HIS FOOT INTO IT.</head>
        <p>PUT HIS FOOT INTO IT.</p>
        <p>Soldier Smashes Window And Gels Imprisoned.</p>
        <p>A soldier who put his foot into the window of Mr. James Johnson's fhop at 78, Orford-lane, Warrington, doing damage to the extent of £B, had his reward at the Borough Court on Wed- nesday,- when he was sentenced to three months' imprisonment, with hard labour. But he went down hard. He was dressed in the khaki uniform of the South Lancs. Regi- ment, and gave his name as John Kelly. "It wasn't anything against the honour of the uniform," he declared. "You are a nice man to talk about honour," commented the Chief Constable, and forthwith read a long list of convictions against the prisoner in various parts of the country, where he had been known by many names. "If I don't do anything worse than break a window, I shall die happy," murmured the prisoner, and, then, turning fiercely to the Chief Constable, bawled "If people knew your charac- ter they would think you a than me. Why don't you get into khaki and do a bit, like me?"</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable: "You want to get out of khaki. That is why you act like this." The prisoner was still hurling accusations and rebukes as he was conducted to the cells below.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>BRANDY GALORE.</head>
        <p>BRANDY GALORE.</p>
        <p>London And North-Western Railway</p>
        <p>Shunters Charged With Theft.</p>
        <p>Four shunters in the employ of the London and North-Western Railway Company were in the prisoners' box at the Warrington Court on Thursday, and they were charged with committing numerous thefts at the Arpley Sidings.</p>
        <p>Thomas William Crozier, Of 24, Catherine- street, Warrington, was charged with stealing within the past twelve months one quilt, three blouses, two clothes brushes, one tin of coffee, one pillow-case, one pair of boy's knickers, a quantity of wool, two boxes of soap, a quan- tity of loose soap, the whole of which was, the property of the Railway Company and was valued at At? 10s.</p>
        <p>James Spencer Sparkes, 58, Norris-street, was .charged with having, on or about Decem- ber 21st, stolen 48 bottles of brandy, to the value of £l4 Bs.</p>
        <p>John James Smith. 155, Knutsford-road, and Benjamin. Armitage, 43, Wakefield-street, were charged with et Wing about 60 yards of blue serge cloth, valuTd at £l3 108., on December 9th.</p>
        <p>In asking for an adjournment until Tuesday next, the. Chief Constable said that as the prisoners had only been brought in that morn- ing, it would have been a physical impossi- bility to have the evidence prepared in time for that Court. The reason he asked that it should be adjourned until Tuesday next was that one of the chief officers in ,the case would he unable to attend before then.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. S. Steel, who represented Sparkes and Armitage, raised no objection to the applica- tion for adjournment, but asked for bail on behalf of his clients. He said that Sparkes was a married man with children and had been in the employ of the Railway Company for over 14 years. Armitage was also a married man and had ,been in the employ of the company for even a longelP period. They could be regarded as being highly 'respectable men. The Chief Constable replied that he strongly opposed bail. There were circumstances whith he was unable to state, but it, was desirable that bail should not be 'granted. The case was adjourned, bkil being refused.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>ROYAL MAIL'S ESCAPE.</head>
        <p>ROYAL MAIL'S ESCAPE.</p>
        <p>Molar Lorry's Obstruction XI sanhey</p>
        <p>Bridges.</p>
        <p>A policeman, discovering a motor lorry standing in Liverpool-road, Sankey Bridges at one o'clock in the morning without a rear light, went up to it, and found that the driver and the fireman were lying asleep. For obstructing the road, the driver—Henry Rowland Evans, 131, Arlington-street, Liver- pool—was ordered to pay the costs, and for having no rear light was fined is. 6d., at the Warrington County Sessions on Wednesday. The Police Superintendent said it Was a very dangerous practice indeed for . a motor lorry without a rear light to be standing in a dark place in Liverpool-road, which was continually used by various kinds of traffic. The Royal Mail had to veer sharply from its course in order to avoid a collision.</p>
        <p>_ The defenflant said that the reason they were standing there was because the bars in the fire- grate had broken. They tried to put tligm right, hut failed to do sb, and had to stay there the night. They could not help 'going to sleep, for they had been working for about forty-two hours without any break, as their firm was short-handed. The lights Went out owing to the strong wind.</p>
        <p>There is something 'Wrong Niith the. woman who prides herself on her lack of pride.</p>
        <p>lEEE</p>
        <p>MUNITIONS TRIBUNAL.</p>
        <p>Bucy Afternoon For Local Adjudicators.</p>
        <p>WORKMAN WHO WAS NOT A HELPMATE.</p>
        <p>The Warrington and District Munitions Tribunal held their longest sitting on Monday afternoon. The proceedings commenced at g.so and did not terminate :until just before five o'clock, when ten cases had been dealt with. 1 Mr. T. J. Ridgway presided and had for assessors Mr. W. Peter Rylands for the employers' panel, and Mr. R. Prest, of Wieans for the workmen's panel.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>WANTED TO ENLIST;</head>
        <p>WANTED TO ENLIST;</p>
        <p>In complaining that his certificate of dis- charge was being unreasonably withheld by a Warri'ngton firm, H. J. Challinor, of n, Elaine- street, Warrington, said that owing • to the " wetness " of the work, his shoulder caused him pain; and the night. turn did not agree with him. He produced a doctor's certificate, which stated that two months ago he was suffeting from rheumatism in his shoulder, but at the present time ". he was capable of folloFing his employment." • The complainant added that he wanted to get into the Army. He was in No. 1 Group, and it seemed as though it would be some time before it was called up.</p>
        <p>It was stated' that the Ipmplainant was engaged upon military work. Since the commencement of the war the department had lost 150 men.</p>
        <p>The Chairman pointed out that the doctor's certificate stated that the complainant was capable of following his employment, therefore the certificate would be refused.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>FRIVOLOUS OBJECTION.</head>
        <p>A FRIVOLOUS OBJECTION.</p>
        <p>Robert Simpson, a Miller at a local foundry, applied to the Tribunal for a discharge certifi- cate on the grounds that he was underpaid and could not agree with his foreman -On behalf of the firm it was stated that the applicant was a bad timekeeper, and that he took days off without leave. He was getting a shilling within the highest paid men in the works. The question of disagreement with the foreman, the firm considered to be frivolous. The applicant said that he was only getting 345. and the rate of wages for milling was 365. His position at the works was uncomfortable because he could notagree with the foreman. This last statement was denied by the fore- man, but he added that on two occasions he had renrimanded the complainant. The certificate was refused.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>LYMM MEN'S COMPLAINTS.</head>
        <p>LYMM MEN'S COMPLAINTS.</p>
        <p>Trarry Gonlden, a finisher, employed by a Lymm firm, asked for a discharge certificate en the ground that he was insufficiently employed.</p>
        <p>The manager of the firm said the real cause of the trouble was that the firm had insisted that the complainant should put a*better finish to his Work and, in consequence of this, his out- put had diminished. His application was refused. A similar application was made by Edward Rowles, another finisher, and this was also refused.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>WORK TOO HEAVY.</head>
        <p>WORK TOO HEAVY.</p>
        <p>A casual labourer employed at Ditton applied for a certificate of discharge on the ground that his work was too heavy and did not agree with his health.</p>
        <p>His foremen wrote that the firm was very short-handed, but would give the applicant his discharge as soon as they could get somebody to replace him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>REFUSED TO TRAIN ANOTHER.</head>
        <p>REFUSED TO TRAIN ANOTHER.</p>
        <p>A Prescot worker named Humphrey Dyson, complained that his employers had unreasonably refused his discharge certificate. He said that he was employed to keep mechanical instru- ments in repair, and that he had refused to train an unskilled man to do the job. He also made certain allegations respecting the foreman. The foreman denied these allegations and said that the real cause of the trouble was bad time- keeping on the part of the complainant. It had been very necessary to get a second man whom they could rely on. This man was a qualified clockmaker. but the complainant main- tained that be was unskilled.</p>
        <p>The Chairman (Mr. T. J. Riagway) commented that in the circumstances he was surprised that the firm had refused the certificate, but de- cided in their favour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>PROVED AN ALIBI.</head>
        <p>PROVED AN ALIBI.</p>
        <p>A well-known Northwich firm brought com- plaints against three of their workmen. The first was against Henry Beckett, of North- wich, likhom they charged with using abusive language and also with failing to obey lawful orders. • •</p>
        <p>It was stated that the hoistman told the fore- man that "he ought to be in front of the Ger- mans" and " that he wanted killing." The time of the offence was alleged to have been early on January 2nd, but the defendant was able to prove that he did not start work until the night of January 2nd. - The charge was dismissed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>THREATENING A FOREMAN.</head>
        <p>THREATENING A FOREMAN.</p>
        <p>Another workman, William James Egerton, also of Northwich, was charged with having threatened a foreman, and also with using abusive language. The foreman at a cooperage works stated that at 10.15 a.m. on •January 3rd the defendant eame into the cooperage. He was asked what he wanted and he replied, "What the has it to do with you?'' He also ,§aid to the fore- man; '" When _I meet you off the .works it will not take Me many minutes to do a bit for The defendant denied that he had. been abusive, but admitted saying to the foreman, "I Will settle this affair, with. you. soine night off the ,works.'' . . He was fined ss.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>BEERY SMELL</head>
        <p>A BEERY SMELL</p>
        <p>The other man, named John Gill, was chargetl with absenting himself from work for the pur- pose of obtaining liquor; and was also charged with using abusive language. A ganger said he saw the defendant go across some fields in the direction of the Farmers' Arms.</p>
        <p>The watchman spoke to seeing the defendant returning from the Farmers' Arms. When about 100 yards from the works he knelt down and broke a bottle. He smelt strongly of beer. The defendant denied the facts as stated, but he was fined 10s.</p>
        <p>MILITARY CROSS FOR LIEUT.</p>
        <p>HORNBY.</p>
        <p>Lieutenant A. Hornby, R.F.A., has been the recipient of the Military Cross. He is the son of Mr. E. C. Hornby, the well-known cricketer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>ECONOMY IN COAL.</head>
        <p>ECONOMY IN COAL.</p>
        <p>Attention is called to the advertisement of the Maximum Company as being of direct interest to householders and all coal consumers in these times of economy. The "Mushroom Spiral" is a scientific device for saving coal and at the same time conserving the maximum amount of heat. They are adaptable to all kinds of grate q• and kitcheners, require no fixing; it is simply placed when the fire is laid, and the mushroom shape keeps the fire compacts which is the secret of complete combustion.</p>
        <p>COAL IS GOLD. MUSHROOM-SPIRALS SAVE</p>
        <p>To be conservative we say, save pennies, for 18 PENCE, or post free 23 PENCE, you can have one for your 'grate or kitchener. New idea (not a fire brick or iron arrange- ment), set in centre of fire, no fixing, no trouble gives great heat, size 5 inches by ft inches.</p>
        <p>The MAXIMUM Co. 28, VICTORIA ST., LONDON, S.W.</p>
      </div>
