      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <p>BIRTHS. MARRIAGES, DEATHS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>TRADES COUNCIL'S</head>
        <p>THE TRADES COUNCIL'S</p>
        <p>DILEMMA.</p>
        <p>Last week we asked by whose authority the Trades and Labour Council ma passru a reso- lution pledging an unknown number of the trade unionists of Warrington to ignore, by opposing the Military Service Bill, the final call to arms that the Government has made in order to save the country from irretrievable calamity. So far as we can see, the Trades and Labour Council, by their gratuitously un- patriotic action, have placed themselves in a false and therefore dangerous position. The Amalgamated Wiredrawers, who have sent a large proportion of their members to the war, while those remaining at home are doing all they can to support their mates in the trenches, are " wrathful " that such a reso- lution should be passed in their name. The iron and steel workers, who, like the wire- drawing, and all other trades for that matter, have refused to stand neutral, but have re- cruited to the full extent of their power, resent this action, though secession in their case took place some time ago. Other socie- ties are moving, and it is quite possible that trouble is brewing of a kind that ought to be unnecessary in such an organisation. But whatever happens, we know the working men of Warrington will remain true to their coun- try, to their fellows, and to themselves. There can be no question of that, and if the Trades and Labour Council find ikemselves on the horns of a serious dilemma they will have the questionable satisfaction of knowing that they brought their trouble upon their own heads. For the moment we are not concerned as to the warnings to the Trades and Labour Coun- cil that appear in the interviews and letters published in to-day's "Examiner." All we are concerned about is that the working men of Warrington should repudiate the resolution the Council have passed, and refuse to allow the Council to libel them by saddling them with the responsibility for this miserable and incomprehensible pronouncement.</p>
        <p>Evidently a case where Reece sojourns to fill the In-(ner)-man.</p>
        <p>A lady was cycling round a corner some- where in Manchester-road, when she collided with a, gentleman, on Wednesday morning.</p>
        <p>Both of them were thrown to the ground and became hopelessly entangled id the cycle.</p>
        <p>Eventually it was necessary for a well- known Warrington solicitor to grasp the lady by the shoulders and shake her free from the machine.</p>
        <p>I was pleased to see the secretary, Captain James Bainbridge, at the annual meeting of the Warrington Agricultural Society on Wednesday afternoon, but regretted to notice that he has still to support himself with crutches.</p>
        <p>During his convalescence, his father, Mr. William Bainbridge, is doing the secretarial work for the society.</p>
        <p>There was rather an amusing incident at the meeting. Mr. Bainbridge had stated that many members had not yet sent in their sub- scriptions, whereupon Mr. J. Ball expressed the hope that all would .do so immediately. He was about to• resume his seat when he added, " I don't know whether I have paid or not. I have forgotten, but I ought to have paid."</p>
        <p>Mr. Bainbridge nodded that he had paid, and Mr. Ball sat down amidst the laughter of those who enjoyed his intenuousness.</p>
        <p>Our good friend, Mr. George Edgar, has a characteristic article in the "Advertisers' Weekly," in which he gives some of his early impressions of public vendors of cures of various kinds at Warrington.</p>
        <p>He seems to have been particularly im- pressed with Sequah, the North American Indian, who was "content 1' with a perfect frock coat, irreproachable linen, a copper- coloured face, glowing brown eyes, and long raven-black hair.</p>
        <p>Besides selling remedies, Sequah " usM to draw the teeth of his audience. It is surpris- ing how many people had hollow teeth in Lan- cashire and were ready to have then yanked out publicly, to the delight of their grinning neighbours. Sequah professed painless dentistry, but some of his patients looked thoughtful when he had tugged at their aban- doned molars. And always the band played." VIGILANT.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>AUSTRALIAN LABOUR.</head>
        <p>AUSTRALIAN LABOUR.</p>
        <p>It is not necessary to say that Labour the world over is in favour of doing all that can be done to finish off the war soon and satis- factorily; but the following gust of fresh air from "down 'under" may have a purifying significance at the present juncture:—</p>
        <p>Mr. Arthur Henderson, President of the Board of Bducation, has received the fol- lowing cablegram through the Agent-General for New South Wales from the Labour Prime Minister of that State: "New South Wales Cabinet desire me to send heartiest congratulations on your determination to remain in Ministry. . Of ten Labour Minis- ters seven have individually declared favour- ing compulsory service during war. Wish you unbounded success." •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>WHAT MORE IS NEEDED?</head>
        <p>WHAT MORE IS NEEDED?</p>
        <p>This week in the House of Commons IV Walter Long said</p>
        <p>Lord Kitchener authorises me to say that this Bill, by bringing in the unmarried men and therefore adding all the others, will give him the men he requires, will enable him to provide the troops that the nation requires, and will enable him to do all that he can and all that is necessary to be done— to use his own words— In order to secure a victory."</p>
        <p>Who will be against giving Lord Kitchener all that is necessary to secure victory, when the limitatiohs of that " all " are specifically stated, and can be easily metP</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
