      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 22, 1916.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>The No-Conscriptionists Were to have had dinner-hour meetings on Wednesday at the Ropery and Dallam• orge, but, for some reason or other, they were not held, and the workmen had no post-prandial speeches and toasts to Anti-Compulsionist leaders.</p>
        <p>It has been announced that a Member of Parliament is likely to speak in Warrington on their behalf, but I understand that one hall at least has been refused. Will the meeting be held?</p>
        <p>I am told that Mr. John Haley, who repre- sented the wiredrawers on the Trades Council and voted for the NO-Conscription resolution, has sent in his resignation as a delegate on their behalf.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable (Mr. Martin Nidiolls) Las a gift for repartee.</p>
        <p>At the Police Court on Wednesday a small</p>
        <p>boy was charged with stealing 4s. 2(1. "It was only tuppence haipney," the youngster kept repeating between his sobs, and at last the Chief Constable halted in his speech with the remark: "He remembers the old price of a pint all right."</p>
        <p>Military procedure and civil authority came into opposition at the Borough Police Court on Thursday morning.</p>
        <p>A soldier was about to enter the Court with his cap on, and accordingly he was asked to remove it.</p>
        <p>He refused, pointing out that in a mili- tary court a soldier had to keep his head covered.</p>
        <p>But he was reminded that he was in the Warrington Police Station, and his cap was removed for him by the Chief Constable.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <p>,After a big break by Reece, the famous bil- liard player, at the Conservative Club on Friday night of last week, it was announced -that there would be an interval for refresh- ments for half an hour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>33 YEARS' SERVICE.</head>
        <p>33 YEARS' SERVICE.</p>
        <p>Late Nlaster And Matron Of Blue Coa School •Receive Presentation:</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. Rose, who recently retired from the position of master and matron at the Blue Coat School, were presented with a massive silver salver and silver inkstand at the con- clusion of the trustees' meeting on Monday. The ceremony took place in the :dining hall in the presence of the children, and there was a large attendance of trustees. On the salver there was inscribed :—" Pre- sented to Mr. and Mrs. Edward Rose by the trustees of the Warrington Blue Coat School. in grateful recognition of their thirty-three years' service as master and matron, 1882- 1915," with facsimile signatures of the trustees. Sir Gilbert Greenall, on behalf of the trustees, congratulated Mr. and Mrs. Rose on their long and valued iervices. Their retirement was a great break, yet it must be extremely gratifying for them to look back upon their successful career at the school where they had turned out very many excellent scholars. They took with them the best wishes of the trustees for a long Iperiod of happiness in their retirement. Mr. Rose, in thanking the trust'ees for their ' handsome present, said that throughout their 331 years at the school, they had always received kindness and consideration at the hands of the trustees, and the sympathy and encouragement had ever stimulated them to do their best for the Institution. They .were extremely proud to have succeeded in gaining and retaining for so many years the confidence and esteem of the trustees, and this personal recognition would always he valued most highly, and treasured as hang as they lived, and would be shown with pride to their children, end retained as A, cherished possession. Of those gentlemen who were trustees at the date of their appointreent, only one (Mr. J. Charl- ton Parr) now remained, but he hoped that the present trustees would long be spared to carry on the good work of the institu- tion, and he wished the school every success under Mr. and Mrs. Weston, the new master and matron.</p>
        <p>The headmaster, at the request of the trustees, granted the children a day's holiday to commemorate the occasion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>LABOURERS' RISE.</head>
        <p>LABOURERS' RISE.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the unskilled mid semi-skilled labourers in the, engineering trades in Warring- ton and district was held in the Socialist Hall. Bridge Foot, last night, to consider the award of Sir G. Askwith in regard to the wages of men in the Manchester area, which includes South- West Lancashire. The. new scale was to come into operation on January 11th. The 3s. war bonus was converted into an advance of 45., thus bringing the wages of labourers in War- rington up to 265. per week. _ .</p>
        <p>deci-sion WflS arrived at.'A-further meet- ing is to be held.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>WIREDRAWERS SAY "NOT FOR US."</head>
        <p>WIREDRAWERS SAY "NOT FOR US."</p>
        <p>OTHERS MAY FOLLOW.</p>
        <p>Last week the Warrington Trades and Labour Council passed the following resolution:—</p>
        <p>"That this Council protests against the insidious attempt to impose conscription upon the country, believing its promoters to be more anxious to Prussianise and enslave the workers than to Lace the question of military necessity consistent with the economic and industrial obligations due to our Allies, • "The ulterior motives of the conscription- ists are too obviously intended to cripple trade unionism, split the unity of the workers, and bring about industrial feudal- ism by pseudo methods: (1) the securing of compulsion of single men. This accom- plished, their insatible desire will then be extended to the married until the masses have been dragooned into an hegemony of militarism comparable only to the ostenta- tious Prussianism we are now, fighting to subdue.</p>
        <p>"We pledge ourselves to oppose any and every form of compulsion, to the utmost of our power, an call upon all free men to</p>
        <p>rally to the movement for its resistance." "The Examiner" ventured to ask for whom did the Trades and Labour Council speak; and the question has had a lively effect upon the Trade Unionists of the town. ;We give some opinions below, but others are it the making, and will be known in due course.</p>
        <p>Iron And Steel Workers Repudiate The Soft Impeachment.</p>
        <p>Of course, it must be admitted that before the war the great majority of working men were opposed to compulsory military service, but the circumstances have changed, and the Trades Council had no mandate to assert that the workers still stand for a principle which the Government avers can no longer be relied upon to steer us out of grave and perilous con- ditions."</p>
        <p>E)OSTMEN TO HOLD A MEETING.</p>
        <p>We are informed that the Postmen's Federa- tion (Warrington and District Branch) are to hold a meeting on Wednesday to discuss the Trades Council's resolution.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>Wiredrawers May Withdraw From Membership.</head>
        <p>Wiredrawers May Withdraw From Membership.</p>
        <p>SUPPORT FOR THE GOVERNMENT.</p>
        <p>Contending that the anti-Government views of the Warrington Trades and Labour Council are contrary to the national interests, the Execu- tive Committee of the Amalgamated Wire- drawers' Society of Great Britain, Warrington district, have resolved that a resolution severing their connection with the Council be discussed at a meeting to be held to-day. As soon as the Trades Council declaration against the Compulsory Service Bill was known a meeting was arranged and held on Monday night, when the opinion was unanimously agreed to that the views of the Council generally, and especially the views expressed at their last meeting, were contrary to those held by the great majority of the members of the Wire- drawers' Society. There are about a thousand members in the branch. They are connected with the Whitecross Company, Rylands's, Houghton's, and the Firth Company, Ltd. "You may take it from me that the resolu- tion will be passed," remarked the secretary, Mr. Arthur Siddall, to a representative of "The Exanainer," who called on him and asked him to explain the committee's drastic action. "It isn't a one-horse affair. All except about twO members of the committee—and there are twenty-three of us—were present. We had to hold a meeting; it was practically forced on us. If we had not had a meeting of the committee, the members generally would have had one on their own, as they are very wrathful at being associated with such unpatriotic views as are contained in the extraordinary resolution passed by the Trades Council. Some time ago, when the Council declined</p>
        <p>to assist recruiting under the voluntary scheme, we protested, and called one of our representa- tives—Mr. John Haley—to give an account of himself.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>CLIMAX.</head>
        <p>THE CLIMAX.</p>
        <p>"This last act of the Council's is the climax, however, and we are determined to sever our connection with them for the duration of the ' war. It is a great pity, of course, and we have not decided to do this without deep considera- tion, for the Council has done some good work in the past, but, to-day,.it seems to be a hind- rance to the good of both working men and the country. "What hurts us particularly at this time is that anyone should not do his best towards en- larging the Army. From our society in War- rington alone as many as 120 members had joined the Forces up to August* and we are not going to neglect doing what we can for them in the way of sending more men along to help them. But instead of giving every assistance to provide ways and means of carrying the war to a successful issue, the effect of the Trades Council's actions is more likely to lend assist- ance to the other side." _</p>
        <p>_ "How was it your representatives' did not speak or vote against the resolution moved at the Trades Council meeting?" we inquired. "We hacks four representatives. One—Mr. John Haley—was present, and the other three, unfortunately, were unable to go. Mr. Haley seems to have spoken as strongly as anyone for the resolution against the Bill. That is against our wishes, for I can say that 99 per cent. of our members fall in with the views of the Gov- ernment. What he should have done was to have ascertained our views on a matter of 60 grave importance before voting for an un- patriotic resolution like that. We have sent him a notification of Saturday's meeting, and if he comes he will certainly be asked for an ex- planatib</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>IRON AND STEEL WORKERS.</head>
        <p>IRON AND STEEL WORKERS.</p>
        <p>Support The Government In These</p>
        <p>Exceptional Circumstances.</p>
        <p>When our representative called on Mr. Joseph Elwell to ascertain the views of the iron and steel workers, that gentleman expressed his gladness at being given an opportunity of dis- sociating himself and those engaged in his trade from the hostile attitude of the Trades Council to the Government's Bill. Mr. Elwell is Warrington's member of the Council of the Associated Iron and Steel Workers' Union, and declares that the vast majority of the 2,000 members in the town are in favour of Mr. Asquith's pledge being carried but. "We are not affiliated with the Trades Council," he added. "We seceded a year or two ago. because of it being dominated by a Socialist section. Naturally, we are up against that. We contended that a Trades Council should be a Labour organisation pure and simple, tainted with no political creed what- ever. So we left them. If we had remained affiliated, I am sure that their most recent action—a disloyal one, to say the least—would have caused us to leave them. In my view, and from the conversations I have had with many working men, the Trades Council reso- lution represents a contrary feeling to that held by the men themselves. Now that Mr. Asquith has given a pledge that industrial compulsion shall not be introduced, nearly all iron and steel workers are for the Compulsory Military Service Bill—certainly the great bulk are with the Government—and will do everything they can to assist them in bringing the war to a successful issue. We support compulsion for the period of the war, of course. I think the wiredrawers are doing right in leaving the Council' as a protest against an absolutely dis- loyal act.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>Workmen Not Consulted.</head>
        <p>Workmen Not Consulted.</p>
        <p>"The Trades Council professes to represent the working men, yet over this matter they have never consulted them. Certainly, there was no time for a consultation before the Trades Congress met, but for their subsequent action there is no excuse. Why was it not suggested, when the strong resolution against compulsion was introduced, that before voting on it the delegates should talk it over with the members of their trade? They, gave ,a vote on behalf of men whom they had not consulted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>TRADES AND LABOUR COUNCIL AND THE WAR.</head>
        <p>THE TRADES AND LABOUR COUNCIL AND THE WAR.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—The whole community, and not the least, the Trade Unionists of Warrington, owe you a debt of gratitude for your trenchant and able exposure of the bombastic claims of this loquacious body. 'Those who have been closely associated for many years with the genuine Trade Unionism of Warrington know full well how frequently the real sense and feeling of Warrington workers have been misrepresented by this pretentious and self-seeking syndicate. The attitude of the members of the Trades and Labour Council in their utter disregard of the thousands of their fellow workmen who have faced and still are facing death and wounds and discomfort indescribable in order to save Eng- lish, women from worse than death, and English . children from ruthless murder, is not merely unpatriotic and contemptible, but is positively inhuman. The attitude of mind which begets such an outlook is almost inconceivable—but as you have said in the article which you pub- lished on Saturday last, and which I hope has been read by every man amongst us—" there it is," and it is necessary to deal with it. Who and what sort of men ( ?) are they who rule the roost on this local gasometer? One has heard of the "Three Tailors of Tooley-street," and verily their " prototypes " yet exist. It is quite certain, however, that the blatant pro- nouncement recently issued in condemnation of the "Compulsion Bill," coming as it has done on the top of more than one " indiscretion " of a kindred character, has stung and irritated the real, genuine Trade Unionists of the town be- yond endurance, and we shall have unmistaken evidence before long of the feeling and senti- ments of the great bulk of the working men of Warrington who are sick ..to death of this travesty, of a local Parliament. That there are honourable and capable men on the " Council " is indisputable but, they are not the men who mould its policy—this is propounded by a few brass-lunged, conceited, cranky Ishraaelites, who have never done any good either for them- selves or anybody else, and their claim to represent 10,000 of the workmen of Warrington is as impudent as it is false. Thousands of Warrington workmen have joined the Colours—how many members of the Trades and Labour Council have done so?</p>
        <p>W•farington workmen have contributed most genel•ously to the various funds which the war has brought into existence. Row many members of he Trades and Labour Council have contributed a single penny to such funds?</p>
        <p>We know from their own lips that they are " neutral " in regard to the war, and for re- cruiting they have done nothing. What sort of hearts have these men (F) to proclaim them- selves " neutral " almost within hearing of the cries of drowning women and God's little ones, who were sent to their deaths by the dastardly pirates when the Lusitania was destroyed— " neutral " when one of England's best and bravest and purest women, whose life work had been given to suffering humanity, was foully murdered in Belgium. " Neutral " while Belgian and French women and girls were violated and murdered. " Neutral " when our Canadian brothers came thousands of miles to help in this terrible struggle, and faced and suffered the horrors of "crucifixion," Sir, such " neutrality " is loathsome, and as the great Australian Labour Minister said a few days ago, between such neutrality and the real feeling and sentiment of honest labour there is a "gulf as wide and as deep as the mouth of hell."</p>
        <p>Yours faithfully. A WORKER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>LORD DERBY AND COMPULSION</head>
        <p>LORD DERBY AND COMPULSION</p>
        <p>Croup System Prevented Total Conscription.</p>
        <p>JUST FAILED TO SAVE VOLUNTARYISM</p>
        <p>Lord Derby, speaking at the annual meeting, of the Lancashire Division of the National Unionist Association in Liverpool on Saturday, pointed out that everything possible had been done to vindicate the voluntary system of recruiting and to avert compulsion. The scheme had only just fallen short of success. But if the group system had not been adopted we should have been confronted a few weeks back with a position far worse than we were in to-day. It would not then have been com- pulsion with limitations, but compulsion throughout the land, and without the know- ledge of the reserves at our disposal. Sir John Simon's plea for further investigations meant further delay, delay that would do no good ex- cept to our enemies. What they wanted was recruits at once, so that the war could be carried on as quickly as possible to a victorious issue. He appealed to them and to all other parties in the State to assist in recruiting. A resolution moved by Sir Charles Petrie was carried, urging the Government to take any necessary steps to bring the war to a speedy and successful cOnclusion, and pledging the meeting to give whole-hearted support to the Govern- ment in any action they might take to attain that object.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>WARRINGTON DRAPER.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON DRAPER.</p>
        <p>Twelve Months' Imprisonment For Receiving.</p>
        <p>Grave charges of stealing and receiving were made at the Manchester Sessions on Thursday against a well-dressed man, Harry Maxwell Comley (44), draper, of Downing-street, Man- chester, and of Queen-street, Warrington, as a result of which he was sentenced to twelve months' imprisonment. It was proved that the police had found large quantities of stolen goods on the prisoner's premises. Prisoner, who denied that he had knowledge that the goods were stolen, was defended by Mr. Acton and Mr. Jackson. Mr. Gilbert Jordan, with whom was Mr. Sandbach, prosecuted, and opening the ease to the jury, said the prisoner was charged with stealing 130 blouses, and also with receiving them, knowing they were stolen. Prior to passing sentence, the Recorder said he felt bound to pass a sentence which showed he appreciated that the prisoner had been pro- perly convicted of it most dangerous form of crime. When a receiver was found and was convicted it was always of importance that one should emphasise the evil that they did to the community. He thought the right sentence to pass was twelve months' imprisonment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>• • .., ~,, , • ' ' ' -',:,'.. -',.tt..,_ • ..--.,-. -' a ..:„.„.., . ____ , I --,.....,,.-.....„, r 7 .. _ . , rifiW ' `, /,' `'..l.** .. -' ' • .. ' ..- -tt.: .' lir ----`-.*. .. • -,..:,' . – ....:,..,.. ‘;‘', . -*---.-: "r' -,. 0. 1 ,-, • ~,,, .....,I, – .. • 4 . ' ...- ~,' -C, .teri,.-:. ,?...:,..A 4: ./.1a,..., .;,,',146.:. ~ . k..;:, • – •, . ....:-. – „... . • ,„„ •,..,..,,, . • ~ . , . ''''. – ',.P. "" • .4'</head>
        <p>• • .., ~,, , • ' ' ' -',:,'.. -',.tt..,_ • ..--.,-. -' a ..:„.„.., . ____ , I --,.....,,.-.....„, r 7 .. _ . , rifiW ' `, /,' `'..l.** .. -' ' • .. ' ..- -tt.: .' lir ----`-.*. .. • -,..:,' . - ....:,..,.. ‘;‘', . -*---.-: "r' -,. 0. 1 ,-, • ~,,, .....,I, - .. • 4 . ' ...- ~,' -C, .teri,.-:. ,?...:,..A 4: ./.1a,..., .;,,',146.:. ~ . k..;:, • - •, . ....:-. - „... . • ,„„ •,..,..,,, . • ~ . , . ''''. - ',.P. "" • .4'</p>
        <p>LAICAL I TELLIGENCE.</p>
        <p>Acknowledgment.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wm. It. Laws, the Police Court missioner,. has received a parcel of clothing from the West Cheshire Guild, per Miss S. Broadbent.</p>
        <p>Fishing Ticket Money for Infirmary.</p>
        <p>The Boil. Treasurer, Mr:Roger. C. Parr, tiegs to acknowledge, with thanks, the gift by Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., C.V.0., of the sum of £l9 14s. 6d., being proceeds of sale of tickets for fishing in the Appleton Reservoir for 1915.</p>
        <p>Against Compulsion. A strongly-worded resolution against the Army Service Bill now before Parliament was unanimously passed by the Warrington Human- ity League Committee at a meeting held on Monday night; and the secretary was instructed to send copies to the Prime Minister, the Secre- tary of State for War, the Minister of Muni- tions, and the Press.</p>
        <p>Warrington Market Prices. At Wednesday's Warrington Market the price of wheat ruled from Bs. 6d. to Bs. 9d. per 701 b., and 4s. Gd. to 4s. 9d. per 451 b. was raised for oats. Straw brought 675. 6d., and oat bales 70s. to 725. 6d. per ton. Flour realised 495. to 535. per 2801 b., 2s. 21d. to 2s. 41d. being the price of thirds. The price of bran and other Ads was is. 10?„d. to 2s. Old.</p>
        <p>The New Archdeacon of Warrington. To-day the Bishop of Liverpool will, at the Palace, collate Archdeacon Spooner to the Archdeaconry of Liverpool on his resignation of the Afchdeaconry of Warrington and the Rev. Canon Howson (son of the late Dean Howson of Chester). rector of Woolton, to the Arch- deaconry of Warrington. Canon Bilbrough will instal the new archdeacons at the 5 p.m. cathedral service.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>Grappenhall Nursing Association. – –</head>
        <p>Grappenhall Nursing Association. - -</p>
        <p>The ilinual meeting of tire Grappenhall Dis- trict Nursing Association will be held at Birchdale, Grappenhall (by kind permission of Miss Appleton) on Wednesday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>Lvmm Armleteers.</head>
        <p>Lvmm Armleteers.</p>
        <p>To men who produce their white cards at Lynim Conservative Club Assembly Room on Monday and Wednesday evenings, will be given armlets. It is hoped that all who are entitled to them will present themselves.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>Stockton Heath Garden Society.</head>
        <p>Stockton Heath Garden Society.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Stockton heath Garden Society will be held in the Church Room, Victoria-square, on Friday evening. ThP chair will be taken by the President of the Association, Sir Gilbert Greenall</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>Patriotism and Pleasure.</head>
        <p>Patriotism and Pleasure.</p>
        <p>Local whist players and dancers will have an opportunity next Thursday evening of com- bining patriotism with pleasure. By attend- ing the whist drive and dance to be held at the Parr Hall on behalf of the Thelwall Heys Military Convalescent Home they will be doing a good turn to the wounded soldiers, besides spending a pleasant evening. By kind per- mission of Colonel Moggridge, the Band of the 2nd South Lancashire Regiment will play for dancing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>Mayor Compliments Famous Billiard</head>
        <p>Mayor Compliments Famous Billiard</p>
        <p>Player.</p>
        <p>After the termination of the billiard match and exhibition played at the Conservative Club on Friday evening' of last week, by Mr. T. Reece, the famous Lancashire billiard expon- ent, and others, the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock) spoke in glowing terms of Mr. Reece's generosity in visiting the town on behalf of charity. He also congratulated the sporting fraternity on their good work accomplished during the present conflict.</p>
        <p>Belgian Artistes In Warrington. An array of Belgian artistes are going to sing and play at the Royal Court Theatre on Sunday (January 30th) evening, and music- lovers of Warrington are in for a real treat. The concert is arranged by the British Red Cross Society and the funds will be devoted to the relief of the Belgian wounded. The artistes who will appear are: Mme. Rizzini, soprano; Mme. Van Hoe, soprano; Mrs. Armand Blackley, mezzo soprano; M. Adrien Van Roey, tenor; M. Colbert, baritone; M. Camby, violinist; M. Caveye, 'cellist; and M. Paul Books, conductor.</p>
        <p>Warrington Anglers' Association. In the 25th annual report of the Warrington Anglers' Association, just presented, the com- mittee state that they have emerged from a very trying year in a manner eminently satis- factory to the members and themselves. At the end of 1914 there was, owing to enlistment and other causes, the large default of £43 Os. 2d.. which has seriously hampered them during the past year. The default for 1915 amounts to about £25, but the committee, by judicious management, have reduced the ex- penditure in proportion, and this leaves them in a position to carry forward a. balance of £l5 9s. id. The committee also regret to re- cord the death of several supporters of the association, many of whom have died for King and country.</p>
        <p>Presentation of Ambulance Certificates</p>
        <p>The undermentioned were awarded the St. John Ambulance First Aid Certificate on Friday evening last week at Cairo-street School :—H. H. Rook, R. N. Tears, C. Sharples, F. Hope, T. Southern, 11. Ashbrooke, N. Shaw, H. Gregory, A. Davis, S. B. Wilde, W. Lawton, P. Phillips, A. Finch, J. Clarke, J. S. Jenks, P. Graham, H. Parr and A. J. Manssuer. After the presenta- tion of certificates, Mr. Percy Graham, on behalf of the class, asked Dr. Manson to accept a silver- plated biscuit jar as a small token of apprecia- tion of his services as lecturer, and Mr. Arthur Davis at the same time handed the instructor, Supt. Baldry, a beautiful silver-mounted um- brella. Seven of the above members,are already serving in Lord Derby's Military Hospital in the R.A.M.C. and one has enlisted in the Tine.</p>
        <p>Golborne-street Baptists' Tea and Entertainment. A goodly number of scholars were in attend- ance at the Golborne-street Baptist Church Schoolroom on Thursday evening, on the occa- sion of their annual tea and entertainment. The entertainment was presided over by the Rev. H. Ogden (pastor). The first item, a pianoforte solo, was well rendered by May Ward, while recitations by Winnie Jones, Elsie Clare, Miss Tomlin and M. Freeman were all thoroughly enjoyed. Beatty Bickers gave a pianoforte solo in good style, while Ruth Ward and Herbert Speakman rendered songs accept- ably. Two action-songs in character by the children were beautifully rendered, one of which, "Saucy Sailor-boys," was particularly enjoyed. During the evening a pleasant little cere- mony was performed, in the form of a pre- sentation to Mrs. Fletcher, a Sunday school teacher, who has recently been married. The presentation was made by Miss Balmford. The Secretary (Mr. T. Longshaw) presented his report, which showed that the past year had been satisfactory, there now being 200 scholars on, the books. Towards the close of the evening Mrs. A. S. Elliott presented the prizes to scholars for regular Sunday school attendance.</p>
        <p>Shipping Obstacles in Leather Trade. The Warrington correspondent of the "Leather Trades Review" says that last week inquiry was all the time heavier than the supply, and in many lines tanners have not only cleared out finished stocks, but have also</p>
        <p>boiled up for Some months ahead. There is no doubt forward contracting could be very much enlarged did not tanners, feel rather nervous regarding their power to supply. In some contracts a clause is now being . added that execution is only guaranteed provided it is possible to obtain adequate supplies of tan- ning materials. The main obstacle in the way of ensuring this appears to be the congestion of shipping, as American makers claim they are in a position tq make full deliveries, but the shipping companies will not accept them; the position is an intolerable one, and obviously an efficient and quick remedy would be for the Government to regard tanning extract as war supplies, and have it shipped by " com- mandeered " boats; certainly it is no use " badgeing " the tannery workers if they are to be held- up for raw supplies. Prices are agitated, offers at "last figure" are oftener than not declined, and in many direc- tions good advances have been already made. It appears reasonably certain the upward move- ment will be regularly progressive and may continue for some time, and, whilst our own and allied Governments' requirements will be protected as to price by agreement, this will not be operative as regards all outside trade, and the very magnitude of the former .will tend towards short supplies and high prices for the ordinary trade.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>DEAFNESS AND BLINDNESS.</head>
        <p>DEAFNESS AND BLINDNESS.</p>
        <p>Special Visit To Warrington, Monday, January,3lg.</p>
        <p>Mr. Thomas Ison, the eminent oculisf. and aurist, of Ison's Eye and Ear Dispensary, Ltd. Leeds, has arranged to visit' the Lion f.i.)tel Warrington, on Monday, January 31st, frxn 11 to 2; Laurence's Hotel, Clayton-square, Liver- pool, on Monday, January 31st, hours 3.30 to 8, and may be consulted on all diseases of the eye and ear. Sufferers from failing eyesight and hear- ing should avail themselves of the opportunity of consultation. Special home treatment. No ear drums, ear cornets, no electric batteries, no painful appliances. Spectacles for weak and ' defective sight. Those unable to avail them- selves of a personal consultation should write for printed form of questions to be answered, sent free. Letters to 71, Great George-street, Leeds.—[Advt.)</p>
        <p>Not A Subject. Sir Francis Burnand, the late editor of "Punch," was requested one night in com- pany to make a pun extempore. "Upon what subject?" asked Burnand. "The King," was suggested. "Oh, sir," he replied, "the King is no sub ject.m</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>Balloting for Quartet Parties.</head>
        <p>Balloting for Quartet Parties.</p>
        <p>In connection with the Brick-street Mission Choir, a very enjoyable tea and social took place on Thursday in the Schoolroom. The choir, with invited friends to the number of 100, partook of an excellent tea. Subsequently the time was spent in a manner enjoyable to all in games, &amp;c. A very interesting and unique item was the balloting of the choir to constitute quartet parties to engage in a contest for prizes, the piece to be selected and rendered impromptu. The contest created great interest and proved a special item of pleasure both to the choir and friends.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>Musical Service at Legh-st. Primitive</head>
        <p>Musical Service at Legh-st. Primitive</p>
        <p>Methodist Chapel. The Legh-street Primitive Methodist Chapel anniversary was held on Sunday, when the special preacher for the day was Councillor D. Lewis, of Widnes. A musical service was held in the afternoon, being presided over by Mr. J. B. Starkey, who gave an interesting address. Solos were beauti- fully rendered by Mrs. A. E. Hoyle, Mrs. C. Worthington, Miss Maud Jones, Miss Petitt, and Mr. J. Dakin. Several anthems and choruses- were efficiently rendered by the choir. At the evening service, which was particu- larly well attended, the choir gave a good inter- pretation of Gounod's anthem, "By Babylon's Wave." Mr. F. H. Banner presided at the organ, and Mr. A. Spruce conducted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>Deaths From Pneumonia.</head>
        <p>Deaths From Pneumonia.</p>
        <p>In Warrington there were 30 deaths during the week ended January 15th. This is above the general average, but two fewer than in the corresponding week in January last year. An un- usual feature of ' the week's record is that while only 3 children under one year of age died and none between one and five, there .are no fewer than 16 of persons between the ages of 25 and 65, and eight of persons over, 65. Pneumonia was responsible for six deaths and bronchitis for two. Other causes were :—Phthisis, 2; other tuberculous disease, 1; cancer, 2; heart disease, 2; accidents; 2; all other causes, 12. Eleven new cases of measles and six of scarlet fever were reported. Births numbered 32, against 38 in the cor- responding period last year. Children's Concert for Soldiers' and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>Sailors' Comforts.</head>
        <p>Sailors' Comforts.</p>
        <p>The children of St. Ann's School gave their second concert on Friday in aid of the fund for providing comforts for soldiers and sailors. A packed audience Thoroughly enjoyed the songs and dances of the children, who had been well trained by their teacher, Mr. W. H. Rigby. The programme included many costume songs and dances, many of which had to be repeated. Two dances " 1816 " and " 1916 " (encored) by Bessie Derbyshire and Jas. Lockett were grace- fully performed. The singing of Annie Lawless and Alec Evans was much appreciated. John Smith gave a recitation entitled "Water Scenes," and caused roars of laughter. Miss Minnie Houghton danced the sailors' hornpipe and her fine display gained for her a well- earned encore. The veteran entertainer, Mr-. R. Scarisbrick, an old boy of the school, gave two of his well-known humorous selections, and Miss Elsie Hewitt delighted all by her singing ,Nc the "Call of the Maytime " and "Maying." Mr. Ernest Hannan was the accompanist. £6 was realised for the funds through this effort.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>Memorial Service to Mrs. John Hassall.</head>
        <p>Memorial Service to Mrs. John Hassall.</p>
        <p>"We speak of an inspired book and an inspired pen, so why should we not speak of an inspired needle?" These words formed part of an address delivered by Councillor George Dale at the Working Men's Mission Church on Sunday evening, when a memorial service was held for the late Mrs. John Hassell, who, for many years, was housekeeper and cook for Sir Arthur and Lady Crosfield at their Warrington residence. Mr. T. Dale con- ducted the opening portion of the service and in the course of a brief address referred to Mrs. .Hassall's association with the Ssociety Class, and her influence among its members. Councillor 0. Dale said that in the death of Mrs. Hassan, the Mission had lost, as it were, -one of its pillars. Born in South Staffordshire in 1851, she went as a girl to Kidsgrove, from where, after many years of useful work in the village, she came with her husband to War- rington. For over 40 years she had laboured incessantly for the many organisations con- nected with the Bank Quay Mission. There was one department, perhaps, in which she had excelled, and that was the one in which she brought her needle into full play. She was a genius with her needle, and her efforts in this direction were not limited to her own church, for, broadminded in all that she undertook, Mrs, Hassell did not hesitate to do whatever she could for social, political and patriotic movements. In her life was repre= sented the bright and cheerful type of a- Christian, and he could not help but feel con- vinced that it was impossible to conceive an end to pure virtue and goodness, and, there- fore, it pointed to an ,immortality for such characteristics. Her life had been an inspira- tion to them all, and, in conclusion, the speaker urged his 'hearers to strive to follow in her footsteps, and thus in the morning of the new day they would meet her again.—During the service special hymns were sung by a large congregation, and a solo entitled "In Summer Land," was rendered by Mr. H. Peden. At the close the i Dead 'March n Saul" was played by the organist, Mr. F. Atkinson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>Musical Successes. Victoria College.</head>
        <p>Musical Successes. Victoria College.</p>
        <p>In connection with the above College, an ex- amination was recently held in Manchester, and the following pupils of Miss Clarice Daly, A.V.C.M., 76, Wilderspool-road, passed success- fully in pianoforte playing :—Preparatory Grade: Annie Davenport, St. Mary's-street, 82 marks, Ist class with honours. Primary Grade: Lavinia Farrington, Arpley-street, 90 marks, Ist class honours. Junior Grade: Doris Massey, Lower Walton, 88 marks, Ist class. honours. Intermediate Grade: Amy Jolley, St. Austin's- lane, 93 marks, Ist class with honours.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>London College.</head>
        <p>London College.</p>
        <p>In connection with the above College an ex- amination was held in the Philharmonic Hall, Liverpool, and the following pupils passed suc- cessfully in pianoforte playing and theory com- bined :—Primary Grade: Lucy Ashley, Manley House, Sankey, 83 marks, Ist class. Intermedi- ate Grade: Lily Sharpies, Algernon-street, 79 marks, Ist class. The above pupils were, also prepared by Miss Clarice Daly, Wilderapool- road.</p>
      </div>
