      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>Call For Unity And Support For</head>
        <p>Call For Unity And Support For</p>
        <p>The Government.</p>
        <p>NO FACTIOUS CRITICISM.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>41, West-hill, Highgate, N., January 20th, 1916.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Lord Derby's earnest message in some of this morning's papers is both friendly counsel and a timely reminder for those eligible un- married men and widowers without children from 19 to 41 years of age who have so far failed to attest, that it is still open to them to escape the fate of the unfortunate remnant who would afterwards come under the operation of the Compulsory Service Bill. Taking all the circumstances into account, one does feel that every consideration, alike of interest and of honour, is now calculated to induce those con- cerned to act upon Lord Derby's sound, and in- deed unanswerable advice.</p>
        <p>That the •pledge to married men should be unredeemed, or the War Office denied a single man who, in the opinion of the Board of Trade, Scan be spared from service at home, and who the military authorities consider necessary for the support of those already at the Front, and the successful conduct of the war, is unthink- able.. No one whose Opinion is for a moment wor.th listening fo would challenge that pro- position. And those are the dominant facts we have to keep steadily before us. Differences of opinion there may be as to whether this method or that is the best way of achieving the main object in. view. But 'these questions of method, however important in time of peace are, comparatively speaking, of altogether minor importance in time of war. Spetrking for myself, I have not seen any reason in what has happened during the last fortnight to alter the opinions expressed in the communication I then sent you, and which you were good enough to publish in your issue of Saturday, the Bth. Similar views were held by • not a few of those who none the less voted in ,support of the Government. I should be the last to criticise such votes. On the contrary, if may be allowed to quote from a letter written to a member of the Catiinet the morning after The Division on the First Beading (that is.to say on Friday 'the 7th), I observed in this com- munication that:—</p>
        <p>"Although °lie of those who wished that before the final measure of Compulsion was resorted to, single eligible men should be required to state publicly before their local tribunals their reason for not offering their services, none the less I am as devoutly thankful as any one that the Government retains the strength sof its position and authority. I hope with all my heart it will continue to do so till the successful conclusion of the war."</p>
        <p>In time of peace criticism is of the very essence of our system of Parliamentary Govern- ment. And even in time of war, within the limits of patriotism and good sense, criticism may do much to serve the National and the Allied cause. But sane criticism is one thing; factious opposition quite another. It is of the highest importance at the present time that the authority of the Government should be main- tained. As Sir George Reid put it in his admir- able Maiden Speech on Tuesday • :- "The glory of the Imperial Parliament in time ot peace was its fearless inquiry into abuses and its redress of grievances; but when the flames of destruction were approaching the fabric of our liberties, let , us ,save our house first and discuss our domestic rearrangements afterwards."</p>
        <p>' Those who are inclined to concentrate too ex- clusively their attention on mistakes that have been made during the war will do well to bear in mind the immense achievements which have be4en accomplished by the British .Empire since the war broke out, and at the same time the huge burden of responsibility :and mass of in- creased work which Ministers carry on their shoulders. In that connection,', perhaps, I may 'fittingly close this letter with another quotation from Sir George Reid, who, in the course of a characteristic, and therefore delighted speech at a luncheon given in his honour on Wednes- day, said:—</p>
        <p>" I am a supporter of this Coalition Government. I went into the House of Commons to see if I could find 22 men more fitted to run the war than the present 22. I have been looking closely for three days— (laughter)—and I'll be hanged if I can find them." (Laughter and cheers.) Sir George Reid has always a humorous, and .a good humoured way of putting things, but here he is simply reminding _us of a fact which not one of us should for an instant lose sight of.</p>
        <p>Yours, &amp;c.,</p>
        <p>A. If. CROSFIELD.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>MAYOR'S OPEN LETTER,</head>
        <p>MAYOR'S OPEN LETTER,</p>
        <p>Young Men Urged To Make Compulsion Unnecessary.</p>
        <p>THE NATION'S NEEDS.</p>
        <p>nig Worship the Mayor of Warrington has received the following telegram from Lord</p>
        <p>"I respectfully beg you to do all in your power to induce men, especially single men, to join the Army under the Group System. It is still open for voluntary enlistment, and I am anxious that as many as possible should take advantage of this before the Military 'Service Bill becomes operative. "The Lord Mayor of London has , obtained excellent results. He opened . a special recruiting office in the Mansion 'House, and has himself appealed to the young men in the Metropolis. " Will it be too much to ask you, if it is not already being done, to do the same in your town?—Derby."</p>
        <p>The Mayor, while urging every single ypung •man to come forward at once and attest under the Derby scheme, and thus so far as Warring- ton is concerned make the Compulsion Bill a ,dead letter, does not propose, for the present, to .open a special Recruiting Office at the Town Rail. He is of opinion that the arrangements -at the existing Recruiting Stations are quite adequate, but in the event of there being any rush, he will immediately take steps to have additional stations opened.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>1I.111)11'S</head>
        <p>THE 1I.111)11'S</p>
        <p>APPEAL.</p>
        <p>To the single young men of Warrington who have not yet attested under Lord Derby's Scheme.</p>
        <p>In the hour of your country's need I appeal to you to come forward at once and offer your services to your King, and do your share in the -defence of your country. Your brothers from Warrington have done splendidly hi the fighting line. Now is your -chance to show that you appreciate their efforts. Every case of individual hardship will be con- - sidered by the Local Tribunal on its merits, ;and there are ample safeguards to prevent any -man being taken whose place is at home. I ask every young man most seriously to con- - sider his position and to consider, at the same time, the needs of the Nation and cif the Empire. If this is done I feel sure•that when the period has elapsed after which the compulsory attes- tation will be in force, it will be found that in Warrington at any rate the young men have done their duty':</p>
        <p>PETER PEACOCK,</p>
        <p>Mayor.</p>
        <p>MR. REDMOND'S DENIAL.</p>
        <p>In response to an invitation to confirm or deny the rumour in political circles in Cork that he had tendered his resignation as leader of the Irish party, Mr. Redmond on Tuesday sent the following message 'Rumour wholly ridiculous—fabrication.—</p>
        <p>Lancs. Regiment has a capable and enthusiastic leader of experience and pleasant disposition.</p>
        <p>Scott-street, has been wounded, for the second time • since he joined the local Territorials. First he was wounded on June 16th, the day upon which our Territorials made their glorious charge upon the German trenches in Flanders. He was then hit with shrapnel in the right arm. The injury now consists of a shrapnel wound and compound fracture of the leg, and he lies in the Liverpool Merchants' Mobile Hospital, France. From the latter hospital he has written two letters to his wife, in one of which he says that his leg is in splints up to the thigh. A letter received on Tuesday contained the following news:—" I was , hit through the calf and the bullet came out in front of my leg. It broke the bone in two places, but it only needs time and patience. My leg was put under the X-ray this morning, and, as I am progressing favourably, I hope to be in Eng- land shortly." Private Clough, who is 42 years of age, was formerly employed a. 3 a wire-netting weaver at the Whitecross Company's Works. He is a IWycliffe School ",old boy," and is a member 'of the Junior Conservative Club. He is a</p>
        <p>good billiard player and trundler, and has won, several prizes at these games. In 1909 he won the "Lady Greenall" Challenge Cup for billiards. He rejoined the Territorials imme- diately upon the outbreak of war.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <p>LIEUT.-COLONEL A. B. LAYTON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>SECOND-LIEUTENANT CORBETT.</head>
        <p>SECOND-LIEUTENANT CORBETT.</p>
        <p>Artillery Officer Awarded Mililary</p>
        <p>Cross.</p>
        <p>Seccind-Lieti*enant and Adjutant R. W. Corbett, the son of Mr. and Mrs. Corbett, of iv, London-road, Stockton Heath, who had the distinction of 'being mentioned in dispatches on January Ist, has now been awarded the Military Cross. Lieutenant Corbett's father was for nearly fifty years in the service of Messrs. W. Mortimer and Co., of the Orford Tannery, retiring about three years ago. The Lieutenant, who is. a native of Orford, has served in the Army for twenty years. Pre- vious to the war he was a Warrant Officer in , the Royal Field Artillery, and left England with the ammunition column of the 7th Division, landing at Zeobruge. His Division took part in the covering of the retreat of the Belgian Army, until they were relieved by Sir John French's Army from the Marne. Ile received his com- mission for service in the field in the first great battle of Ypres, and at the storming of Hill 60 was engaged on the ammunition supply. After that he was appointed Adjutant of the Trench Howitzer Brigade of the 2nd Army. In this iSosition he is doing very efficient work, and is held in high esteem. During his experience as a soldier, Lieutenant Corbett has done duty in each of the four king- doms, in addition to spending eight years in India. His wife and two children are now re- siding at Ethelstone, Penketh.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>MR. HERBERT A. BLECKUV.</head>
        <p>MR. HERBERT A. BLECKUV.</p>
        <p>Granted Commission In Yeomanry Regiment.</p>
        <p>• Mr. Herbert A. Bleckly, who has been engaged in the engineering department at the works of Messrs. Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Co., Ltd., has been gazetted a second-lieutenant in the Montgomeryshire Yeomanry (Territorial Force). Mr. Bleckly is the son of Mr. Herbert S. Bleckly, director of Messrs. Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Co., Ltd., and resides at Hale, Cheshire.</p>
        <p>The young officer was educated at King's Col- lege, Chester, and on completing his technical courses at Manchester Municipal School of Tech- nology, went to work at Dallam. He is joining his regiment immediately, and will be attached to a battalion which at present is in Ireland.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>FIGHTING FAMILY.</head>
        <p>A FIGHTING FAMILY.</p>
        <p>Batter 3 -Sergeant-Major W. J. Price</p>
        <p>Obtains D.C.M.</p>
        <p>Battery-Sergeant-Major William Joseph Pri, e. of the Royal Field Artillery, whose mother lives at 30, Alexandra-street, Warrington, has been awarded the D.C.M. for services rendered whtle serving "somewhere in France." He recent'y wrote to his wife, who resides in London, sayin4 that his officer had told him he would bring the action under the notice of higher authority, be,. did not mention the particular act he had per- formed. Since then his name has appeared in the " London' Gazette."</p>
        <p>Battery-Sergeant-Major Price, who is* 42 yes-:, of age, is a native of Aldershot, but resided in Warrington for many years. He is an ol:1 soldier, having served in a London Territori9.l Battalion for quite a long time. Indeed, he is In the possession of a long service medal. He teaching in London when war broke out, and he immediately enlisted. He had also pre- viously followed the teaching profession at S. Mary's Schocil, Warrington, where he himself was educated.</p>
        <p>Four of his brothers are also in Rig Majesty-13 Service. Samuel James Price, the well-known assistant master of St. Mary's School, became Lieutenant-Quartermaster in the local Ter 1- torials in November, and his new rank has since been gazetted. Albert Edward, who is married, belongs to the R.A.M.C., and is stationed at a Liverpool Hospital, while George, who is also a married man, is with the Navy at sea. The remaining brother. Richard, enlisted recently in the Royal Munster Fusiliers. He had previously been engaged in the Education Office. Their father, the late Joseph Price, was in the Army for forty-eight years, fought in the Indian Mutiny, and was at the relief of Luck. now. He was at one time stationed at Orford Barracks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>FEW SUCCESSFUL</head>
        <p>FEW SUCCESSFUL</p>
        <p>Recruiting Tribunal Rejects Majority Of Appeals.</p>
        <p>Of the fifty men who applied to the Warring- ton Recruiting , Tribunal this week to be exempted from military serrvice, or, at least, be relegated to ,a later group, only .two were pro- visionally exempted (on the ground of being engaged on important work) and about ten were allowed further time.</p>
        <p>The Tribunal sat on three days, and gave all cases careful attention, the siftings lasting from three to four hours on each occasion. The Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock) presided. Next week the Tribunal will deal with applica- tions from those in Groups 6,7, 8 and 9. Thirty- three men have given notice.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 22, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 22, 1916.</p>
        <p>TERRITORIAL ROLL OF HONOUR.</p>
        <p>The latest casualties to hand in the ranks o 4 ,he local Territorial Battalion are as follows:-</p>
        <p>WOUNDED. Private James Rylands. Private J. J. Clough (second time).</p>
        <p>TOTAL CASUALTIES TO DATE. Killed Wounded Missing Prisoners Total</p>
        <p>563</p>
        <p>WOUNDED.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>PRIVATE J. J. CLOUGH.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE J. J. CLOUGH.</p>
        <p>Wounded For The Second Time, But Progressing Favourably. Private John James Clough, of the Warring- ton Territorials. whose home address is 30,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>PRIVATE J. RYLANDS.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE J. RYLANDS.</p>
        <p>Suffering From Concussion Caused By' Shell. ,</p>
        <p>Private James Rylands. of the Warrington Territorials; whose wife resides at 36, Factory.. lane, is at present in a French hospital, recov- ering from the effects -cif concussion, received while .on duty in the trenches. In a - letter to his :brother- George he thus describes his experience:— " I consider myself very lucky, for I had a narrow escape of being blown into the air. When the Germans see a working party about they start shelling it, and we have had to run for it a few times: They follow us with. their shells along the trench, .and one -time they were dropping in the mouth of the trench, but with a bit of luck we managed to get through it. One .of the shells dropped about five yards from me, and I had a few bricks thrown on top of me. Consequently, I write this letter from hospital. I have had a bit of a shaking up. The last time I went, to the trenches I had the trench blown in on* top of me by a concussion shell and was almost buried.. One</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>ON HONOUR'S SCROLL.</head>
        <p>ON HONOUR'S SCROLL.</p>
        <p>BACK FROM GA LLIPOII.</p>
        <p>Two Local Invalids Progressing Favourably.</p>
        <p>The husbands of two sisters, who now reside at 22, Sharp-street, are at present in hospital after taking part in the Dardanelles operations.</p>
        <p>The first- is Driver Thomas Woods, of the rmy Service 'Corps, and is in St. Thomas' -.3xiliary Military Hospital, Liverpool, suffer- ing. from nephritis. He formerly worked at Messrs. Richmond's works for 13i years, and is now 41 years of age. He joined the Army in the early part of the war, and went out to the Dardanelles in June. He has not been wounded, but has suffered from various illnesses. He was removed from Alexandria on November 24th, and arrived in England on December 10th. He is progressing fairly well. His brother-in-law, Sergeant Alf. Moorhouse. has had much Army experience, for he served in the South Lanes. Regiment for 15 years. At the outbreak of war he was a special reservist, and immediately rejoined the Colours. After a time he was appointed instructor in the 6th South Lancashire Regiment, and later left with his regiment for the Dardanelles. He is at present in the Lord Derby Hospital, Winwick, suffering from shock. He is a native of Stock- port, and had worked at Messrs. Joseph Cros- field and Sons, Ltd. for about eighteen months prior to the war. The two sisters have a brother serVing as chief petty officer on a battleship in the Mediterranean.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>" TOMMIES' TEA."</head>
        <p>" TOMMIES' TEA."</p>
        <p>Nearly £4OO Raised For Our</p>
        <p>Wounded.</p>
        <p>The recent "Tommies' Tea" at the Parr Hall has proved to be as good a financial suc- cess as it was a social.</p>
        <p>A Meeting of the Executive Committee which had the arrangements in hand was held on Thursday afternoon, Councillor Henry Roberts presiding, and the accounts submitted by the Treasurer (Mr. H. J. Westbrook) showed that the total receipts amounted to .2395 19s. 11d. The total payments were £l4 68. Bd., and this left a net balance of £3Bl 3s. 3d. The acCouuts were received and adopted. It was decided to allocate this amount as follows: _ _ •</p>
        <p>-Stockton Heath Working Party, £4O. Thelwall Working Party, £25. Moore and Daresbury Working Party. £l6 3s. 3d. Lord Derby Military Hospital. £3OO.</p>
        <p>The money &amp;voted to the Win Wick Hospital will be spent in providing comforts, aids and appliances for the soldiers of that institution. It was further decided not to pay the whole of the ..t3OO to the hospital at once, but to inti- mate to Colonel Simpson that be might draw on the Treasurer up to that amount for the purposes mentioned as occasion required. On the motion of Councillor Roberts, a hearty vote of thanks was passed to all who had so generously given their time, labour and money to the cause with such successful results; and also to Mr. H. B. Houghton (the hogarary secreary) and Mr. H. J. Westbrook (honorary treasurer) for their willing and' able work. The Working Parties, it may be explained, are engaged in making articles required for the soldiers at the hospitals, and as the money ' which has been allocated:to them will be used in purchasing materials, the whole of the pro- ceeds of the Toramies' Tea" will eventually go to the patients at the Military Hospitals. The various Working Parties gave up their own individual efforts to raise money in order to make a combined effort with the " Tommies' Tea," and it was thought to be only fair that they should be recompensed for their self- denial.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>60,000 LOST, RELATIVES.</head>
        <p>60,000 LOST, RELATIVES.</p>
        <p>15,000 Belgians _Put Into Touch With</p>
        <p>Their Friends.</p>
        <p>The usefulness of the "Register of Belgian Refugees" was demonstrated to the Royal Statistical_ Society this week by Mr. T. T. S.</p>
        <p>de Jastrzebski.</p>
        <p>_ At the end of November last the card index, he said, contained the names of between 230,000 and 240,000 civil refugees. During the year 60,000 searches for missing relatives and friends had been made for individual applicants, and in 15,000 cases the searches were successful. In addition some 40,000 searches were made for foreign committees. He estimated that at least 70 per cent, of the employable refugees in the United Kingdom had found work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <p>Warrington And South Lancashire Men Who Are "Straffing Them" Slowly And</p>
        <p>LOCAL MEN ON A BATTLE CRUISER.</p>
        <p>" Boys" from Warrington (3), Lymm, Newton, St. Helens, Earlestown, Widnes,</p>
        <p>Manchester, and two Cockneys, who are real Gems.</p>
        <p>to hear from one of the boys. I've been a reltder, of The Examiner" now for a few years; although I'm far away from home I get it every week, and I can assure you it causes a lot of trouble among the boys when I've read it. There is a good number of us from South Lancashire aboard. They have even christened our stokehold the "South Lanca- shire Regiment." Do we look down- hearted? Bet your life we aint! We are waiting patiently for "The Day," and when it comes well be there. I cannot describe the weather; it's absolutely wicked. We were at sea with no provisions but pineapple chunks. We lived on pineapple, bread and tea—some- times, if it would stop in the basin—for four days. Has German Navy Been Put On Wheels?</p>
        <p>No wonder there's no seasickness in Per Faderland's. Navy. They are never out to be seasick. I'm inclined to believe they have put wheels on the ships in order to use them for armoured trains. For us it is continually "Come in, coal ship "; 3,000 tons, and "Go out again.' = We are just strafing them slowly</p>
        <p>but surely. Never a ship do we meet but that it is one of our own, and we rejoice that we are able to do it. But I say "Gott strafe the man who said 'A life on the ocean wave."</p>
        <p>Dear Editor. I give you full permission to make whatever use you like of this letter and photograph. Wouldn't the boys laugh to see their photos in the centre of "The Examiner"? All the boys on the photo belong to Warring- ton, Lymm, Newton, St. Helens, Earlestown, Widnes, Manchester, and a few . more Lanca- shire towns; three Warringtonians and two Cockneys, and they are real gems, the two on the very front sat down. Before joining the Navy I was a potman on the caustic at Crosfield's, Bank Quay. Dear Editor, I hope you don't mind the writing. You cannot expect anything else. She's wobbly.! Well, the boys hope to see it in "The Examiner." So I'll close now, old chap. Au revoir for the present, yours very sin- cerely, •</p>
        <p>J. E. O'ROURKE. Roll on "The Day."-</p>
