        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>FIRST GROUPS MEN</head>
        <p>FIRST GROUPS MEN</p>
        <p>Assemble At Orford Barracks This Week.</p>
        <p>MEMORABLE SCENES.</p>
        <p>On Thursday tlie most casual of observers mist have had thrust before his notice even if he had forgotten the date, the fact that Lord Derby's day of calling up England's manhood under his recruiting scheme had dawned.</p>
        <p>Along the thoroughfare leading to Orford Barracxs, the pride of many a mother's heart marched with jaunty, easy style and firm of tread to that great and gaunt building, stand- ing out like a fortress, which many brave lads have seen for the last time. As one stood an watched the future conquerors of (lumen militarism marching by, women and children raised a cheer which was answered by lusty throats in typical British fashion. The majority seemed mere youths, with every now and then in string contrast, a six-foot recruit standing out conspicuously from the rest; on all countenances was that look of doggedness and never-say-die spirit that stamps the English soldier different from the rest of the world.</p>
        <p>"What cheer, Kitchener?" shouted an urchin near a cinema hall, which raised a laugh from the company, and the new ruddy-faced recruit blushed with confusion. In the open courtyard a staff of officers and subordinates awaited the arrival of the first batch.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>Fitting Them Out.</head>
        <p>Fitting Them Out.</p>
        <p>After the necessary preliminary medical examinations, standing to attention they answered to their respective names; then in small groups, under the charge of corporals, &amp;c.,</p>
        <p>'ere marched to the storeroom for their new</p>
        <p>uniforms and kit-bag. Hero in the capacious storehouse were piles of khaki uniforms in different sizes, the stock seeming sufficient to clothe the British Army. "Say, sir," said one raw-boned St. Helens youth, -laughingly, "this coat is about three sizes too small. Crikey I I look like George Formby." "Right ho! my lad, we'll soon La you right."</p>
        <p>"Say, mate," said another to a sergeant, "how do you wrap these • something putties on straight ?" Any other day might have earned this bud- ding soldier seven days' C. 8., but on this occasion a kindly word of reprimand did more good than punishment. The one thing most noticeable throughout the day was the adapta- bility of the recruits to their new sphere of life. Orders were obeyed with quick - precision, and the packing of kit-bags performed with light- ning rapidity. There were lads from Earleitown, St. lielens, Widnes and Cardiff and other centres, all in fine spirits and impressed with their own import- ance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>Sent To Their Units.</head>
        <p>Sent To Their Units.</p>
        <p>The recruits from the surrounding districts were immediately on equipment railed to the various units, there to be trained into ship- shape form.</p>
        <p>Ksking one of the officers what he thought of the men, one was vouchsafed the reply: "Rather good specimens of manhood, but wait until a month or so has passed and you won't know them. There will be many mothers of England proud of their sons ere long." That was all, but in his voice was a ring of pride and elation. Owing to various reasons it is not compatible to give the totals or the destination of the new recruits, but one thing might be mentioned, and that is that there will be no aggravating delay in the dispatching of ;nen to their respective centres.</p>
        <p>In the borough during the week the topic of conversation has been concerning the billeting of the new arrivals, but we believe it will not be necessary for householders to make any pro- vision for the present groups at any rate.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>CARTERS DISPUTE.</head>
        <p>CARTERS DISPUTE.</p>
        <p>The Mayor Fails To Obtain</p>
        <p>Amicable Settlement.</p>
        <p>LEFT TO BOARD OF TRADE.</p>
        <p>Masters and men in the carting trade in War- rington are again at variance, and the crisis has become so acute that the Board of Trade has been asked to step in. The dispute began towards the close of November, when the United Carters' and Motors' Association applied to the Warrington Team Owners' Association for an advance in wages of ss. per week. To this the masters re- plied that as there was in force an agreement, subject to three months' notice on either side, the matter could not be dealt with immediately. The general secretary of the men's union, Mr. Alfred Hilton, then wrote threatening to with- ' draw labour from Warrington. On the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock) hearing of this, he at once took steps to convene a conference between the employers and the men's representatives.</p>
        <p>_ It will be recollected that When the carters went on strike in 1913 a serious dislocation of traffic resulted, and various works in the town were greatly hindered in carrying on their' trade. After a fortnight's rest from work, the men were granted a rise of 2s. per week. It was the possibility of a recurrence of this hindrance to trade which prompted the Mayor to suggest a conference for Wednesday at the Town Hall, and it was hoped that means would be found of arriving at an amicable settlement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>UNIONS INVOLVED.</head>
        <p>THE UNIONS INVOLVED.</p>
        <p>At the opening of the conference objection was immediately taken by Mr. Hilton to the presence of the general and local secretaries of the Tram- way and Vehicle Workers (Alderman Jackson, of Manchester, and Mr. E. Plinston, res- pectively) on the ground that the latter had no application for consideration before the employers. . 1 The employers decided that they would not deal with the matter separately with each Union, supporting this attitude by pointing out that the agreement of 1913 had been signed by representatives of both organisations. The conference was almost breaking up when the Mayor persuaded Mr. Hilton and his fellow delegate (Mr. J. C. Francis) to agree to repre- sentatives of the other Trade Union being pre- sent. All having resumed their seats, the employers offered 2s. a week additional war bonus (2s. having been given in March last) the conditions being that each man should work uninterruptedly for six days, and any loss of time in the morning should be taken off overtime at night. IThe offer was refused, and the employers in- timated that they were going to submit the dis- pute to the Board of Trade.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>DRINK AND LOST TIME.</head>
        <p>DRINK AND LOST TIME.</p>
        <p>In an interview with an " Examiner " repre- sentative, Mr. Hilton said that, considering the increase in the cost of living and the fact that the carters could get higher wages by going into other trades, the offer of 2s. a week extra was not enough.</p>
        <p>A carter looking after one horse now receives 268. a week minimum and, if be has charge of two horses, 295. a week. There are about four hundred men involved.</p>
        <p>A prominent official of the employers' association said that in any agreement which might be come to they would insist that the men should work regularly. The conditions of hours were more important than the advance in wages, because it meant that, if men lost time, firms could not get through their work, which was most esgential nowadays. "There is, no doubt about it that some of the men are losing a con-. siderable amount of time," he continued. " some cases they fail to work on as many. as three and four days a week. It is all through the drink."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>SOLDIER " KILLED " AT NIONS`, STILL ALIVE.</head>
        <p>SOLDIER " KILLED " AT NIONS`, STILL ALIVE.</p>
        <p>A letter has been received in Berwickshire this week from Michael Keogh, of the Royal Scots, who was reported officially as killed at Mons. Ile is a prisoner of war, and his rela- tives had heard nothineaf him since the battle.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>LOST PERSIA.</head>
        <p>THE LOST PERSIA.</p>
        <p>Mr. Harold Smith's Decidedly Improper Charge.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Monday, on the motion for the adjournment of the House, Mr. Harold Smith, M.P. for Warrington, con- firmed the statement we reproduced a few weeks ago that he was aboard the Persia up to the day before she was torpedoed. He communi- cated this fact to the House in. a charge he brought Against the Board of Trade of falling to, take adequate steps to ensure the safety of pas- sengers on ocean-going steamers, and he sug- gested that the neglect of the Board of Trade was responsible-for the loss of life on the Persia. Mr. Smith's Complaint.</p>
        <p>Mr. Smith said he knew the Board of Trade issued 'instructions last August, but were they insisted upon or merely permissive? He spent some days on the Persia, and left her only on the day before she was torpedoed. All the time he was on board he never saw any boat drill. He believed shipowners deprecated boat drill - because they thought it frightened the passen- gers, But discipline in cases of emergency was essential, and there could not be discipline un- less there was some authority directing .the passengers as to what they had to do. In the Persia the passengers never sat down to a meal but every port and scuttle in the dining-room was open. He desired to know whether in any one of the tragic instances of the sinking of large steamships lives had been saved by the carrying out of the instructions that life-saving apparatus should be stowed on deck, so as to be readily got into the water, or apparatus that would float off the ship's deck on submersion. He did not believe that, starting with the Lusitania and finishing with the Persia, the President of the Board of Trade could point to a single life that had been saved by those provisions which his department suggested, but on which it did not,insist. On every naval ship rafts were provided which would hold some— thing like sixty men. Was there one on these ocean-goinc, steamers? He had never found one. A week or two ago he crossed the Channel on a ship which carried 1,200 troops. Every soldier was compelled to wear a lifebelt, but thirty or forty women on board were without any protec- tion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>Mr. Runciman's Counter-Charge.</head>
        <p>Mr. Runciman's Counter-Charge.</p>
        <p>Mr. Runciman (President of the Board of Trade) said that the case which the hon gentle- man had presented to the House had been spoiled by utterly ignoring the technical advice which had been given in these matters for many years past, and in every case he had attempted to colour his accusations rather in terms of an indictment against the Board of Trade. His (Mr. Runciman's) personal feeling was in favour of a supply of rafts rather than of boats for saving life in disasters at sea. He did not know what ground the hon. member hod for saying that there were no rafts on the Persia. How did he know that? •</p>
        <p>Mr. Smith :I made an inspection, and I could find none.</p>
        <p>Mr. Runciman said it did not follow because Mr. Smith could not find tht.m that seats were not fitted with tanks. The hon. member was making a very serious charge. Did he mean that there should be a Board of Trade inquiry into the loss of the Persia? Mr. Smith: Yes. -</p>
        <p>Mr. Runciman : Then why does he make state- ments which would prejudice such an inquiry? Such a course is most decidedly improper, and the hon. member ought to have known better. Does he wish an inquiry? Mr. Smith: My object was to draw the atten- tion of the right hon. gentleman to the fact that thousands of lives had been sacrificed through the neglect of the Board of Trade. Mr. Runciman : The hon. member is totally unable to substantiate such a charge, and he could not proteed more improperly than by making charges here, where no evidence can be produced. In such matters the Board of Trade is bound to act on technical advice, and there is not a single recommendation made by a techni- cal committee set up for the purpose at any time during the last two years that has not been faithfully carried out by the Board of Trade.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>VOLUNTEER ORDERS.</head>
        <p>VOLUNTEER ORDERS.</p>
        <p>SOUTH-WEST LANCASHIRE REGT. (VOLUNTEER).</p>
        <p>Warrington Company. Headquarters, Drill Hall. ORDERS FOR WEEK ENDING JAN. 29th, By W. PETER RYLANDS, Commandant. Parades.</p>
        <p>To-day, 3 p.m., Extended Order Drill, Bank Park. Monday, 8 p.m., Route March. Thursday, 8 p.m., Instruction in Outpost and Advance Guard. Special. Saturday next, January 29th, Scheme inter- cepting White Force (Widnes) convoy of motor lorries, area, triangle, including Penketh Lane End, Fiddler's Ferry Station, Cuerdley Cross and Bold Bridge. Scout Cyclists, 2 p.m. Advance Guard, 2.30 p.m. prompt. Penketh and Sankey Detachmenfs, 2.30, Drill quarters. Orderly Sergeant for the week—Section' Commander Mathias.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>MUSKETRY.</head>
        <p>MUSKETRY.</p>
        <p>Third Round for Mackwor-th Praed Challenge Bowl, to-day, 2.30 p.m., Warrington v. East Croydon (First Surrey Rifles). TUesday, 7.30 p.m., Drill Ilall Range, Firing Practice, all branche:.</p>
        <p>SECTION B. RESERVES. No.l Squa.d.—As detailed above. No. 2 Squad.—To-flay, 3 p.m., Bank Park. Monday and Thursday, 8 p.m., Rifle Exer- cises. Penketh and Sankey Sections. To-day, 3 p.m. Tuesday, 8 p.m. Squad Drill, under own instructors. Saturday next,lls detailed for Company.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>MEMORANDA.</head>
        <p>MEMORANDA.</p>
        <p>I.—Saturday next, a good muster of all branches required. Further details of the schema will be issued later.</p>
        <p>2.—Penketh and Sankey Sections are notified that the Drill Hall Range will be open for one month on Tuesday nights for Practice and. Class- Firing under their own instructors.</p>
        <p>RAIDING THE GERMANS.</p>
        <p>Lancashire Regiment Granted Seven</p>
        <p>Distinctions.</p>
        <p>A LOCAL D.C.M. The story of how Lance-Corporal Keating, of Catherine-street, Warrington, won his D.C.M. was published on Tuesday morning. A fortnight ago we were able to record an inter- view which one of our representatives had with Keating, but the following details amplify his story. His regiment, the 11th Lancashire Fusiliers, has only been in France since September 25th, but already it has been granted two Military Crosses and Five Distinguished Conduct Medals.</p>
        <p>Cutting the German Wires. The story of how Keating won his D.C.M. is as follows:</p>
        <p>"On the night of December Bth last Lance- Corporals Beating and Beddows crawled to the enemy wire, and cut a passage through it in • preparation for a raid. The wire was very thick, and not only in front of but on the enemy parapet Sentries and patrols were heard and their heads seen from time to time inside the enemy trench. Secrecy was essential to success. In the last few strands was hidden an alarm steel wire, which was cut with great difficulty. When cut the alarm was given, and a hurricane of fire burst forth from the enemy trench, actually over their heads and within twelve feet of the men. They lay on the parapet until the fire subsided, eluded a patrol, and returned, having been out for three hours and twenty minutes from our trenches."</p>
        <p>On December 28th Lance-Corporal Keating took part in a raid on the German trenches and again distinguished himself.</p>
      </div>
