      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>SAMPLE</head>
        <p>SAMPLE</p>
        <p>JUSTIFYING ROUGH WEATHER.</p>
        <p>Rev. A. M. Mitchell Sticks Up For</p>
        <p>Nature.</p>
        <p>Heading an article "Something ..To Abuse," the Rev. A. M. Mitchell, in his Parish Maga- zine, discourses amiably about the weather. He says : " The Englishman is always ready to abuse something or somebody. The weather usually provides a safety valve and useful outlet for the store of harmless abuse within. "The weather! If all the abuse heaped upon it daily could be materialised the world could not contain it; the horizon would be en- tirely swept away. Fancy if you can) a world without a horizon! Certainly tile weather of December has justified to some extent the abuse heaped upon it, for it has been ususually trying, provoking, and disastrous, which is saying a great deal. "Rain by day and rain by night has not sufficed; it has also been a case of storm at sunrise, storm at sunset. The hymn 'for those in peril on the sea' was never more necessary than at this latest Christmas-tide. . .</p>
        <p>"Dirty weather," as the sailors say, is not sufficiently strong language in this instance; the old sea dogs must by this time have sub- stituted ' filthy ' for dirty." Filthy weather,' that's just it. "But when we have indulged our love of abuse to the full let us calmly consider that filthy weather so full of discomfort generally- and so distressingly trying to so many indivi- duals is accompanied by blessings which far outweigh the inconveniences, annoyances, mis- haps and calamities which follow in the train of high winds anti tempestuous rains. Rain and wind are Nature's benediction to earth and air, and therefore to man. Winter provides earth's bath. Mother earth must wash and be clean. The earth would not be, could not be healthful and fruitful, if to any consider- able extent deprived of these two purifiers and fertilisers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>Preparing For Spring.</head>
        <p>Preparing For Spring.</p>
        <p>"The winter prepares the land for the spring; the rain is the forerunner of the sower, the true and faithful pioneer of the husband- man and his helpers. "The winds cleanse and purify the air, mak- ing it sweet and wholesome for man and beast —all things that have life. Were it not for storms and tempests, now and again, the air would become putrid, full of noxious germs„ death instilling instead of health giving. "Microbes are no friends of man; they are dispersed by the strong high winds, making the air a clean and wholesome thing to breathe.t pleasant to inhale. Pure air makes life worth living, without it we should miss the joy of life. -</p>
        <p>"The damage done by strong, high winds ' is magnified beyond reason. "A moment's reflection must convince the sane that the damage caused through a big, 'long storm is very insignificant compared with the good resulting from it. Wrecks on sea are not unknown, of course, but comparatively they are few; land accidents and disasters are ,very infrequent. It is really marvellous the few tragedies 'which are .recorded as on sea or • land in consequence of a violent and even pro- longed storm. -**•We shall do well to think sensibly about the,much-abused weather, to enter more deeply into Nature's ways and methods; to remember that Nature herself is held in the hollow of God's hand, that hand so powerful that nothing human or material can be snatched therefrom, that Hand which, gripping all, up- holding all, is yet opened continually to supply all human and animal need, and fill all things living -with plenteousness."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>SILLY WAGER.</head>
        <p>A SILLY WAGER.</p>
        <p>Actor's Sevolver Shot And its Sequel.</p>
        <p>. '</p>
        <p>A remarkable shooting affair took place at Todmorden Railway Station on Sunday. A travelling pantomime company stopped, at Todmorden to.change. One of the company was leaning out of the carriage window, smoking a pipe, when another member offered to bet that he could hit Ciao pipe with a bullet without touching the smoker. The challenge was accepted and the challenger fired with his revolver. The bullet shattered the pipe, but it also struck a third member of the, company who was seated in the compartment, passing through his cheek. Medical asailitance was obtained, and after treatment the wounded man was allowed Ito continue his journey,</p>
        <p>_</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>Arempt</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>BANK NOTES WORTH £1,80".-) DESTROYED BY FIRE.</head>
        <p>BANK NOTES WORTH £1,80".-) DESTROYED BY FIRE.</p>
        <p>At the public examination at Hull on Monday of Sidney Grantham, formerly a farmer, but now a trooper in the East Riding of Yorkshire Imperial Yeomanry, whose deficienehr amounted to £4OO, the debtor attributed his insolvency to a fire in October. He stated that Bank of Eng- land and Treasury notes of the value of £1,805 were destroyed. He also lost furniture, jewellery and silver plate worth £4OO. He was awakened early in the morning by a dog barking and found the room in which the notes were locked up on fire. He tried to save them, but the heat was too intense. The examination was adjourned.</p>
        <p>lan</p>
        <p>,yiurNwmsvP9*.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>PUBLISHED IN THIS PAPER.</head>
        <p>PUBLISHED IN THIS PAPER.</p>
        <p>Did You See It?</p>
        <p>Four. years ago a Warrington resident made a statement which was published in our local papers, and which aroused great interest, not only here, but for many miles around. Our readers will, we are sure, be gratified to know that the facts are ienthusiastically endorsed to-day—years after. On 'March 21st, 1911,. Mr. C. W. King, of "Cheshire View," Manchester-road, Warring- ton, said :—" I caught a severe cold, which I think must have affected My kidneys, because I had an attack of •sciatica afterwards which laid me up for three months. A sharp, cat- ting pain used to start in my left hip and shoot right down to my knee, and I was in dreadful pain, .night and day. At times I was almost helpless. I could not get any sleep at night, and was confined to the house for three months. "I tried many remedies before I used Doan's Backache Kidney Pills, but was unable to get any relief.- This medicine, however, soon did me good, and by the time I had taken five boxes I was quite Cured and able to return to my _work.</p>
        <p>"I am only tob pleased to give this 'account of my experience for the benefit Of other sufferers. I have every reason now to speak highly of them, and never miss an opportunity of recommending the medicine." (Signed)" C. W. King." On March 16, 1915—four years later—Mr. King said :--L" I heartily confirm all I said in my testimonial for Doan's Pills four years ago. They did me a great deal of good, and if I get a touch of the old trouble they never fail to remedy it." Colds and chills throw a heavy burden on'the kidneys, because they close up the pores, so that the impurities which should pass out through the skin have to he disposed of by the kidneys. Colds are, therefore, one of the commonest causes of serious kidney and bladdet disorders; and it is desirable to take a course of Doan's Backache Kidney Pills- during con- valescence from a cold or. influenza. This assists the kidneys in the, extra work ‘hrown upon them, and safeguards you from backache, rheumatism, gravel, dropsy and distressing urinary disorders. Price 2s. 9d. a box, of all dealers, or from Poster-McClellan Co., 8. Wells- street, Oxford-street, London, W. Don't ask for backache or kidney pills—ask distinctly for Doan's Backache Kidney Pills, the same as Mr. King had.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>, • , 6'l-1 – # – – '•' ' • I , , _ – . •</head>
        <p>, • , 6'l-1 - # - - '•' ' • I , , _ - . •</p>
        <p>....... . . . ... . • l ..111. , r:g : +., :, 4:;"::---- • *.r.,. : ; ),c,:„. ..• I- T, C . .. , 3 : ....,..... ~..... • ~..,,d...._,rw._....._...„. _:,,.... ...,.., ~..</p>
        <p>LYMM</p>
        <p>Cyciists Without Lights.—A couple of minor charges were made at Lymm Petty Sessions on Wednesday. Percy Ha.ughton, of Watercress Farm, Lymm, was fined 2s. 6d. for riding a bicycle without a light, and Vincent Hinton, of Fletcher's-lane, Rush Green, Lymm, was fined the same amount for a similar offence.</p>
        <p>*Private A. Thornicroft Returns To The Firing- line.—Private A. Thornicroft. of the 10th Battalion Cheshire Regiment, who was wounded in the forearm on November 4th, was last week home on a short furlough, looking none the worse for his exciting experiences at the Front. Private \ Thornicroft is now fully acquainted with Army life, and anxious to inflict return blows on the Germans. He returned to the firing-line a day or so ago.</p>
        <p>Khaki Visitors.—Amongst the khaki visitors to Lymm a day or so ago were Privates Dodson and H. Kay, both of whom are local Terri- torials. The former was home on a short leave from the Front and was rather reticent about imparting information respecting his doings oift in France. • Private Kay was enjoying a well- meyited furlough from his training centre in Shropshire, prior to his tieing drafted to the firing line for a second time. He was wounded some time back.</p>
        <p>Fire Brigade Fiasco.—Great excitement pre- vailed in the village on Saturday night by an alarm of fire, which rumour had it, had broken out at Dingle Bank, the residence of the Right Hon- Giles Runt. Although the firemen were scattered about in numerous places they very soon rallied together and turned out in quick style. On arriving at Dingle Bank they were informed that a mistake had been made and that the fire was at Dane Bank, the residence of Mr. Alfred Watkin. Again they met with a "disappointment," for no trace of fire could be found. The firemen then realised that a -cruel hoax had been played upon them. Meanwhile the real fire, which was in a field adjoining the Tennis Courts, had been extinguished by several gentlemen. There was no damage of note.</p>
        <p>Sham Fight At Millington.—The members of the Cheshire Volunteer Regiment (Altrincham Battalio4 under the command of Sir Arthur Haworth, took part in an interesting sham fight on Sunday in and around Millington. The battalion was divided into two groups, namely, the Red and Blue forces, the former comprising the platoons in and around Altrincham, and th 3 latter the 'Lynam, Oughtrinkton and Bollington platoons and the mounted sectipn. The outrine of the scheme was that the Red forces were I 0 attack 'Warrington and endeavour to get at least ;three men through. The Blue forces were to prevent them at Millington. ATthoug'h the con- testing parties were engaged until late in the afternoon the Red forces did not succeed in their endeavours. The-umpire gave his deeiSion in favour of the Blue 'forces, complimenting the men on their fine display. Weiklinj At The ,Cao_gregational Cleurch.—A pretty wedding took Place on Wednesday .after- noon at the Congregational Church between Mr- Walter Turner, son of Mr. W. Turner, of Shaer-street. Latchford, and Miss Fanny Sutton, yo,ungest 'daughter. of Mrs. Richard Daniels, of Newfields, Lyman. The bride, who was charmingly attired in a costume of light blue with White hat to match, was given away by her brother., Mr. Thomas Sutton, and the best man was Mr. *John Rose. There was a large congregation, for the bride is a meniber -of the Unix&amp; Choir.</p>
        <p>Mr. Leather played •stable selections on the organ, and the ‘choir sang two appropriate wed- ding 'hymns. The offidiatirrg clergyman was the Rev. Shields Gilt Numerous beautiful wedding presents have been received.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>INTERESTING LEU ER c SAPPER PERCY CLARE.</head>
        <p>INTERESTING LEU ER c SAPPER PERCY CLARE.</p>
        <p>Sapper Percy Clare, of !Booth's Hill, attaehed to the Royal Engineers at the Front, has iwrit- ten home the following "interesting letter : " I am sendiag this letter home by a young fellow who has promised it shall be delivered into sour hands.. He is going Thome on leave— lucky fellowl in one of your letters a short time ago you wanted to know if we were in the same ptace. Yes, we are 'Wing in the same place, ;but it takes no .about two hours to get into the trenches, and we go by motor wagons as far as they dare go. It then means a walk for half an hotnr through roads of mud and slush: At present we are haling fairly good weather, oo it ,does not take so long to arrive in the firing-line, but the trenches are still full of water, which cannot drain away. The part of the line where we are stationed is all chalk, quite different to Ypres, for there it is all sand and gravel..</p>
        <p>"we blew up four mines the other day to let the New Year in for the Germans; they tried it on New Year's night when we were going up. They sent dozens of trench mortars, but they did no demage. The majority dropped short. Then they began to shell us. This time they landed behind tho trench, for which we thanked our lucky stars. We had a rough time during the holidays, for we were in action. on Christmas Eve, Christmas Day, New Year's 'Eve and New Year's Day. So you see we have had our fill.</p>
        <p>"Tell my brother Joe we are now a little \higher up, the other side of the trench stores. He will know where that is, for we used to go creeping round the corners for timber for the mines."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>PRIVATE WALLACE MeHUGII.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE WALLACE MeHUGII.</p>
        <p>Home On Short Furlough After Bombing For Nine Months.</p>
        <p>Private Wallace McHugh visited his home at the Rookery, Booths. Hill, on Sunday on a short furlough from the Pront. Looking hale and hearty and in the pink of condition, he states that he left all the Lymm boys in the best of health and good spirits, all of whom anticipated the war only lasting a short time. The weather conditions, he says, are extremely vile where he has been stationed. A local Territorial, he was mobilised with his corps at the outbreak of war, and was drafted to France about twelve months ago. Shortly after arriving at the Front he was placed in the hand grenade section, and has had some very weird and exciting experi- ences. He is twenty years of age, and in civil life was employed as a tanner at the Grap- penhall Tanning Co.'s tannery. He is a well- known member of the Lymm Subscription Band and is greatly interested in music. Of -a fine patriotic family, his father, Private James McHugh, is an old Volunteer, and soon after the outbreak of war joined the sth Batt. Cheshire Regiment as a National Reservist. He is at present stationed in Anglesea. Private Wallace McHugh returns to the firing line to- day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>GUN IN POCKETS.</head>
        <p>GUN IN POCKETS.</p>
        <p>Fines For Carrying Firearms Without</p>
        <p>Licences.</p>
        <p>Two local men were fined 10s. each at the Lymm Petty Sessions on Wednesday for carry- ing guns without licences. In the first case, P.C. Gould said that when he saw the defendant coming towards him,. he concealed himself in a wood. He stopped. the defendant who, in reply to a question, said he was looking for "beaters." He was asked to open his ,overooat, and there the constable found in his two inside pockets two halves of a double-barrelled gun. Defendant said he was after a rabbit for his Sunday's dinner. The defendant pleaded that he did not know it was necessary' that he should have a licence in order to carry a .gun. He carried it in his pockets for the sake of convenience.</p>
        <p>The Chairman of the Bench (Mr. A. Watkin)- in imposing a fine, said he did not believe the defendant's story. When stopped by a policeman, the other de- fendant said ,he was " working " ferrets: Thi :.vas LQt-Ivadtd, and Lui had no entridgert</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>BROTHERS FINED £2O.</head>
        <p>BROTHERS FINED £2O.</p>
        <p>.</p>
        <p>he 20th he asked for a loan of 6d. to purchase some drink and promised to start work on the ollowing day, but the claimant had not seen him since. •</p>
        <p>The Bench decided that "Fleetwood should pay 19s. in lieu of notice, and Bs. damages and the costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>Coal Dealers' Cruelty To A</head>
        <p>Coal Dealers' Cruelty To A</p>
        <p>Mare.</p>
        <p>SPECIAL CONSTABLES AT WORK.</p>
        <p>Two brothers in partnership as coal dealers at Lymm, were heavily fined by the Lymm magistrates on Tuesday for causing unnecessary suffering to a mare. The evidence disclosed a shocking state of affairs, and if it had been within the power of the Bench—which consisted of Messrs. A. Watkin (presiding), S. W. Gould, R. H. Gotobed, S. Davies, A. W. Callender and T. J. Ridgway—it was stated that the defendants would have been sent to prison. The case was that in which Jeremiah Gough, of Oldfield Cottage, Rush Green; and Joseph Gough, of Booth's Hill, Lymm, who carry on business as coal dealers in Thelwall and Lymm, were summoned by the R.S.P.O A. for causing unnecessary suffering to a mare Mr. T. S. Steel prosecuted on behalf of the Society, and Mr. Arthur Browne represented the defendants.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>Too Weak To Help itself.</head>
        <p>Too Weak To Help itself.</p>
        <p>Opening the proceedings, Mr. Steel said thatl the defendants were formerly colliers at Pem- berton, and came to Lymm about eight months two to start in business as coal dealers. They had three or four horses, and the chestnut mare in question was a very old one. it was in a very poor condition. On Friday, December 31st, the defendant, Jeremiah, had been working the horse .and brought it back to the stable at 5.30 in the evening. Working for them was a boy named Johnson, and when the horse came in he ordered the boy to take it to a field at Thelw.all. It was taken .there,. without being given any food. The boy was told to get on the 'horse's back., but the boy said it had, so many sores on it that he would lead the animal. At that time it was very wet. There was a little pastire in the field and a small haystack, but Ithe home was too weak to help itself. Jeremiah went to the field on Saturday morn- ing and seemed to have seen the condition of tile horse, but .did nothing to it. On the Sunday morning at about eleven o'clock, the boy John- -son took it some food and found the mare stand- ing in 'the field With its bead hanging down. It was trenibling and appeared to be very cold. On the same ,day, Mr. James Hampton, the section leader Of the special constables, in eonsequenee ,certain information, went to this field and 'there found the mare lying down in a very crihausteti ,condition. It was in a most shocking condition. He communicated with Police Sergeant Davies and, at 2.30, ac_com- pa-riled by another special 'constable, they went to flie field. The animal had struggled and worked itself to a position about twelve yards away. It could not get up. They gave it some hot water and offered it seine hay, which it ate ravenouily, it ate the food as long as it could and 'then fell down exhausted. Covering it up, they tried to get it to stand and walk out of the field, but they could not. Had To Be Destroyed.</p>
        <p>Inspector Dee was sent for. He tried to get the horse on its legs, but it collapsed. When at last they did get it up, it had to be held in position. The hind quarters appeared to be paralysed. It was quite hopeless to move it and was, therefore, destroyed on the spot. Mr. Steel submitted that the defendants were 'totally unfit to have authority over animals. The inspector visited the stables and found that they were in a filthy cqndition and the horses appeared to be starving. Later, the stables were again visited and another of these poor horses was lying in the stable and groaning from greatt pain. It was suffering from paralysis, and had to 'be .destroyed 'in the stables. The details I showed a most shocking and revolting case of cruelty. The animal should not have been sent Into the field When 'in such a condition. It was old, emaciated and had sores on its back. Edward Johnson (13), of Bradshaw-lane, Latchford, gave evidence in support of this statement.</p>
        <p>Cross-examined 'by Mr. Browne, witness main- tained that the 'horse had been working all the week-. Ile denied 'that he was told only to take the 'horse ont for exercise. When he took it out, 'he aid not know 'that there was anything wrong With the 'horse, except that there were sores on its -baelc.</p>
        <p>In rerply to the Chairman, :the boy said that althOugh -hit was so young, Ike was responsible for the feeding of the 'horses. Stabiles Filthy.</p>
        <p>'James Hampton, a blacksmith, of Thelwall, and a section leader of the special constables, in reply to Mr. Steel, said that the sores on the horse were not protected from the weather.</p>
        <p>Cross-examined, he adznitted it was quite pos- sible for paralysis to come on at once. ,James Harrison, a gardener, residing at Thel- wall, and who is also a special constable, de- posed that he had seen the horse working one day during the week previous to the Saturday. In all his life he had never seen -a horse in such a condition. He was surprised that it had not been reported .before. Sergeant Davies gave evidence as to visiting the stables. He said that they were in a filthy condition. In one stable there was no sign of bedding or food. The remaining horses were dirty. Inspector Dee, of the N.S.P.C.A., ,said that even if the horse had been in good condition, the weather at the time was not fit for it to be turned out. From the bills that had been supplied to him, he calculated that the horse was provided' with only 61b. of food daily, whereas it should have been supplied with fifteen or sixteen or eighteen pounds. Addressing the Bench, Mr. Browne declared that the boy Johnson was mistaken; the horse had not worked all that week. On the Saturday morning it was thought that the horse should have some exercise and he was told to take it out and bring it back to the stables. Perhaps the boy did not understand or, perhaps, he did not hear what was said to him.</p>
        <p>Jeremiah Gough, in the witness box, stated that the horse was given as much food as pos- sible.</p>
        <p>" Specials " Complimented.</p>
        <p>The Bench retired for a few minutes and, on their return, the Chairman said that they con- sidered the case was the worst that had ,ever been before the Court. They felt very strongly that this yoang boy should never have been put in charge of the horse, and they believed every word that he had said. If they could have done so, they would have sent both the defendants to prison without the opti,on of a fine. They were sorry they could not do so, and the defendants would be fined £lO each and costs, the alterputive being two months' hard labour.</p>
        <p>Mr. Steel, on behalf of the R.S.P.C.A., said he wished to express, both to the police and to the special constables, thanks for having assisted the Society in bringing the facts befote the Court.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: We feel strongly that the special constables have given their evidence very straightforwardly and have been of great use.</p>
        <p>Inspector Sutherland said the police were much obliged to the "specials "; it was they who had brought the case to the notice of the police.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0182" n="art0182">
        <head>WORKMAN SUMMONED BY FARMER.</head>
        <p>WORKMAN SUMMONED BY FARMER.</p>
        <p>For haring left his work without notice, Wm. Fleetwood, of Hig)ier-lane, Lymm, was sum- moned by Wm. Henry Barlow, of Mopper Brow, High Legh, to attend the Lymm Petty Sessions on Wednesday, but he failed to put in all appearance. The case, which had been adjourned from a previous Court, however, was heard in his absence.</p>
        <p>• Mr. Barlow claimed 195., a week's wages, and 16s. due to loss of trade. He said that Fleet- wood was engaged for the threshing season on September 19th, at the rate of 19s. a week and his food. He worked until 21st November, but did not turA Cori Dkcember Ist hei came and asked for his insurance card, and on</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <head>WANTUD A " LIVENER."</head>
        <p>WANTUD A " LIVENER."</p>
        <p>Objects To Keeping Policemen In Idleness.</p>
        <p>"I was badly in need of a ' livener,' " was the plea of Frederick Taylor, labourer, of The Green, Partington, who was charged at the Lymm Sessions on Wednesday with being drunk on the premises of the Greyhound Inn on December 4th. He was also charged with using obscene language on the same date.</p>
        <p>P.C. Bolsover said that at 2.45 p.m. on the 4th of last month, in consequence of what the de- fendant's nine-year-old child told him, he accom- panied her to her home, and• there heard the defendant making use of obscene language to his wife and children. • Later, on the same day, he found the defendant in the Greyhound Inn. He was drunk and standing by the bar, cursing the manager. He threatened the constable and all the licensees of Partington. His wife had made several complaints about him. The defendant commented that the witness's evidence was a pack of lies. Mr. Clarke, the manager of the Greyhound Inn, said that when the defendant visited his premises, he (witness) refused to serve him be- cause hp had already had sufficient.</p>
        <p>The defendant said that he went into licensed premises because he was badly in need of a " livener." The policeman, added the defend- ant, called him an ugly name and he did not think it was fair that he should pay to keep the policeman in idleness, and then be Called that.</p>
        <p>The constable denied the allegation that he used a defamatory expression. The defendant was fined 2s. 6d. for using obscene language and 7s. 6d. for being drunk on licensed premises.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <head>OBSCKNE LANGUAGE.</head>
        <p>OBSCKNE LANGUAGE.</p>
        <p>Local Magistrates Determined To • Stamp It Out.</p>
        <p>Three men were brought before the Bench at the 4.4ymm Sessions on. Wednesday on charges of using obscene language, and when imposing a fine on one offender, Mr. A. Watkin (who was pre§iding) commented that there were a good many of these cases and the Bench were going to do their best to put Vim down. _ .</p>
        <p>The first case was that in which William Lowndes, labourer, of Fletcher-lane, Lymm, was charged with using obscene language on December 14th.</p>
        <p>Police-Sergeant. Davies said that as a result of the defendant's conduct and language, on this date, his wife was crying out "Murder." It appeared that he had thrown something on the fire, and his wife thought that it would set the house on fire.</p>
        <p>Defendant: I was only lighting a cigarette with a candle. He contended that even if he did use obscene language it was in his own house, and it would be impossible to hear it in the street. He was fined ss.</p>
        <p>William Simpson, a farm labourer, of Ditch- field-lane, High Legh, was fined 7s. 6d. for using obscene language on December 26th; and Michael Fahy, an Irish labourer, of North- wood House Farm, High Legh, was fined ss. for using similar language on the same date.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>FOLLOWING A CLUE.</head>
        <p>FOLLOWING A CLUE.</p>
        <p>Police-Sergeant's Shrewd Work As A</p>
        <p>Detective.</p>
        <p>The exploits of Sherlock Holmes are suggegted by the conduct of a Lymm policeman, and the story, as heard at the Lymm Petty Sessions on Wednesday, reads very much like a plot from one of Sir Arthur Conan Doyle's novels. The case was one in which Thomas Cox, a boatman, was charged with stealing on the night of January 13th, five hens from Mr. Thos Warburton, who resides near Lymm" Bridge. Mr. Warburton- said he kept about thirty fowls in a garden at the rear of his house. At five o'clock on the 13th January he shut them up in the hut, and they were then quite safe. Next morning he saw that the wire fencing had been lifted up so as to enable .a man to get underneath. Witness then missed some fowl. On the afternoon of the 14th he was shown five fowls at the Lymm Police Station, which he identified as being his property. He valued them at 4s. 6d. each.</p>
        <p>Sergeant Griffiths said that on the 14th of January he received information that five fowls had been stolen from Mr. Warburton. He went to the scene and there traced narrow clog marks which went in the direction of the. canal. He made inquiries and found that a boat had been there during, the previous day, but had gone away during the night. He traced the boat to Burfoot Wharf and found the prisoner in his 4abin. He examined the. latter's footwear and found that the size of the clogs corresponded to the size of the footmarks near Mr. Warbur- ton's cote. He proceeded to examine the boat and found that it had a false bottom. He lifted up a plank and, underneath, found four fowls. The'prisoner then said "I May as well give in," and produced the other fowl.</p>
        <p>The prisoner pleaded guilty, and said he dii not know what came across him.</p>
        <p>He was sentenced.to,lldays' hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>GRAPPENHALL.</head>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL.</p>
        <p>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT</p>
        <p>Grappenhall Detachment.</p>
        <p>Orders for week commencing 23rd January Parade (Uniform). Saturday, January 29th. Fall in at Head- quarters at 3 p.m. sharp.</p>
        <p>Drill. Tuesday, January 25th. Ftil in at Head- quarters at 7.45 p.m. sharp. Musketry. Monday, January 24th. Friday, January 28th. Fall in at the Range, Bank Quay, at 7.30 p.m. sharp. See list at Headquarters for names of men required to attend on each date.</p>
        <p>Orderlies. Section-Commander C. d. Hutchings. Private W. Johnson. • By Order.</p>
        <p>F. A. FROST, 0.C., t• (Platoon Commander).</p>
        <p>• Headquarters, 22nd January, 1916. • A Church Parade, in which the Stoekto: Heath detachment will jOin, will be held on Sue day, the 30th inst. Full particulars in next orders.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>THEL WALL.</head>
        <p>THEL WALL.</p>
        <p>Soldiers For The Convalescent Home.—. The monthly pound day at the Thelwall Heys Military Convalesce/A Home was he'd on Thursday, and resulted in gifts of money and goods to the value of £l3 being received, for which Mrs. Aylmer Frost, the honorary house- keeper, wishes to express her thanks to the donors. The honorary treasurer has also to acknowledge gratefully the receipt, throUgh Mr. Robert Hutton, of the sum of £B, being amount collected by the employes of gessrs. Peter Walker and Co., Ltd. 'towards the funds of the hospital. The following patients were admitted on Saturday of last week from the Lord Derby War Hospital, Winwick :—Lance- Corporal Alfred Ewens. • 2nd South Wales Borderers; Lance-Corporal B. Wilkin, 9th , Essex; Privates Fred Driver, 4th Leicesters; R. . Tweedale, Ist K.O.R. Lancashires; W. Forsyth, 10th Scottish Rifle;; F. Baines, 2nd West Yorks; Charles Trigger, Ist Royal North Devon; William Williamson, K.0.Y.L.1.; William Murray, Ist Lancashire Fusiliers; ,David, ~Lister, lath -SakieeWin;'Yord; D., L:igh,t,i Infant 7; and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>What About The Fire Brigade?—ls the local tire brigade ready to meet any emergency? The matter was referred to at the Parochial -Com- mittee meeting on T.uesday evening, and it was suggested that the brigade had not practised for about twelve months, though the equip- ment was in good order. Ultimately, the com- mittee decided that a special meeting of the Fire Appliances Committee should be held, and that more frequent practices should take place.</p>
        <p>Present At The Parochial Committee Meeting. —The monthly meeting of the Parochial Com- mittee was held at the Council Schools on Tues-</p>
        <p>day evening. Mr., G. Piling presided, and there were present: Messrs. J. 11. Clark, H. J. West- brook, John Hallows, F. Warburton, F. Pear- son, C. Dukes, F. Rose, W. Mclntyre and J. McDermid. The officials present were Messrs. W. E. Hough (acting clerk) and S. Wright (surveyor).. .•</p>
        <p>Distribution Of Armlots.—The khaki armlets arriVed at Stockton Heath on Wednesday after- noon, and already they are becoming conspicuous about the village. The distribution has been going on at the Church Rooms and, each even- ing, numbers of men have presented their white cards for the armlet, as a preliminary to the step by which they will exchange the khaki armlet for a khaki suit. Altogether about 200 armlets have been distributed.</p>
        <p>New Lighting System.—The Warrington Cor- poration are suggesting certain alterations in the lighting arrapgements of the district, and it is intended to give a better system of lighting at a reduced cdst.</p>
        <p>At the meeting of the Parochial Committee on Tuesday evening, the following letter was read from. Warrington As more than half the lamplighters have enrolled themselves for mili- tary service, the Gas Committee have decided to fix clocks for the lighting and extinguishing of the lamps. They are purchasing two types of controllers, one to light in the evening and extinguish at midnight, and the other type, with additional mechanism, to light the lamps early in the evening and extinguish at daybreak." Mr. McDermid, on behalf of the Lighting Com- mittee, said the letter had been considered and the committee recommended that quotations should be secured for both types of controller for • the existing lamps and also for twelve additional lamps with the double type con- troller.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>GREAT DIVIDER.</head>
        <p>THE GREAT DIVIDER.</p>
        <p>Memorial Service To Fallen Heroes.</p>
        <p>St.-Thomas' Church was filled on Sunday after- noon with a congregation which offered prayers on behalf of the Stockton Heath men who have given their lives for their country during this war.</p>
        <p>The names, as read by the Vicar, the Rev. James Collins, were: Perez Henrique, James Lockhart, Harold Richardson, Peter E. Richard- son, ,and Benjamin Savage. The service was most impressive. It opened with the hymn "Now the labourer's task is o'er," and was followed by the Psalms "Out of the deep have I called unto Thee, 0 Lord," and "The Lord is my shepherd." A, short address was given by the Rev. W. G. J. Lasseter, the vicar of Antrobus. He selected as his text: "And there was no more sea" (Rev. xxi. 1). He said that death was the great divider for the time being, but there was this consolation: they had the great sympathy of God Himself. The time must come for all when they should stand and see a new earth, when there should be no more separation, when "there shall be no more death, neither sorrow, nor crying, neither shall there be any more pain." There would be no mystery of life and death, and they would see God face to face. God would reveal all in His own good time. A number of prayers from the Burial Service were said by the Rev. James Collins, and the organist played the Dead March. Other hymns sung during the service were "The saints of God! their conflict past," and "On the Resurrection morning."</p>
        <p>Among the congregation were the Stockton Heath platoon of the Cheshire Regiment of the V.T.C. under Platoon-Commander F. Warbur- ton and the Church Scouts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>FLOODED CELLARS.</head>
        <p>FLOODED CELLARS.</p>
        <p>Councillor J. Pemberton Asks For Common Justice.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Pemberton, of Heathcot, Stockton Heath, and a member of the Warrington Town Council, has written to the local Parochial Committee complaining that in time of floods the cellars of some shops which he owns become flooded. In his letter, which was read at the com- mittee's meeting on Tuesday evening, Mr. Pemberton-stated that for some-years there had been very serious complaints with regard to the surplus water drain passing his shops in Stock- ton Heath., Every time there was a heavy rain- fall the cellars were flooded and so annoying and disagreeable was this that, unfortunately for him, the tenant in the middle shop had left. The late tenant had toldohim he had repeatedly complained of it. The committee would under- stand what a serious matter this was. Being a lock-up shop, the 'collar as used for stock purposes, and when eatables were placed there it was not only a serious loss to the tenant, but there was also the danger that some of the stock which had not been badly damaged would be sold to the public. "I do think," added Mr. Pemberton, "in common justice and in equity, that the Council ought to do something with this defect in the public sewers, I, on my part, have done everything that an owner can do, and it is only fair that I should get proper ser- vice for the rates that I pay instead of damage. I am sure it would pay the District Council to put this matter right, at whatever cost, as it ,is to their benefit, because they are now losing the rates on the shop and I am also losing my rent."</p>
        <p>The Surveyor commented that if the cause of the flooding of the cellars was the surface roads it was a matter for the County Council. It was left to the Surveyor to inspect the property and report to the Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
        <head>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath Detachment.</p>
        <p>Orders for week commencing 24th January : Monday. Drill at Headquarters. Fall in 7.45 sharp.</p>
        <p>Thursday. Drill at Headquarters. Fall in 7.45 sharp.</p>
        <p>, . Saturday. Uniform. Parade. Fall in at Vieforia, square 3 p.m.</p>
        <p>Sunday. Church Parade. Pall in Victoria-square al 9.45 a.m. to attend service at Grappenhall (By request of the O.C. Grappenhar Detachment). By Order. F. WARBURTON, Officer Commanding.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <head>PAD GATE.</head>
        <p>PAD GATE.</p>
        <p>UI Is water Lecture.—The usual meeting of the Wesley Guild was held on Monday evening, when a very interesting lantern lecture on " Ulls- water." was given by Mr. C. Wilcockson, of Stockton Heath. The slides shown were all made from actual snapshot's 'taken by the lecturer, which added additional interest to the subject. lie described the various interesting walks along the Lake, the beautiful vegetation, and among the. numerous places visited were Penrith Castle, Pooley Bridge, Kirkstone pass, Patterdale, and last, but not least, a climb to the skimmit of Helvellyn. A hearty vote of thanks was pro- posed by Mr. J. Fryer, and seconded by Dr. Bennett. The Er. A. J. Alcirton Occupied the Chair. The lanternists were Messrs. W. E. Plinston and Appleton.</p>
        <p>Prize Distribution and Cancert.—The annual prize • distribution and concert in connection with the Padgate Wesleyan Sunday School was held on Friday of last week. Tea was provided for the teachers and scholars, after which a concert was held, presided over by &gt;yor- ton; The childreu of the primary departznerit</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="3" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <head>AN4'.</head>
        <p>AN4'.</p>
        <p>played a prominent part in the programme, and reflected much credit on the training they had received from Miss L. Wright and her assistants. The items given were: "Old King Cole," "Nursery Rhyme Tea Party," and " Good-night "; and recitations were given by Jack Davies, May -Sutton and Alfred ,Taylor. The adult children had been carefully trained by Mrs. Dakin and others, and caused much amusement by their action songs entitled "Snow- balling" and "The •Singaphone," the part of the professor in the latter being efficiently taken by Mr. H. Monks. Recitations were also given by Roy Datkin, Gordon Heckle, Eileen Morton, and Alice Meredith, and a solo was contributed by Miss Marion Ball. Miss Hilda Lunt gave a pianoforte selection, and Miss Wright and Miss Sutcliffe were the accompanists. The prizes were afterwards kesented to the scholars by Miss Mason, one of the teachers of the Sunday school, and oranges and nuts were distributed at the close of the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>'2i</head>
        <p>'2i</p>
        <p>FEARNHEAD.</p>
        <p>Risley Independent Methodists.—The annual prize distribution took place in the schoolroom on Saturday evening. Mr. G. Holmes occupied the chair, and made some very interesting refer- ences to his younger days, the many prizes he had gained, and the source of pleasure they had Leen to him in later years. The prizes were presented by Mrs. G. Brimelow, of -Croft, who spoke a few v ords to the children and advised</p>
        <p>them to continue their attendance at the Sun- day school. Games were indulged in and gramophone selections were played during the evening, the gramophone and records being kindly lent by Messrs. A. Jackson and A. Wor- rall. Votes of thanks were proposed by Mr. R. Bold and Mr. J. Higham.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>A First-class Stenogranher.—At a recent ex- amination held for the granting of teachers' certificates in connection with the Gregg system of shorthand, Mr. T. Lowe, who is a keen stenographer, was a highly successful candi- date, passing first-class with honours. As a member of the Order of Gregg 'Artists he worthily upholds its commercial status.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>GREAT SANKEY.</head>
        <p>GREAT SANKEY.</p>
        <p>A Three-days' Party.—The annual winter treat given to the scholars of St. Mary's Sunday School, Sankey and Penketh, was • held on Thursday, Friday and Saturday of last week, the division being necessary owing to the large number of scholars. On Thursday about 100 of the seniors sat down to tea, after which a num- ber of games and competitions gave them a jolly evening. On Friday the juniors had their turn, the youngsters doing justice to the good things -provided, and, after tea, spending an enjoyable evening with the magic lantern of their old friend, Mr. James Woodward. On Saturday evening the two top classes from each school and the gible Classes and teachers held a social evening. A concert occupied the first</p>
        <p>half of the evening. Miss "E. Duttim and Messrs. J. Kay and E. Darlington were the singers, Miss Hobson the pianist, and Miss Higham, elocutionist. The Sankes Choral Class, under the direction of Mr. J. Ray, rendered two anthems. After supper the evening was given over to games and dancing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>LIGHTS ON VEHICLES.</head>
        <p>LIGHTS ON VEHICLES.</p>
        <p>The Rules That Prevail hi Cheshire. The special attention of the public is drawn to tie advertisements which have recently ap- peared in newspapers throughout the county, and to the posters exhibited at Police Stations anit other places, setting forth the requirements of the Order made by the Secretary of State. It is important for the public to bear in mind that Part I. of the Order, which is in operation throughout the whole of Cheshire, requires every vehicle, when being used on any highway be- teen half an hour after sunset and half an hour before sunrise, to carry two separate lamps, one showing a white light to the front and the other a red light to the rear. This applies not only to carriages, carts, vans, lurries, and other vehicles, but to bicycles and tricycles. Handcarts also come within the requirements, but need only carry one lamp which should show a white light to the front and a red light to the rear. Part 11. of the Order refers to the prohibition of the use of headlights and restrictions on other lights on vehicles, and 'prohibits the use ' on motor cars of headlights of all descriptions, and also provides that, excluding the rear lamp, not more than tivo lamps showing a light to the front may be used on any. vehicle. In order that the public may be the better able to understand exactly what area is affected by Part It, we are informed by Mr. W. Leah, the Acting Chief Constable of:Cheshire, that he has recently been in coMmunication with the Home Office on this point, and has received al intimation that the boundary of the area affect&amp; by Part 11. 'is the Chester-Frodsham-Runeorp main road. It therefore follows that all vellicleF travelling along the said main road or any other road to the west thereof, including The whole of the Wirral Peninsula, must comply with the re- quirements of Part 11. of the Order. We should advise persons requiring further information to obtain the same from their local police. The city of Chester is outside. the area of Part 11.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
        <head>SAWING A LIVE SHELL.</head>
        <p>SAWING A LIVE SHELL.</p>
        <p>BLUE FUNK.</p>
        <p>Effect Of A Parsons' Battalion</p>
        <p>On Germany.</p>
        <p>"The Huns could hardly fail to be seized with blue funk if they were faced with a battalion of parsons," declares the Rev. A. M. Mitchell, Vicar of Burtonwood, in his Parish Magazine.</p>
        <p>Commenting on the. appeal of a thousand unmarried clergymen fo the .Bishop of London for permission to enlist,. Mr. Mitchell writes:— f` Hip, hip, hurrah for the London parsons, the single oncs, the eligible ones! Good luck to the 1,000! May success attend them! May ten or twenty thousand more follow their patriotic. example! What will the good Bishop say? Can he, so patriotic himself, resist such an appeal? If by any choice his Lordship should say 'Nay,' the 1,000 will not therefore desist from an endeavour to get as quickly as possible into khaki, but will renew their ap- peal and agitate, agitate until it is granted. "Is there to be one hiw for the, young lay- man and another for the young clergyman? Is one to be unstarred and the other starred? Is the able-bodied civilian to be forced into the ranks whilst the equally able-bodied parson is chained to home and comfort? In a war . . . for the overcoming of evil, the casting down of the stronghold of cruelty, the deStruc- tion of the lying wonders of Satan, they can- not reasonably and consistently stand aloof; if •the place Of the lay brother i in the rankf., there also is the place of the cleric. • - "A battalion or two of priests and deacons in each army at the Front—western or eastern,— would act as leaven where leaven is needed. They would cheer, strengthen, and inspire their lay brethren in arms, in the Moment of danger, in the hour of battle, in the day of pain and anguish. "The Huns, confronted by clerical batta- lions, could hardly fail to be seized with blue funk. Wait and see."</p>
