      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0204" n="art0204">
        <head>Otburrli</head>
        <p>Otburrli</p>
        <p>QUAKER'S VIEWPOINT.</p>
        <p>The Moral Law Condemns War Between Nations. A highly interesting address entitled "War and the .Moral Law," was delivered by Mr. Bryce Leicester, of Liverpool, at the Friends' Meeting House, Warrington, on Sunday evening. The speaker introduced the subject by. the following quotation from the writings of Pro- fessor Kant: "There are two things which : above all others fill me with awe and, wonder, the starry within." heavens above and the moral. law After briefly describing the habits and Surroundings of Kant and the revolution in education and thought which followed the teach- ings of the discoverer of the moral philosophy, Mr. Leicester proceeded to show how the statute law of a civilised country was founded and built up on the average moral law of the popula- tion of the nation, 11e illustrated the fact that law was founded on conscience by pointing out that the Lord Chancellor of this country re- joiced in the sub-title of the Keeper of the King's Conscience. Emphasis was placed on the -fact- that moral, •intuitive teaching was not a monopoly of any individuals, organisations or churches, but that it was to be found in men and women in all walks of life and examples of notable cases, in which moral law had been the guiding force in the actions of great men who dissociated themselves from all religious • communities and beliefs, were given. Following this, many quotations from the writings of soldiers, such as the Duke of Wellington, General Grant, General Butler and Field-Marshal Sir Neville Chamberlain were read, showing their opinion as to the abomina- tion of war. It was pointed out that the usual methods of international intercourse by diplomatic • means were unlikely to preserve .peace, as diplomacy itself was on a very low moral basis. Mr: Leicester then contended that war amongst nations could only he avoided by the growth of the moral _law among individuals and not by means of armaments or even treaties, which, later, had no force, except as they were the outcome of the moral growth in the people concerned, as witnessed by the 100 years of peace which has resulted between this nation • and the United States. of America, under the • Treaty of Ghent, which had the moral force of both populations behind 4t to give it its • strength. The speaker read an exposition of the • late Alexander Machtren, showing that: "It is by no means necessary to suppose that th,e Apostles saw the full bearing of the truths they . had to preach, in their relation to social con- - ditions. .They were inspired to give the Church their principles. It remained for future agE,I, 'Ander Divine guidance, to apprehend . . . these principles." As the growth of moral feeling had swept away slavery, so would it in time sweep away war, intemperance, impurity . and the miserable anomalies of our present civilisation. •' As it was difficult to-day to believe in the sincere teligion of the Samaica. or Carolina slave owners, so difficult would it be in the future to • believe in the sincere religion of those Who to- day upheld the " Christianity " of war. In conclusion, it *as' urged that each in- dividual must so foster his or her own moral growth as to spread its 'influence until all nations Were leavened and the highest ideals of moral law became the lowest standard of the civil or .statute law of the country.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0205" n="art0205">
        <head>FLJI'UIIE OF RELIGION.</head>
        <p>FLJI'UIIE OF RELIGION.</p>
        <p>Bev. Irvine Lister Looks Fon% ard Hopefully.</p>
        <p>A striking sermon on "What will be the Future of Religion?" was delivered by the Rev. 'lrvine Lister at the Cairo-street Chapel on Sunday evening. When war broke out, said the preacher, our fundamental religious beliefs received a severe blow, from which thousands had not yet re- covered. War could be described as a cataclysm which swept away a good deal of what had been held sacred in many lives. It was one of the things which had shaken the ' foundations of morality, truth and religion in many hearts, and, as yet, the reconstruction had not begun. lie remembered hearing a gentleman, not long ago, describe his experi- ences on the first Sunday after the fateful 4th of August. With the whole of the Christian service, in which formerly he had joined whole- heartedly, he found himself out of touch. It seemed to him unreal, a mockery, and during the recital of the Beatitudes a hitter 'cynicism stole over his mind, vitiating what had always been to him a beautiful and uplifting act of worship. That man had an intense struggle— a struggle of which many men were not capable —to get back to any positive faith: No one had collected and tabulated all the instances of men and women who had lost their religious idealism and their faith. No one could tell of all the agony, mental and spiritual, which the war had caused, but we knew that it would be great, and that there were large numbers of men and women in all parts of the country, who, for the time being, had lost faith in God and humanity. For them, religion was not, and never could be, the same. The old ideals and hopes had gone, and new ones would have to. be gained to take their place. Would the war, with all its suffering and uncertainty, bring men and women back from indifference to seek the consolations of religion? On this point there was much difference of opinion. Some eminent leaders already claimed fil;it that effect had in part been realised. Many who gave no thought at all to religion had certainly been brought to a consideration of</p>
        <p>For OakfilasOrlifddings a else. 1 *Ben . a t BAKING .poWDEA,</p>
      </div>
