      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0207" n="art0207">
        <head>Revival M Religion.</head>
        <p>Revival M Religion.</p>
        <p>Yet this slight movement had given hopes to some that there was going to be a revival of religion, and that it was going to be a revival of dogmatic religion, which did not presuppose any change on the part of the Church, but only on the part of the individual. If we thought that in the future there was going to be greater recognition of the authority of the Church and - more obedience to it, be for one thought we were doomed to disappointment, and he doubted whether such a thing was desirable. The Church of authority and dogma had had its .day. A religion bound hand and foot by creeds and professions had had its trial and lost its power: Before the war, men and women were weary of it; and now it stood under greater condemnation than ever. It did not seem to him that the future of religion would be in a whelehearted return to a forme' condition of affairs; it would not be to some- thing which had been before'; but would be something new, something which would pow into the hearts of men and women that which would revitalise them and give them hope. Last week he pointed out that the failure of the Church consisted in not having applied the principles of Christian religion to out society. Now, was there any hope that in that direction lay the future of religion? Was it possible ,that the future, of religion would be in this more effective sphere. We must all feel that that was the great task of religion in the future—to forsake all its obscurantism, to come down from its high throne of authority, to discard its sombre shadows and its futile .emotions, and become really and truly what religion ought to be, a very practical affair.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0208" n="art0208">
        <head>Religion is Prhcticable.</head>
        <p>Religion is Prhcticable.</p>
        <p>A close study of the first two or three con tunes revealed dearly one great thing—reli giou was a practical affair; it disseminated joy and happiness, it destroyed doubt and distrust, fear and hatred, and by its application to the common life was actually establishing an ideal society. There was the promise of great things in those early times, but there was no fulfil- ment. We know what happened to stop the beneficent effects of that simple, practical faith. A great Church was organised, the splendours of Pagan Rome established them- selves around the altars of Christian Churches, and there was an end of the simple beauty of the Christian faith. Did it not seem that soma awful blunder was there made? Did that simple, practical and effective religion require to be organised and controlled untiL its very life was choked out of it? Would it not have continued its healing and redemptive work if it had not been imprisoned in creeds and dog- mas and. articles? This great ecclesiastical corporation destroyed the very essence of the Christian religion, it hindered its work, it killed its spirit, and never agairi had it the opportunity to lay its hand upon society. And here was the indication of •what religion must be in the future. We must get back to that first 'fresh spirit away from, all the deadly influences of dogmatism and authority, away , from the dry bones of Nicene and Athanasian creeds, back to the' real life of our Lord, who, as a great and wise teacher, went from place to place creating joy and pea.ce and hope. It was not too much to hope that the war would sweep ,aw'ay what had passed for 'religion for so many centuries, and bring us back to the real light and sweetness 'and joy of Christ's human, life. Our hope for the. future was not in beliefs of. one kind _or another, but rather in the acceptance of Christ's way of life, in having His disposition and of looking upon society with the eyes of love, and of acting within society with a .heart full 'of love.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0209" n="art0209">
        <head>11% 0 CRISES.</head>
        <p>11% 0 CRISES.</p>
        <p>nCI . L. L. Roberts On Church A</p>
        <p>Country. At St. Peter's Church on Sunday, speci sermons were preached on behalf of the Church Pastoral Aid Society. The preacher in the morning was the Rev. E. L. Roberts, curate of St. Luke's Church, Liverpool-road, who Spoke of the characteristics .of our Lord and of the lessons which we could learn from. them. - Jesus, he said, lived a life of poverty. In .this respect there was a great lesson for us to-day. An ex- travagant life was a sin .at any time, but chiefly so to-day. Simplicity was another character- istic of our Lord's • life. There had been too much respect shown in the past to men of pom- posity. 'Great demands were being made upon us at the present time ()Wing to the war. People had responded magnificently to their country's need, but, in the midst of all this we'should not forget that our Church, too, was passing through a great and critical time. We should not forget the fact that the Church was suffering terribly, and, if it was to continue the good work which it had already done, we should have to give it every possible support. Toil was another characteristic of our Lord.. Be claimed no rest, except when he went up into the moun- tains, to pray to His Heavenly Father. " then, was another lesson for us. Idleness was sin in these days. Every man and 'woman shOuld be doing something, antk if possible, some work for King and country. Although Jesus had many troubles He never became .sorrowful or downcast. So it should be with us.</p>
        <p>•ere,</p>
        <p>Midst all the stress and strain of these bitter days, we should keep up our hearts, and bear our troubles manfully. The preacher at the evening service was the Rev. J. R. Jones, vicar of Qreat Sankey.</p>
        <p>There were good congregations throughout the day.</p>
        <p>One of the Old Church of England Societies. On Monday evening the annual meeting was held in the Mission Room, John-street, Mr. T. S. Steel, vicar's warden, presided. The Rev. E. R. Clare, the newly-appointed organising- secre- tary for the Diocese of Liverpool, Manchester, Chester and Bangor, was the speaker. He dealt With the various phases of the work of the Society, and pointed out that it was one of .the oldest societies connected with the Church of England, and in consequence .of the war, like other societies, there was a decrease in the subscriptions. He urged the necessity of sup- porting the society at the present time in view of the fact that there never had been a greater call to prayer on behalf of evangelistic • work. The war had made itself felt in every- People were touched and ready to receive spiritual counsel and help as .never before. The large number who .mourn or who are oppressed, as well as the thousands in training for the Front needed the help and sympathy 9f Christian prayers. The Society assisted parishes in. densely-populated; districts to maintain a body of clergy to help the people, many of whom had given of their best in the service of their country. , _ _ _ . _</p>
        <p>On flip proposal of Mr. T. Reddish (people's warden). seconded by the Vicar (the Rev. B. Percival), a hearty vote or thanks was accorded to the Chairman' and the speaker.</p>
      </div>
