      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0213" n="art0213">
        <head>WOMAN'S WORLD.</head>
        <p>WOMAN'S WORLD.</p>
        <p>son—the lady in question—has studied at the Free and National Dental Hospitals. The latter has been, until lately, the only' institu- tion of the kind in London open to women students. There is every probability that open- ings for women dentists will multiply in the near future, and this accounts possibly for the increase in the number of students. There will be a demand for women dentists in ele- mentary and other schools, and several have I already founded good private practices.</p>
        <p>The Medicine Garden. Mrs. Norman Grosvenor has formed a society for encouraging the culture of medicinal herbs in cottage gardens. Years ago every garden of any size had a. portion set aside for the culti; vation of medicinal plants, and each season the leaves were carefully gathered and dried, and the roots were 'dug up and prepared for For every ill to which flesh is heir a ' remedy was found in the herb garden. Also many of the cottagers added to their scanty earnings by the sale of various plants, and Mrs. Grosvenor hopes,„ with the help of the members of her society, to revive this village industry. Chelsea once boasted a famous Physic Garden, which contained no fewer than three thousand native and foreign medicinal plants, but it is now devoted to general botanical , research.'</p>
        <p>The Pinafore Corsage.. The success of the pinafore corsage is due to its adaptability, as well as to its economy. By its means a dress can be worn for all ordinary,i day occasions, and easily converted into a demi-toilette for smart evening wear. For the latter purpose all that is required is a square or V-shaped underbodice and long elbow sleeves of a diaphanous fabric. The pinafore corsage is capable of Much variety, doubtless one of the many reasons of its popularity. It may be quite an important affair, or merely a pair of more or less ornate braces. The sales offer ' many opportunities of securing materials that can be used for the confection of this style of dress for the smallest possible outlay, and with the added assurance that the fashion is Inot a ,passing whim, but has come to make a long stay. .</p>
        <p>Interchangeable Suits. Another decidedly economical mode that will have a vogue next Spring is the suit in differ- ent materials, it has already had some suc- cess, for it is, when the fabrics are judiciously chosen, the ideal of smartness. Here again the sales should prove a great help. A piece of velvet, or velveteen, sufficient for a short- basqued coat, could be matched in two different</p>
        <p>cloths, and two skirts be made to wear with the coat. Short lengths, always sold at low prices, would answer well for such a purpose. It may also be quite worth while to buy two suits that could be interchanged. Such, for instance, as a velveteen, and a plain cloth suit. Provided the colours admitted of the plan, the coats of the suits might be inter- changed, thus providing variety, with no extra expense. With an eye to future needs, short pieces of brocade or broche will prove a good purchase, for when Spring comes round again plain silk and satin skirts will be. worn with little coats or pelerine capes of 'these handsome materials, and vice-versa. To the clever home dressmaker these little garments present no difficulties, for they are quite easily made.</p>
        <p>Leather Wraps. This winter the mot exclusively, fashionable fabric for outdoor .garments is leather, a some- what unreasonable and unpatriotic mode, be- cause this material is required in large quan- tities for the equipment of soldiers, although not perhaps the particular kind of leather employed for women's wear. A very novel coat is of white leather, trimmed with black fur and black patent leather. A jacket of the same fabric has been designed that will in all probability become popular in less expensive materials since it is attractive in shape and appearance. It fastens down the front. and ' has outside flat pockets at the sides. The leather used for these coats has been through many processes, which have rendered it as supple as the finest cloth, and the coats are so cleverly cut they never look clumsy. Leather. has the advantage of imparting great warmth, and is impervious to cold winds. Dull metal buttons are 'generally used to fasten these leather coats. Sid and leather hats are also very much worn in Paris and London.</p>
        <p>Dearer Linen. The effect of the war is ever being brought home to us by the unexpected shortage of articles in daily use. Among these may be mentioned washing soda, string, dress fasten- ers, bottles, tins, buttons, and writing ,paper. A large part of our stationery materials came from Gerniany and Austria. Japan is sending various Substitutes, but the cost of freightage naturally adds very considerably to the price of the finished goods. All linen goods, too, will be very much dearer in the near future. Much of the flax from which linen yarns were made came from Belgium. Hointeholders who can spare the cash, and whose stocks of -linen' are low, will do well to buy during the sales. Some of the shops are offering beautiful goods at. most reasonable prices.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0214" n="art0214">
        <head>HERE AND 'MERE.</head>
        <p>HERE AND 'MERE.</p>
        <p>A Prophet. Joe: Congratulate me, old man. I'm going to marry Miss Peachly. Fred: Things are coming out just as I pre- dicted. Joe: What do you mean? Fred: I told her when she refused me that she would live to regret it.</p>
        <p>Not Her First Choice., lie was excessively fond of dancing. Also he was very clumsy. She, toe, was excessively fond of dancing. But she was also the personification of grace. And now she was suffering. Already he had torn her train with his ungovernable feet, and her dainty slippers bore the marks of his toes. At last she could stand it no longer. "Let us sit out the rest of this dance," she suggested. "I am tired," . Be was reluctant. "I thought you said you could die dancing!" he accused. "So I could," she replied. "But there are pleasanter ways of dying than being trampled to death."</p>
        <p>Courting- Dues, . A respectable farmer, in the parish of Cum- nock, being a widower, went -courting a young lady, the daughter of a neighbouring farmer, in the parish of Auchinleck. The farmer, who was no great orator, but was young, had a good person, and was in affluent circumstanc9s, addressed his fair one bluntly, and proposed without much ceremony. The lady replied, in the , same frank and open way, "'Deed, Jamie, I'll tak' ye, but ye =van gie me my dues courtin' for a' that." The wedding took place accord- ingly.</p>
        <p>do, so that we cannot always be buried in tur favourite' subject.—Your sincerely, Niece Edith Buck, 11, Walker-street, Warrington."</p>
        <p>Drawing, Painting and Modelling.</p>
        <p>Nephew Harold Whittle, 123, Cartwright- street, Warrington, who writes to thank me for his prize--" a very nice book," .which he says he has read and found "very interesting "-r-gives his favourite school subject as. modelling, which teaches one to make "many useful little things." Nephew Horace Ilimsworth, 15, Fitzherbert- street, chooses for his favourite lesson, drawing. " If one learns to draw well it may possibly lead to a good situation. lam very fond of drawing," he says. "One could sit and draw for I don't know how long, it is nice to it and draw till you are really tired of it. I hope by giving my best attention to' my teacher when I have a lesson-that it will teach me to be clean, attentive ,and to do my duties." Niece Lilian Himsworth, 15, Fitzherbert- street, Warrington, writes Although I like most lessons at school, when thinking over the different lessons I think my Choice will be paint- ing. Many people have a great artistic love, and this, mingled with patience and persever- ance, enables a person to become a great Artist. Some artists are renowned, (throughout the world for their great skill in painting. I should like to learn sufficient to he able to paint beautiful pictures, which I am. sure are -always admired in every home and other .places."</p>
        <p>Geography.</p>
        <p>Niece Mary Roche, 6, Bostock-street, Bank Quay, says her favourite subject at school is geography, because it aids you when travelling from place to place and when reading of the war and the doings of our soldiers in different parts of the world; and because a knowledge of the maps of the world will enable you to help others by telling them where different places are situated. ' This energetic little niece sends us another new member—Annie Dowling. Niece Marian Barton, 42, Wakefield-street, also selects geography. - "It is just my delight when I know we are having that lesson," she says. "Geography tells us about our own country and other countries. It tells us what we get from different countries and what they grow. There are many other lessons I like, but I like that best of all, be- Cause I. like to know about the country I live Niece Marian won a prize in last September, when writing in praise of geography.</p>
        <p>History.</p>
        <p>Niece Louie Minshull, 29, Wakefield-street,</p>
        <p>writes :</p>
        <p>"Dent Uncle Jack,—My favourite subject at school is history. We are learning about the Frence Revolution in the reign of George 111. T like studying history because it is very in- teresting. We learn about different periods as</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0215" n="art0215">
        <head>HINTS ON HEALTH.</head>
        <p>HINTS ON HEALTH.</p>
        <p>Useful Information In Popular Language. How Colds Are Caught.,</p>
        <p>The majority of people do not yet recognise ! the need of keeping apart from 'others in the event of colds. Mothers, however,' ought to be on the alert to keep their babies isolated from beople with a " cold in the head." 'Allow no one with a cold to kiss the little one, nor to wipe •its tiny nose with a handkerchief that has been used. The baby should be provided with his ovn individual handkerchief and also his per- sonal towels and drinking cup. lie should not be held by one who is sneezing and wheezing in the grip of a cold or influenza. The infection in sukh disorders is found in the discharge from , the nose and throat, and in the air close around ! the patient, Which becomes infected with drop- lets of expectoration given off when he coughs or sneezes.</p>
        <p>Licking Postage Stamps. Licking a postage stamp may be the first step towards a tuberculosis camp, and chewing a !pencil that somebody else may have used, bears all the possibilities of an early funeral , for, the chewer. These are conclusions to be drawn from a recent address by Dr. Samuel G. Dixon, State Health Commissioner of Pennsyl- vania. Millions of people moisten postage stamps with their .tongues every day. These may have been shoved across 'a soiled counter or torn off with dirty hands an instant before, but, unfortunately, a habit, plus convenience, leads the majority of people to the custom of affixing them in this manner. In view of the fact. that there are thousands of 'sufferers from tuberculosis and other diseases, these seemingly insignificant acts are fraught with danger.— "Good Health."</p>
        <p>Dr. Olsen On Tobacco. Writing in "Good 'Health," Dr. Olsen says : "At the present time the yearly consumption of tobacco amounts to about One 'hundred million pounds, or somethin,, like two pounds per head. The total cost of this large amount of tobacco is -.130,000,000 in round numbers, which would .be sufficient to build and equip ten Dreadnoughts yearly:" Dr. Olsen says tobacco is a poison, and its influence on the human 'body is always poisonous, the effect differing only in proportion to the amount taken and the susceptibility of the user. No one, he says, can honestly claim that the habitual use of tobacco in any quantity, small or large, benefits health, increases strength, prolongs life, or in any other way exerts a healing influence upon the human body.</p>
        <p>we go up into higher classes. The French Revolution just shows us how the French people were treated in those days. It was a revolt of the French people against the King and Queen and the rich people: Remember me to Uncle Ned."</p>
        <p>Composition Nephew ,John Webb, 27, Catherine-street, Warrington, writes:— . " Dear Uncle Jack,—My favourite lesson at school is composition or essay writing. It is just like examination, because we are nearly always writing about history, geography or grammar. Our essay for Momlay is "The Cotton Towns of South Lancashire.' " P.S.—I got my wish at Christmas; iny brother has come home for three months on munitions."</p>
        <p>Cookery.</p>
        <p>Niece Annie Eaton. 22, Marson-street,.says:—</p>
        <p>_ . . "There 'are plenty favourite subjects at school, but I think the one I like best is the cookery lesson. We have this subject once a week, that. is, Monday. The reason why I like ' it is because we learn how to cook and how to be economical, so that if I ever have a house to keep I will know how to manage it." Niece Eva M. Morrell, 47, Price-street, War- rington, thinks cookery' has a right to be her favourite school subject. "it is most in- structive and interesting, and even sometimes exciting. At home I like to help mother with the cooking. but my brother often 'teases me and says he will be poisoned; yet when my 'cooking. is done he is .nearly always the first to sample it. Cooking is one of those subjects which' is most useful- when a girl has left school, whilst I am able even now to tell Mother new recipes. I eagerly look 'forward to my cooking day at school, and I am very glad to leave the 'three R's,'as people say, alone for my favourite lesson." Niece Merrell, it will be remembered, sent in the best eSsay last week. "I was," she writes, "more delighted than ever to see that -I had won the prize again. I hope it will be as like as the last one'l got, for it was a beautiful book."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0216" n="art0216">
        <head>Home Science.</head>
        <p>Home Science.</p>
        <p>Niece Erica Bolton; 22, Marson-street, favours home science, which she says is "a great thing to learn, because. it teaches me things which I will have to-do when I am older."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0217" n="art0217">
        <head>Citizenship.</head>
        <p>Citizenship.</p>
        <p>Niece 'Beryl Malkin, Royal Exchange Inn, Golden-square, Warington, writes:— " To one that likes almost every subject it is very hard to choose the one one likes best. I think I like citizenship the best of all lessons, because it is very interesting, and refers to the government of-our British Empire. Citizenship tells us all about our Government, Navy and Army, and our Kings. It is an oral lessori, and I do like to sit and listen to the teacher telling us about the Government, and our King's privileges. I think it is a thing that every British subject ought to know. Citizenship col-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0218" n="art0218">
        <head>CHAT and GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>CHAT and GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>By MARJORIE.</p>
        <p>Women Workers. The Young Women's Christian Association is applying for a subscription of X 25,000 by the end of February. The association maintains 35 hostels,- besides twice as many clubs in London alone, but now that hundreds of thousands of girls are streaming into factory' and business life there is hardly any limit to which it could usefully employ additional funds and resources, to the furtherance of -the welfare of these workers. Everywhere women , and girls who, before the War, lived mainly for pleasure, are now intent only on serving their country to the hest of their ability. One notable characteristic of women war workers of all degrees is the unobtrusive way in which they perform the' most disagreeable as well as the most laborious tasks. The women of the British Empire will have to find Permanent fields and channels for their activities when peace returns, and the world settles down anew.</p>
        <p>Women Engineers.</p>
        <p>WOmen have this year been admitted to take the full course of the University B.Sc. Degree at the Engineering College, London. The Metropolis boasts one expert woman engineer,' Miss- Griff, who took up this profession when it was exclusively men's work. As might be expected, she met with great opposition, but continued her ,training, working for some five , years at the bench with men, and finishing her course in the laboratory of a famous , engineer. Later she obtained from various technical colleges certificates which testified to her proficiency in every department—auto- mobiles included. Miss Griff advises girls seek- ing a profession to take up engineering, and those with a love of outdoor life to train for the mechanical side of farming. She takes pupils in her workshop, and is a firm believer in the American system of oil tractors for farm work of all kinds, and hopes soon to see electric milkers in universal use. Miss Griff holds several public appointments, and among them the position of consulting engineer to the Women's Service Munition Department.</p>
        <p>Women Dentists, 'An important co.ncesion has been made to women by the ,loyal College, of Surgery, which has this year admitted .the 'first woman - licentiate of dental surgery. Miss C. S. Watter-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0219" n="art0219">
        <head>lIONIE NOTES.</head>
        <p>lIONIE NOTES.</p>
        <p>Profitable Recipes And Useful</p>
        <p>Suggestions.</p>
        <p>The " Staff of Life," In an article on the above subject in the January "Good Health," Dr. A. B. Olsen says: "Ordinary baker's bread is made from a very fine starchy flour of wheat, which is prepared by a patent process of rolling. The best part of the grain is removed in the form of bran, grits, shorts, seconds, &amp;e. What remains in the fine white flour consists chiefly of starch with a small percentage of gluten or nitrogenous matter, and only a fraction of the mineral salts normally present in the wheat. It is inconceivable that such a flour can possess all the nourishing properties of .the whole wheat berry and it certainly does not. Such flour is not only the most expensive but at the same time the least , suitable for building and maintaining a sound, healthy body. If people were content to have the old-fashioned wholemeal bread it would not only be cheaper, but at the same, time, it would be in reality the 'staff of life,' a food that they could rely upon for ample nourishinent. Naturally the millers and bakers supply what is demanded:by the people and would doubtles be quite as willing to supply bread made from the whole wheat meal flour if that is what the people want. Wholemeal bread is not only a more perfect food, but it also possesses a sweeter and more nutty flavour, and it is in , every way far more tasty than white bread. The natural colour of good bread, both inside and outside, is a characteristic greyish-brown. White is an artificial colour which is produced in an artificial way, and is a sure sign that the bread has been robbed of much of its best and most nourishing properties." Getting Rid of Cockroaches.</p>
        <p>- - One of . the most effective, simple means •of ridding premises of cockroaches is by dusting with commercial sodium fluoride. This has been</p>
        <p>.c.;ten-s-i-;-ery--;;;Cin—lunch. rooms, bakeries and hotels- with complete success, and at little trOuble and cost.. The sodium fluoride should be thoroughly dusted on the floors, shelves and hiding places of the insects with a blower, if possible. The immediate. effect is to cause the . insects to rush about more or less blindly, and death follows in the course of a few hours.</p>
        <p>Fumigating with burning pyrethrum powder is a good remedy, but the premises need to be sealed up for from six to twelve hours. Traps are very effective. A simple one consists of a large, deep vessel, against which a number of sticks are placed on the outside and bent over so that they project into, the interior of the</p>
        <p>.".....:".•:...:••:••:••••:•.:•&lt;...:.4i;••:•::•+,:•••:..:-.:•.:. ::. • .... 4. + Children,s Corner. 4; ..:. ... •:. ____ 4. ..... .:. •:. Conducted by UNCLE JACK. 4, .:.• , ..:. ::.::.0.4.,:.,.......) • • • • • • • • • • • • • • • Children, Join This Corner! Enter The Competitions! Write Your Best! , Always Be Glad! Uncle Jack, " Examiner," Warrington,</p>
        <p>A GOOD COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>Mt DEAR CHILDREN,-</p>
        <p>You will all agree that we have this week had one of the best competitions in the history of the Corner. It Will be seen how varied are the tastes of my nephews and nieces, and how well they can state their preferences. It is quite unnecessary for me to summarise the opinions sent in—each letter speaks so well for itself. It has been more difficult than ever to decide which was the best, but my final examina- tion of the essays leads me to give the prize to</p>
        <p>Jennie Dowling, . 8, Dostock-street, Your loving, UNCLE JACK</p>
        <p>sel for a few inches. The vessel should con- tain bait, preferably stale beer, of which roaches are very fond. Another trap which has been successfully tried out consists of tin bread pans With nearly vertical sides, about three inches in height. The bottoms and sides of these should be greased With a little rancid butter, and the tins placed where roaches are numerous. The cockroaches are unable to climb, out because of the greasy sides. and they may be shaken out into boiling water and the pans used again without re-greasing. Traps of this nature placed in infested places will destroy large quantities of roaches 3111t1 keep them in check without the use of poisons or insect powders.—Bulletin of -U.S. Dept. of Agriculture.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0220" n="art0220">
        <head>Reading.</head>
        <p>Reading.</p>
        <p>"8, BOstock-street, Warrington.. "Dear Uncle Jack,—My best subject at sehobl is reading. I take very great interest in read- ing, as it enables me to follow the news of this and other countries. It is nice to be a good_ reader at present to read about the war, and the work our gallant soldiers and sailors are doing in France and elsewhere; and if at any time I should be in need of work .1.. could follow the newspapers daily and I may be able to obtain a situation, because a great many people advertise in the paper. If couldn't read should have to rely upon other people to find me work. Being a good reader; I can itay in the house on winter nights reaclihg.to my parents, whose eyesight is not as good now as it used to</p>
        <p>"Your loving niece, " JENNIE DOWLING:'</p>
        <p>"Dear Uncle Jack,—As my favourite subject it school, I think reading far excels any other which I might have any interest .in. •It gives knowledge,. and I think it a favourable pastime for everybody. I think a cosy chair, a bright tire and an interesting book are the pure. delights of a cold winter's evening. lam not in the library, but I would like very much to join. I attend Wycliffe, School, and we have an hour's reading every morning, which I thoroughly enjoy. But, of course, there is other work to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0221" n="art0221">
        <head>DOCTORS' JUSTIFICATION.</head>
        <p>DOCTORS' JUSTIFICATION.</p>
        <p>Did All He Could For Dying Drunkard.</p>
        <p>"The jury and I thought there had been some neglect of duty on your part, but we now agree that you did all you possibly could for the de- ceased." These words were ,addressed by Coroner Brig- house to, Dr. Liston, after an inquest which had been specially adjourned until Monday so that the aoctcir might give evidence. The deceased was Peter Traynor, a labourer, aged 60 years, of 5, Mill-lane, Warrington. The ,1 evidence at the previous hearing was that he was employed by Messrs. Pearson and Knowles at Dallam Forge. About six months ago he complained to his wife that a piece of slag had fallen on his left foot, but the foot soon got better. Just before Christmas, however, it started to swell. Dr. Liston was called and later the Man was removed, to the Workhouse Hospital, where he died on Monday, the 10th inst. Dr. Jagger, the resident medical officer at the Union Hospital', was of the opinion that death was due to diabetic gangrene, and Mr. A. Bottomley (clerk to the Board of Guardians) protested that it, was not fair to the patient, to the Guardians, or to the medical staff that a man. should be sent to the institution when he wa in a dying condition. Dr. Liston's Efforts For The Man.</p>
        <p>Dr. Liston, therefore, was requested to give evidence at Monday's hearing. He said that the man first came under his care on the last day' in the year. 'He was suffering from 'alcoholism and did not say anything about his swollen foot at all: On the sth inst. witness was sent for and saw Traynor at the house. His left foot was slightly swollen and bluish. His right, leg was also swollen. He was still suffering from the effects of alcoholism. Witness called to see him on the next day, and found he. had gone out, but Mrs. Traynor knew not where. Witness then told her to get him to the Union Hospital, where some control could be exercised, as he. was not in a fit state to go out. On the evening of the next day—Saturday—Mrs. Traynor called to say that she did not know how to get her husband into the hospital. and witness, therefore went to the relieving officer himself to make arrangements for his .immediate admission. Next day he telephoned to Dr. Jagger and dis- cussed the condition of the man. In reply to the Coroner, Dr. Liston said there was no sign of gahgreno on the Thursday. To Mr. Arthur Browne, who represented the widow, witness said that, although a piece of slag might have fallen on a man's foot six months ago that had not been an attribute in. his death: Mr. Bottomley remarked that he quite appreciated what the doctor had said. The Coroner observed that it would be in- teresting to know where Traynor was (.11 the Friday when the doctor called. Perhaps !se was having some more drink. Dr. Liston added that. in his view, alcohol had precipitated Traynor's death. The Coroner : Dr. Jagger said that it was diabetic gangrene. Dr. Liston: Alcoholism is the cause of that.</p>
        <p>The jury found a verdict in accordance with the medical evidence, and then the Coroner made the complimentary remarks given above. Mr. T. S. Steel (on behalf of Messrs. Henry Greenall and Co.) represented Messrs. Pearson and Knowles' Coal and Iron Co., Ltd.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0222" n="art0222">
        <head>INTELLIGENCE.</head>
        <p>INTELLIGENCE.</p>
        <p>An Irish Opinion Of Our Gm crime' Departments.</p>
        <p>it is a great mistake people make to slippose that the standard of intelligence in Government departments is higher than the average outside or that the best, brains are attracted there. The best men will not submit to the stultifying of their intelligence, and they remain. outside. It is the second-rate, industrious people, who have no personal initiative, • who are attracted to the Civil Service, and if by chance there are first-class men, they cannot move without the consent of the hierarchy above them. Just as water cannot rise beyond its own• level, so. no Government department can exhibit a higher intelligence than is possessed by its head man- darin.—" Irish Homestead."</p>
        <p>:ains a little history, and it is also general know- [edge. "Please give *Uncle Ned my best wishes." Physical Exercise</p>
        <p>Niece Annie Massey, Hope Field Cottages, Sandy-lane, Lymm, writes:— "The subject I like best is physical exercise, a thing one needs, for it builds up 'the body. When yo,u are marching it teaches you to walk straight up. Whcn drilling you are always in the fresh air, and fresh air is most beneficial."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0223" n="art0223">
        <head>First-aid.</head>
        <p>First-aid.</p>
        <p>"Dear Uncle Jack,—The lesson which T favour the most is first-aid. It is a very in- teresting subject, also a very useful one. We learn the different ways of bandaging and also bow to attend to a person who is taken suddenly ill, before the doctor arrives.</p>
        <p>"1 hope to be made one of your nieces. "Lily Webb, "27, Catherine-street. Warrington. "I have entered through my brother, John Webb."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0224" n="art0224">
        <head>NEW MEMBERS</head>
        <p>NEW MEMBERS</p>
        <p>I have great pleaotre in welcoming tht! following new 'members 'and giving them the number. under which they are entered in my big book:—</p>
        <p>542 Annie Dowling; 8, Bostock-street, Warring- ton; 13 years of age; attends Evelyn-street Council School.</p>
        <p>,543 Lily Webb, 27, Catherine-street, Warring 'ton.</p>
        <p>' 544 John Leicester, 30, Priestley-street, Bank Quay-; nine years of age; attends Trinity School.</p>
        <p>AN OLD NEW MEMBER.</p>
        <p>• Nephew Win. Leicestee,' 30, Priestley-street, Bank Quay, writes:— . " Dear -Uncle Jack,—l have not written letters for a long time now, as I have left school,- but I take A great interest in the ' Corner '.now, so I ask you a favour. Will you let me start again, please? My brother john 'would like to join. His age -is !line; name is John Leicester; is school is Trinity.—Your late, and hope -Will be again nephew, William Leicester." Nephew Wm. Leicester is welcomed heartily back to the fold. 'His name had not been taken from the Corner register, so that his number is still, as of old, 465. A CONUNDRUM.</p>
        <p>Niece Annie Massey, .of Lymm, asks the following question:— " From which word of five letters can you take two and only one will remain?</p>
        <p>Fortunately she also sends the answer, whier is " stone " (st—one). NEXT WEEK'S COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>You will see that our membership is rapidly reaching the GOO mark. I should like to have at least 700 nephews and nieces before the summer comes, and then we will try to make it an even 1,000. Would not that be a nice, comfortable</p>
      </div>
