      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0226" n="art0226">
        <head>MO PRICE OF COAL.</head>
        <p>THE MO PRICE OF COAL.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Ctreenall, president; of the Lancashire and Cheshire Miners' Federation, speaking at the annual meeting of the Federation at BoHolt on Saturday, said the triple alliance of miners, railway workers, and transport workers was an. experiment which they would have to. approach • with caution and in a fair and reasonable man- ner. It would be a powerful organisation,, and he believed' it, was calculated to achieve for the workers fair and reasonable conditions. The miners, said Mr. Greenall, had, done their bit. to help to bring the war to a victoriousissue. They had sent 20,000 persons from the collieries of Lancashire and Cheshire to the.Arnly, and in contribution for ambulances and in different ways Lancashire miners had given £70,000. In addition they had invested in the war funds £70,000. The miners of England had decided to ask for an advance of wages. Ile denied that the miners were responsible for the dearer coal. The miners had nothing whatever to do with the question of the coal prices, and no applica- tion for an advance of wages could be made until the price of coal was put up. When the question of the lsper cent., which was demanded by the miners to meet the increased cost of living, was raised it was justified by the selling price</p>
        <p>- of. coal. Had the Government dealt with the coal question at the outset of the war, as they did with the railways, and worked the mines in the interests of the community, the miners would have been perfectly satisfied to go on working at their old wages. The Government was to blame in not secur- ing for the community coal, both for domestic and industrial purposes, at a reasonable price.</p>
        <p>The blame for the increased price of coal ought to have been put on the right shoulders, and he put the blame on the Government, in that they did not take every means to secure that employers and coal merchants did not 'take advantage, of circumstances to fleece the public.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0227" n="art0227">
        <head>EXCHANGE OF PULPITS.</head>
        <p>EXCHANGE OF PULPITS.</p>
        <p>. The annual exchange of ministers attached to Free Church denominations took place on Sun- day evening. For many years this has been an interesting and nelpful event in Warrington and district. After the evening services 4 con- siderable 'number of people proceeded to Bold- street Wesleyan Church, where a communion service was held, it was conducted by several ministers, and the l?ev. J. J. Glover, of Risley, delivered an impressive address. • During the week united prayer meetings were held, in two churches—on Monday evening at the Emmanuel (Reformed Episcopal) Church, Bold-street, and on Tuesday evening at the Primitive Methodist Church, ,Legh-street. The final meeting took place on Wednesday even- ing in the Wycliffe Memorial Hall. The Rev. H. Ogden (secretary of the Free Church Coun- cil) presided over a fair attendance, and the Rev. G. Curtis, of the Hill Cliffe Baptist. Church, delivered an address. Speaking from the words': "And we know that all things work together. for good to them that love God, to them who are the called according ,to His pur- pose," he, said that here we had a universal principle. We sometimes alloWed little things to slip by, and thought they were insignificant, but we should take notice of even them. The cement in the wall, in between the big ,stones, was just as particular for the strength of the wall as the big stones. The little falls which, we had worked together for our good. Peter, !after his fall, was a better and grander man, and ready to do anything for his Master. How harmonious also Was this principle. We had to have discords, even to , make harnionious music, and, in our life, God used black as well as white colours—they worked together har- moniously.- We should not take one thing in</p>
        <p>our lives- and let it hinder us in the others, but take the sorrows. and joys together, and</p>
        <p>all would work together for our •-good. How beneficent also, was this principle. A chemist could get good even from peison, and believers in Christ could get good even from sorrow. We could get goodness and strength in •these ter- rible times of strife. God was working for a purpose. He sent us back in order that we might go forward. Our brave lads who had gone out to fight our -battles had done so in order that God's Church could go forward. It was God's way. We ehould not wander from His path in• these trying times, for we had proof, in the declaration of His own Word, that all things worked for good.</p>
        <p>family of cousins all striving to do their best and to make the world happier bt the little, but continuous and valuable efforts? 1 propose, therefore, that the next 'prize shall be given to the one who sends in the greatest number of names of ,new members to the Children's Corner. As you cannot in three days see all the friends whom you wduld like to join the Corner, this competition will not close until Tuesday, January 31st, and the name of the prize winner will be announced February sth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0228" n="art0228">
        <head>"THE ONCE-UPON-A-TIMES."</head>
        <p>"THE ONCE-UPON-A-TIMES."</p>
        <p>I don't care for the papers you Big, grown-up folks read through and through; The one I take is full of rhymes, And called "The Once-Upon-a-Times"; . It has a famous reputation, 'And an enormous Circulation.</p>
        <p>It tells of Red Riding Hood, And those poor Babes hoSt in a Wood; (lives latest news of Miss 80-Peep, Says if she's lost or found her sheep; What Lord Mayor Whittington is doing, And if the Frng has gone a-wooing.</p>
        <p>About the Woman in a Shoe, Who had so very much to do; The "Once-Upon-a-Times" knows all, • As well as Humpty-Dumpty's fall; They'll either have to fry or poach him, ' And, till then, no one may approach him.</p>
        <p>The Hatter, Dormouse, and March Hare, And other friends you'll meet with there; In fact, the oldest and the best Of papers it must be confessed; . And children of all sorts and ages, Find entertainment n its pages. —ADA L. HARRIS, in "Child's Companion."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0229" n="art0229">
        <head>RULERS OF ENGLAND IN RHYME.</head>
        <p>THE RULERS OF ENGLAND IN RHYME.</p>
        <p>First William the Norman, then William his son, • Henry, Stephen, and Henry, then Richard and John; Next Henry the Third, Edwards one, two, and three, And, again, after Richard three Henrys we see; Two Edwards, third Richard (if rightly I guess), Two Henrys, sixth Edward, Queens Mary and Bess; Then Jamie the Scot, and Charles whom they slew, . And then ,followed Cromwell, another Charles too; Next James called the Second ascended the throne, , Then William and Mary together came on, And Anne, Georges four, fourth .William all , past, With Victoria, Edward seven and George five last.</p>
      </div>
