      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0232" n="art0232">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>Pah !</p>
        <p>The Japanese innkeeper whose wholesome habit it is (vide opening paragraph in last week's "Hints on Health ") to furnish each guest with a fresh toothbrush daily, is at the opposite polo from an unpleasant Yankee I heard of many years ago. He shared an Eng- *ishman's stateroom on the voyage across the "Herring Pond," and on the first morning coolly made uso of his companion's toothbrush. The owner, who was presumed to be asleep, wit- nessed the dirty trick, and speedily hit on a plan of revenge. On rising he took the article, now for him impossible of its former use, and vigorously brushed his toes with it. "Say, stranger," exclaimed the uneasy Yankee, "isn't that a (turned nasty use to put a tooth- brush to?" "Not such a nasty use as I just now saw it put to," was the sufficient answer. Words.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>Miss Thackeray observes that "Clever people generally make the same remarks as stupid people, only in different words." I suppose that, although they are deprecated by " Vigilant " in • ,ast week's." Gossip." the coiners of new words must be classed with the clever. Anyhow, as I el.owed some time ago by a list of examples copied from their works, Dean Swift and Lewis Carroll are entitled to high rank among them. By-the-bye, some words and phrases which striginally had a particular meaning have come to mean exactly the opposite. Isaac Disraeli points oat that • A most striking instance of the change of meaning in a word is in the old law term let without let or hindrance,' meaning void of all opposition. Bence I will let you' meant '1 will hinder yon.' and not as ve should now think. I will give you free leave.'" Another is the word " prevent," and yet another is " high-minded," which, as is clear from the use made of it in the Authorised Version of the Scriptures and from the works of even such comparatively modern writers as Cowper, was by our forefathers regarded as the reverse of complimentary.</p>
        <p>Tea-drinking. May I supplement last week's article on this subject? Writing in MO, our most famous diarist mentions having sent fort cup of tea, then, as he proceeds to explain, "a new China drink." We should now put India or Ceylon for China, for I imagine that only about one-tenth of the hundreds of thousands of pounds of tea annually imported nowadays comes from China. But to revert to the days when tea-drinking was a novel sensation and a luxury affected only by the well-to-do. About the period referred to by Pepys, the " Mercurius Politicus " contained an advertisement commending "that excellent and by all physicians approved China drink," and incidentally traces the word "tea", or "tay" to the Chinese " teha." Tea-houses soon becam? popular, and the proprietor of one of these stated in his advertisement that this drink " maketh the body active and lusty. It helpeth the headache, giddiness. and heaviness thereof. It removeth the obstruction of the spleen. It is very good against the stone and gravel. It taketh away the difficulty of breath- ing, opening obstructions. . . . It vanquisheth heavy dreams; easeth the brain, and strength- eneth the memory," &amp;c., &amp;c. Certain superior persons of the other sex denounced it as fit only for women; and, indeed, the ladies seem to have taken very kindly to it from the first. Dean Swift, by-the-way, has his fling at them on this account in his "Journal of a Modern Lady." Waller, the poet, who flourished in the time of Charles 11., wrote enthusiastically al it as "the best of herbs," repressing "the vapours which the head invade," and keeping "the palace of the soul trerene." Cowper, adopt- ing a phrase coined by Bishop Berkeley, wrote, not of "the cup that cheers" (as so many per- sist in misquoting), but of -` the cups that cheer, but not inebriate." Sairey Gamp, Mt) liked her teapot handy so that she might refre,h therefrom when "so dispoged," was wont to diinte it (may I say?) with the "wee drappie " —and that reminds me that our neighbours be- yond the northern border who are supposed to have a "strong weakness" for whiskey, really have a greater one for tea. So, for that matter, have the operatives in our own factory districts. If one's fairly extensive and vat ied observation goes for anything, many of them have the tea- pot on the table at every meal. The ultimate effects are in not a few cases deplorable, corre- sponding as they do to nicotine poisoning and alcoholism in the eases of inveterate smokers and heavy drinkers of spirits.</p>
        <p>r', ",:` 4</p>
        <p>Liberty. This truly great word and its synonym hare been much in the mouths of local speakers of late. Each exponent has his point of view, and it were idle to pretend that there is no conflict. It would therefore be impossible to give a definition that would satisfy everybody;• hence one turns from the dictionary to the poet- philosophers who have expressed themselves freely, not to say passionately, on the subject. The one who declares that "the greatest glory of a free-born people is to transmit that free- dont to their children," puts in a nutshell what Dryden. Addison, and most of the poets express in more elaborate terms. " Upborne by thee." exclaims one. " my soul disdained the terms of empire offeed at the hands of tyrants." Another, urging "0 never let it perish in your hands. but piously transmit it to your children," reminds us hew dearly bought it was, " the price of SO much blood." One might multiply quotations of equal application to this present. I may - close the reference with ,Addison's aspiration:—</p>
        <p>." Do thou. great liberty, inspire our souls, And make our lives in thy possession happy; Of our deaths, glorious in thy just defence."</p>
        <p>In a 'Quake.</p>
        <p>A friapd of mine who has lived some years in F.tafford. sends me his 'experiences of the earth- (wake shock which did some damage in that region last week-end. Earth tremors have been periodically felt in most places in which T have lived: for the most severe of the kind within in: own memory. 1 have to go back to well-nigh half a century. when really serious damage was done to Itrthin public buildings in my neigh- urlibod .</p>
        <p>In the Banger Zone.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>Eat 'em Eat 'em</p>
        <p>However, while it may by no means be said that we are out of the danger zone, thee parts have not been visited by.a severe earthquake for ever so long. Whatever may have been the case it! times past, we aro now fortunately exempt from 'quakes of the Complex order. No doubt many slight ones pass unnoticed. For the rest, the majority are reported as having occurred late in the evening or in the early hours of the morning, the conditions of observation being then more favourable. As one authority has noted, the streets are then quiet, observers are generally lying down, and when they awake, as persons often do between .one and two a.m., they are in a nervous condition: their senses are alert and ready to detect the slightest move- ment.</p>
        <p>,Met His Match. .17 have just been told the following, and it is new* to me:—An old pensioner who lived out in the onntry was on his way home with his quarterly pay, and in a lonely part was stewed by a rougTh fellow with, "1 know where you've been—you've been for your pension, and I'm going to have it." "Well," responded the old soldier, putting himself into an attitude of defence, "I fought for it, and if you get it you'll have to fight for it." The fellow did not *top to argue the point further.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>Only A ontbal I Game. "'rear into 'etn ! Tear 'em up! dlive, boys! 'Clew up that line! _ 'up 1" yells the coach. "Raw, raw, raw !" yelled the boys in the leachers. oi "Mercy !" remarked the lady missionary from Abyssinia. "Is this .a cannibal country, toe", 4</p>
        <p>qt•</p>
      </div>
