      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0235" n="art0235">
        <head>"FOR OVERSEAS."</head>
        <p>"FOR OVERSEAS."</p>
        <p>Preparation And Departure Of</p>
        <p>British Company.</p>
        <p>POT AND POTTER.</p>
        <p>BY SYDNEY JEFFERY.</p>
        <p>Do you remember stopping by the way to watch the potter thumping his wet clay? These pots he . was making for the service. of his follow men, and his 'clay he selected from the richness of the public places. When he had moistened it so as to render it pliable and responsive to his will, he would roughly trim it to shape. To this bowl, maybe, be would add a piece; from this one he would chip away a piece. When they had baked in the sun he surveyed the rows of his handiwork; and a proud smile crossed his face. Surely his craft was good, and the clay—not inferior. So at length he packed up the pots, and took them away to the markets of Humanity. And each pot had a price. For him each pot had, too, a distinct indi- viduality. As one by one he saw them borne away in all directions he knew they would be put to a great variety of uses—sonic ingenious and surprising, others commonplace and unin- teresting. At careless hands some, lie know, would conic to an early end; so that their fragments would bestrew the ground before their worth had been truly proved and full credit earned for their Maker. Some again (lie thought sadly) would last long marred in beauty by an ugly crack or a missing frag- ment. Yet still he cheerfully remembered that, before the majority disappeared for ever after the mysterious fashion of men and pots, long times would be passing and work per- formed of great value. Thus his life was not lived in vain.</p>
        <p>Old England is a potter, and her sons are the clay. They are transformed by the won- derful organisation of the British Armies; and the markpts to which they go are the bloody battlefieMs of Europe. Just, as we watched the mantifacture of the clay pots, so now we may watch that of the human ones. Few things could be more interesting at present than the preparation of a British Company " for overseas." From the great mass ‘vhich the country has to offer, the raw—very raw-- material is sorted and concentrated for train- ing at certain points in these isles. Then again there is sifting; and in less time than it. takes to say "Gott strafe England ' one has, selected, the basis of an overseas com- pany. Of course there are drills te be gone through—discipline to ho instituted and ex- nlained in theory and practice. Then There is kit to be provided. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0236" n="art0236">
        <head>RECRUIT'S KIT-ASTROPHL.</head>
        <p>RECRUIT'S KIT-ASTROPHL.</p>
        <p>" Kit" is an experience which every recruit is anxious to undergo. When at length he realises his desire be realises also that more difficulties are wrought by kit than this world dreams of. Of course there is first provided the " small " kit—such a host of oddments (all with a definite and sensible use) that the making of their -various acquaintances consti- tutes an education for a proportion of the men. Then as soon as a man has been se- lected for overseas there comes " equipment —water-bottle, haversack, cooking and ration tins and canvas covers; general service blan- ket, straps; belts; gun and so on. Very im- pressive it is to notice the efforts of the men to don this full kit, over greatcoat, with neat- ness and rapidity, and to march easily and freely, pretending not to feel likcion over- stocked Christmas-tree or a one-man-band. Blanket-folding and compressing, too, is very exciting.</p>
        <p>Apart from Company Orders, this is the outward sign that a man has been selected for one of the Expeditionary Forces. But mean- while other events have been taking place. The gruff regimental doctor has been rushine them through a severe examination; no small number he has commended to the gentle care of his colleague the dentist—and certainly some of the men look very "down in the mouth " as they cross the barrack square thither. But eventually they are fitted with fine new dentures,. and are 'beginning to feel more peaceful. Stay their peace is doomed to an untimely end. " Inoculation" now takes its Ulm.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0237" n="art0237">
        <head>INOCULATION.</head>
        <p>INOCULATION.</p>
        <p>It is interesting to note the different ways in which inoculation affects men, and itow repeatedly they inquire as to one another's symptoms. The idea itself is so simple— merely the injection,of dead bacilli, germs to "saturate" the body as it were and exclude living ones—thus acting as a safeguard against enteric fever and other diseases. Of course, two days' " sick leave " are allowed to a man after accepting this boon—which he does voluntarily. While some lose all desire to eat for a day or so, and others develop a tendency to. sickness or headache, many simply evince a desire to sleep, and .others again lay aside their pipes for a- day. On the contrary, some appear to take to their pipes more than ever! The great number remain totally unaffected either by the first or second doses: and are quietly thankful that they will enjoy the ensuing immunity- from the living germs. for so many years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0238" n="art0238">
        <head>OFFICERS' PART.</head>
        <p>THE OFFICERS' PART.</p>
        <p>Meanwhile the officers, in'intervals between drilling the company and making all prepara- tion for it, hold careful inspections of kit along the long corridors of the barracks. Each man sets out his clothing, full toilet outfit, and best of other requisites upon his " bis- cuits " (Array mattresses). All is carefully overhauled and eventually pronounced satis- factory. Belief is plainly written upon the faces of the now reconciled men, and they have now no worse experience to contemplate than a two-days' visit home to bid farewell to friends + relatives and home. Of this leave all are naturally able to take advantage by the provision of free " railway warrants."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0239" n="art0239">
        <head>QU 0 VADIS?</head>
        <p>QU 0 VADIS?</p>
        <p>So at last arrives the day of departure. It is five o'clock and the great square of the bar- racks is thick with clouds of morning mist. Over the hard ground can be dimly heard the shuffling of feet; vague forms pass to and fro. The corporals—many newly created such —strut up and down with a slight hint of im- portance, us they bear the lanterns just like so many Yuletide carol-singers whipping in the party to sstart on its rounds. Then in long, straight, motionless lines the men fall.; and no sound breaks the stillness save an occasional cough or the steps of the officers as, headed by the colonel, they slowly pass along making the final inspection. In an adjacent corner of the square the drum-and- fife band discourses merry jigs and lively tunes. The ground is lined by practically the whole barracks staff, who have turned out to watch them depart. _</p>
        <p>. Quietly resuming his position before the company, the colonel says ,a few words. He is a fine man ot middle age, and his hair is steely after a strenuous military career. To the corps he has just brought high honour by reL ceiving from His Majesty a Companionship of the Bath. Clearly his voice rings out amid ,the hush. The men go at a great calling; in them is reposed a profound trust, and he knows they will keep it, and keep it well, according to the traditions of their corps. They will meet with temptations, hut he knows they will overcome them. In the workshops they will be called upon to give of their very best, and ho depends that their devotion will be whole-hearted. He will watch for such; and would only remind them'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0240" n="art0240">
        <head>Continued in next column.</head>
        <p>Continued in next column.</p>
        <p>I/ V</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 22, 1916.</p>
        <p>NEW SAYINGS OF JESUS.</p>
        <p>Documents Recovered From Dust Heaps Which Give Hitherto Unknown Teachings Of Jesus.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0241" n="art0241">
        <head>ROMANCE OF ARCH/jEOLOGY.</head>
        <p>A ROMANCE OF ARCH/jEOLOGY.</p>
        <p>ENIEEM</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0242" n="art0242">
        <head>BY ARTHUR</head>
        <p>BY ARTHUR</p>
        <p>Most people are aware that in recent years there have come to light scraps of document containing sentences which are reputed to be the utterances of Jesus. Others, who are more widely read, know that reputed " teachings " exist in the ancient writings of the Church.</p>
        <p>Though the task and the subject is of the greatest interest, we do not remember to have seen any attempt to gather these so-called "say- ings" together in popular form, and as the sub- ject cannot fail to be interesting to many, .we propose to attempt it for the readers. of " The Examiner," giving a brief account of the dis- covery and the whereabouts of each quotation. In the year 1849, at a Mohammedan Mosque near Agra, Dr. Duff discovere4 an inscription. which purported to be a saying Of Jesus. The written words are; "Jesus, on whom be peace, has said</p>
        <p>The world is merely a bridge: Ye are to pass over it, but not to build your dwellings on it."</p>
        <p>Discovered far away •in 'lndia. an echo, per- haps, of something spoken by missionaries long ago, the discovery reminds Us that many reputed teachings of Jesus were carried in memory and in memory only. Some, as we know quite well; found no place in, the Gospels. One of these Paul rescued from oblivion in his reminder to the Elders of Ephesus that Jesus had said It is more blessed to give than to receive.</p>
        <p>That other sentences were cherished we can- not doubt. Repeated by missionaries, they would .be quoted by disciples in their exhorta- tions to one another; often lost, but sometimes recorded in a more or less accurate form, and sometimes, by happy circumstance rediscovered in later centuries.</p>
        <p>Some of them come Z,I) o the Gospel teachings that we cannot doubt that they are, to say the very least, faithful echoes. Others convince us both by their style and flavour, and however loth we way be to invest a stray sentence recovered from the dust of Egypt with the thithority that attaches to Gospel teaching, we feel that it cannot be set aside.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0243" n="art0243">
        <head>FOUND IN EGYPT.</head>
        <p>FOUND IN EGYPT.</p>
        <p>As the documents found in Egypt are possibly the oldest, we will quote them first. In 1897, while excavating at Oxyrhynchus, Dr. Grenfell- and Dr. Hunt discovered a large col- lection .of papyri dating from the first to the seventh century of the Christian • era. Most valuable among the many fragments was one which professed to contain some "Logia" or say- ings of Jesus. In 1904 a somewhat similar piece of good fortune came to them, and they found also a fragment of an unknown Gospel. The discovery of the papyri of Egypt is a. romance of archteology. The rubbish of the ancient towns on the Nile was deposited in pits, and owing to-the dryness of the climate and soil, even the most fragile objects have been pre- served. Mingled with the remains of pottery and building rubbish, thousands of documents have come to light. The writing material of Egypt was a kind of paper made from the pulp of rushes. This crisp paper, or papyrus, has wonderful tenacity, and recorus twO thousand years old are preserved with perfect clearness. Letters, agreements, bills—the contents of lawyers' offices and grocers' shops—have been found in large numbers, and scholars have had the very happy task of studying the every-day language of the people and taking note of social sidelights which come from these documents. .</p>
        <p>Incidentally, much light has been gained upon the language of the New Testament, and certain technical and business words that occur therein can be read for the first time by us in their true application.</p>
        <p>THE EARLIEST CHRISTIAN DOCUMENT. The discovery that concerns us for the moment was that of some fragments which con- tained some " Login " or " Sayings " of Jesus. It is well-known that in the first century there existed a document called the "Login," which is the Greek word for sayings or teachings. It was probably the earliest Christian writing, and contained a collection .of the words of Jesus. Unfortunately for us, this precious book is lost. Yet it seems quite probable -1.1.. tat in these fragments of papyrus we have some pages from it, for it is to be noted that ,qich sentence be- gins with " Jesu login," that is " Jesus saith." Here are those discovered in P. 497</p>
        <p>Jesus saith, Except ye fast to the world, ye shall in no wise find the kingdom of God; anti except ye make the Sabbath a real Sabbath ye shall not see the Father. . . and then shalt thou see clearly to cast cut the mote that is in thy brother's eye. Jesus saith, I stood in the midst of the world and in theiflesh was I seen of them, and I found all men drunken, and none found I athirst among them, and My soul grieved over the sons of men, because they are blind in their heart and see not. . . .</p>
        <p>Jesus saith, Wherever there are (two) they are not without God, and wherever there is one alone, I say, lam with him. Raise the stone and there thou shalt find Mc; cleave the wood and there am I.</p>
        <p>Jesus saith, A prophet is not acceptable in his own country, neither doth a physician work cures upon them that know him.</p>
        <p>Jesus saith, A city built upon the top of a high hill and stablished can neither fal! nor be hid. Jesus saith, Thou hearcst with but one ear . .</p>
        <p>The " sayings " discovered in 1991- are similar In style. and none of them. in the opinion of the 'discoverers, can be placed at a later date than the year 300 A.l). There are forty-two incom- plete linei upon the fragment, and the following 'sayings aro decipherable:—</p>
        <p>These are the words which Jesus snake . . . and Thomas, He said to them, Every one that hearkens to these words shall never taste of death,</p>
        <p>Jesus saith, let not him who seeks cease until he finds, and when he finds he shall be astonished; astonished he shall reach the king- dom, and having reached the kingdom he shall rest.</p>
        <p>Jesus saith. The kingdom of Heaven is within you; and whoever shall know himself shall find it . . . know yourselves and ye shall be aware that ye are the sons of the Father. Jesus saith, Many that are first shall be last and the last first.</p>
        <p>6" FOR OVERSEAS."—Continued.</p>
        <p>that there is always something they can learn in the Ordnance. . . .</p>
        <p>"Good luck!" he cries. "Are you down- hearted 9"</p>
        <p>"No VI thunder back the host of voices</p>
        <p>Dawn is breaking. A word to the officers; salutes; and the colonel is gone. The cap- tain assumes command.</p>
        <p>" Form fours! - Right turn! . Quick— march !" The band is playing tunefully. " Right wheel!" The steady stream looks neither to the right nor to the left.</p>
        <p>" Hip, hip, hurrah !" deafeningly cry the hundreds who remain. The company is too full to reply. Far on the road the ring of its smart step presently dies away. That night it crosses the moonlit • waters; within two dozen hours it can hear the guns firing for God.</p>
        <p>" Wonder how inany'll come back ?" re- marked one clerk to another. _ " Dunno. There's a topping show on- at the Rip.' this week; have you been"</p>
        <p>Wis•Wa</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0244" n="art0244">
        <head>lOUNFIELD.</head>
        <p>lOUNFIELD.</p>
        <p>Jesus saith, Everything that is not before thy face and that which is hidden from thee shall be revealed to thee. For there is nothing hidden which shall net be made manifest nor buried which shall not be raised.</p>
        <p>There was al6o found a fragment of a Gospel which reads:</p>
        <p>Take no thoughts either for food or for your raiment.</p>
        <p>His disciples say unto Him, When wilt Thou be manifest to us, and when shall we see Thee? He saith, When ye shall be stripped and not be ashamed.</p>
        <p>IC is a delicate task to comment upon words that come to us as these have come. Rescued from dust heaps they have no external authority to support them. Their only appeal is in their evident Christlikeness. From some of them there is no escape. 'they speak to us at once. Such a saying is " Raise the stone and thou shalt find Me, cleave the wood and I am there," which tells us that Christ comes near to us in the hour of toil. Another tiospel teaching has "Where two or three are gathered in My name there am I in the midst "; this one gives assurance that where a lonely man or woman faces a task they at once lind the company of the universal Lord.</p>
        <p>Dr. Warschauer gives an interpretation which is worthy of quotation, though we prefer the one just offered. His paraphrase is "Let the prophet pour tempestuous scorn upon the heathen wor- shipping dumb stone or hewn trunk of a tree: Nevertheless raise that Stone and there we shall find Him who made it. cleave the wood and there is lie who caused it to grow; behind the poorest symbol stands the augue, zteality which it symbolises."</p>
        <p>The saying, "Know yourselves and ye shall be flivaro that ye are the sons or the Father," will stir much reflection.</p>
        <p>QUOTED BY THE FATHERS OF THE CHURCH.</p>
        <p>We take next some reputed teachings of Jesus preserved in early Christian writings. several of these are impressive, and have com- mended themselves to many capable scholars, a 1 genuine teachings. In "The Dialogue with Trypho," written about A.D. 10n by *Tustin Martyr, we read that Jesus said.</p>
        <p>In whatsoever employments I may surprise you, in these also .will I judge you. . • Since Justin lived in Ephesus and followed close upon the Apostle John in that city, it is possible that he received the saying almost at first hand. At first reading this striking pas- sage seems to echo "In an hour that ye think not"; but upon reverent consideration it reveals a much deeper meaning. It tells us that the real self is most truly seen when we are taken unawares, and urges that we be always ready.</p>
        <p>Origen, another of the early Christian apolo- gists, quotes the striking saying, • He that is near Me is near the fire, and he that is far from Me is far from the kingdom,</p>
        <p>a passage which does not reveal its meaning in a moment, but which fascinates and compels thought. Origen also records.</p>
        <p>Pray for the great things, and the small shall be added unto you; pray for the heavenly and the earthly shall be added unto you. This corresponds so closely to the Sermon on the Mount that few will find! it :difficult to accept it as a distinct and true echo of the voice of Jesus.</p>
        <p>We are indebted to Clement of Alexandria for another arresting quotation. Ile wrote, "It was not of grudgingness that our Lord gave the charge in a certain Gospel,</p>
        <p>My mystery is for Me and the sons of My house. •</p>
        <p>The passage, "Unto you it is given to know the mysteries of the kingdom." at.. once comes .to mind. It is especially interesting when we remember that the ,Mysteries of the Greeks were secret societies, to which men were initiated. The disciples,- he seems to say, are the .true initiates. This is the thought stated so well by Paul in I. Cor. ii., a chapter which might almost be called a commentary upon the text. " My mystery is for Me and the sons of My house." •</p>
        <p>In the Clementine Homilies, which date from about the year A.D. 200, the saying of Jesus is, quoted, Show yourselves approved bankers. As the word is that which occurs in Matt. xxv. 27, it would. perhaps. be better to translate " money changers "or "money handlers." We do not know enough about banking in 'Palestine to know the exact shade of meaning wllich at- taches to the words, but the idea behind it may perhaps be paraphrased. " You are entrusted with precious things, show •yourselves worthy to•handle them." Last under this bead we quote from the " Traditions of Matthias." The book itself is lost, and is only known to us by numerals quotations made from it. It appears to have been an early exposition of the sayings and doings of Jesus. The saying is, • Wonder at the things before you.</p>
        <p>If wonder encloses the idea of reverence, few of us will heiitate to treat the words with respect. for we shall feel that it is in entire harmony with the spirit Of the Teacher who stood in reverence before the lily, the sparrow and the grass of the field.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0245" n="art0245">
        <head>NEW INCIDENT.</head>
        <p>A NEW INCIDENT.</p>
        <p>We put last a supposed teaching of Jesus which stands latest in point of date. In the Codex Beza, a manuscript tlospel of the sixth century, which now rests at Cambridge, we have a striking addition. In the opening verses of the sixth chapter of Luke's Gospel .there are records of two -encounters which Jesus had with the Pharisees on the question of Sabbath observance. Between these two, following verse 5, thi..; manuscript adds:— On the same day, He beheld one at work on the Sabbath, and said to him: Man, if thou knowest what-thou art doing blessed art thou but if thou knowest not, thou are accursed anll a transgressor of the Law,</p>
        <p>The manuscript is of authority, and the words cannot be lightly set. aside. If they record a true utterance of Jesus, as many capable judges agree, how shall be interpret them? A very in- teresting exposition might be given. Here is a man found working in the field upon the Sabbath. Possibly Jesus is blamed for this breach of the law.. The Pharisees may have argued that the man was doing so because of the dangerous teaching of Jesus that • "the Sabbath was made for man, not man for. the Sabbath." It will be noticed that the words of Jesus carry neither blame nor approval. He seems to say, "What is the motive behind the labour?" Is it gain? Does he break the Sab- bath to earn money? If so, he stands con- demned. On the other hand, has he gone forth in an enlightened mood determined to act out the teaching that "the Sabbath was Made for man," and as a protest against the narrow views and habits of the Pharisees. If se, he was not to be blamed.</p>
        <p>The passage is a difficult one, and this inter- pretation may not be correct, but we shall be safe in saying that it is meant to teach that motive is the important ,thing and that by motive we shall be judged. Here again words which arrest the intellect, and for many will compel the heart, we have</p>
      </div>
