      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0247" n="art0247">
        <head>Old Captain Of St. Malo.</head>
        <p>The Old Captain Of St. Malo.</p>
        <p>WITHIN THE SHADOW OF THE CASINO.</p>
        <p>BY HAROLD MAYBURY,</p>
        <p>It is midnight. - Overhead a pale wan moon struggles fitfully through the scudding clouds, and away in the distance, a mere speck, the revolving light of a lighthouse, shining out as a guide to mariners off this rocky coast. Seafaring men have a dread of this part of Brittany, and rightly so, for the submerged rocks and cur- rents off St. Male are veritable death-traps for the unwary, and many 'brave hearts lie beneath the waves, as Pierre can testify. That is the name of the old captain of St. Malo. His is a familiar figure in this pic- turesque old-world town facing the English Channel. Small of stature, with eyes blue as any Southern sky, clean shaven, and skin fair as a Avoman's, wrinkled of feature and gro- tesquely bandy-legged, one would not. think that most of his life had been spent on the wild and tempestuous seas, but, then, maybe, you have never met. Grand-pere Pierre. That is the name the peasant folk and children in these parts have given him. He lives in an old tumble-down cottage, a mile or so to the south of the sleepy town, with his niece, a fair-haired girl of dighteen, who keeps house for him. Borne to the ears is the sound of surf breaking on the seashore and the lamentable and plaintive cry of cur- lews disturbed in their silent watches on rocky headlands and crags far out in the bay. Ex- cept for these noises all is silent. Most people are abed. Few of the inhabitants patrol its mean and narrow streets; the win- dows of the tall granite-built houses are all shuttered; the street lamps are dim, without the slightest gleam of cheerfulness; and the salons and cafes have long since been emptied of their habitues; yet within the little cot- tage where Pierre lives an oil lamp casts its fitful rays in dark corners and recesses. • The Anger of the Sea.</p>
        <p>Seated at a table is Pierre reading. Out- side, the wind has increased in fury, and away in the distance the dull boom of tormented wal es hurling themselves on rocky headlands and beach proclaim the approach of storm. Also .can be heard the shrill cry of seagulls. " God help those out there to-night," he mut- murs.to himself, looking through the windows into the black pight, and ero the words have left his lips, into the sky can be seen the red glare of a rocket from a ship in distress. " Mon Dieu, the sea is calling again," he says, and with the utterance this old man of the sea dons his. oilskins and sou'-wester. HO does not hurry; instead, with slim precision, he mounts the ricketty stairs to a room where his niece is sleeping. "The little one slumbers," he says; " it is well, for there is work afoot for old Pierre." The window panes rattle, so he places wedges of paper in the woodwork, unlifts the latch of his humble abode and walks out into. the night.</p>
        <p>Already in the distance many voices can be heard, with every now and then the sound of a whistle shrilly piercing the midnight air. Near the ramparts of the town a goodly num- ber of peasant women with shawls around their heads are congregated. Silhouetted against the .sky the monster towers and tur- rets of the castle erected by Anne of Brit- tany four centuries ago, as also is the pointed spire of the Catholic Church not far front the ramparts and fortifications, now well-nigh obsolete. The scene is ghostlike,' the only patches of colour being the white horses rac- ing madly onwards to dash themselves against the worn stone steps and breastwork of the walls leading to the beach, and the monotonous flashing beams from the light- house. Up above, on the front or promenade, the motley crowd peer anxiously into the inky darkness, awaiting a second signal which never came: Pierre is.amongst them, but he does not speak, for he knows that no boat sent out could live in such a sea. In his eyes there is a dull look of hopelessness and despair, for he realises that across the broad expanse of waters men such as he had once been have • paid the toll of the sea. There- upon Pierre wishes the women and few mon facing the sea good-night, and-wends his was homeward in silent thought. The journey has been unavailing. Sharp Contrasts. • -</p>
        <p>it is summer time. On the spacious sands French boys and girls make merry, whilst their parents parade the "front " and visit the sights of St. Maio, with its old associa- tions and historic buildings. On the crest of a bill is the Casino, a palatial construction, with dome shining like beaten silver in the brilliant sunshine. Far out to sea can be seen fishing smacks 'with copper-coloured sails, rid- ing on dancing waves. . In the streets the sellers of shrimps and shell fish greet you in strange tongue, and above all an odour of tar and kerosene from the docks blending with a stencil from the waters, which certainly has not rosemary in its composition. There is gaiety and laugh- ter is to be heard on all sides. And it is war- time. In the Casino a few wounded French soldiers aro playing roulette, others are escorting " petite " and chic mademoiselles through the spacious grounds, whilst many are seated in little alcoves facing the sea. Far below in the town a French regiment in blue grey uniforms march to the strains of the " Marseillaise " to the station, where a train awaits to take them to their. destination. Life in all its varying and changing phases meets the view. A pastel sky reflects itself on a calm sea. Mounting a winding path in the direction of the Casino is a fair-haired daugh- ter of Brittany carrying a bunch of beautiful Madonna lilies. She is Grand-pere Pierre's niece. Practically within the shadow of the Casino is a solitary grave marking the last resting-place of this old man of the sea. It was his last wish that he should be buried with his proud face towards the mighty ex- panse of water, and his wish has been ful- filled.</p>
        <p>To the pleasure-seekers who pass this sacred spot the carefully-tended grave is as others, but to the Breton fisher folk and peasants the spot is sacred and revered. At the head is a tombstone, whereon is engraved these words: To the old Captain of St. Male, An a tribute from the Captain and Sailors • of the Ss. Thorhfield. (Within call of the sea.)</p>
        <p>Yes, Pierre ia, not forgotten, for even the seagulls in stormy weather hover o'er his grave, seemingly taking him messages by their shrill and melancholy Cries. Some say that on the approach of dread storm his form can be seen wending its way front the old cottage to the 'sea front clad in. oilskins and sou,'-wester, but, of course, that may be only a superstitious Breton tale or a phantasy Of the night. At least each .Sabbath morn the solitary figure of a young girl can to this day be seen carrying flowers to a spot of tender memories.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0248" n="art0248">
        <head>NEXT WEEK: " The Old Inn-keeper of Hiedelburg."</head>
        <p>NEXT WEEK: " The Old Inn-keeper of Hiedelburg."</p>
        <p>ASIA'S TIMBER MINE.</p>
        <p>An immense wood mine has recently been dis- covered in Tonquin, Annam in Asia. The wood was originally a pine forest, which the earth swallowed up in some cataclysm. Some of the trees are a yard in diameter. They lie in a slanting direction and in sandy soil, which covers them to a depth of about eight yards. As the tip branches are well preserved, it is thought the geological convulsion which buried them cannot be of great antiquity. The wood furnished by these timber mines is imperish- able.</p>
        <p>manwsw!spiowjPll.lwll"mr'"3ll.ll`</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0249" n="art0249">
        <p>.</p>
        <p>1•11111•1</p>
        <p>NEW BOOKS.</p>
        <p>methods of criticism which he uses in this book, chiefly to his treatment of intellectual values. We agree that he will encounter op- position, but perhaps it will not be quite such as he imagines. The restricted sense in which he uses the term "Naturalism" as the alter- native to Theism will, • we doubt not, meet with determined opposition from those who hold the naturalistic position, but who do not accept the mechanistic theory. And .his insist- ence on natural selection as the sole factor in determining adjustments, on the naturalistic hypothesis, will, we imagine, rouse the antagonism of those who have been contending, for some considerable time, that this is a one- sided and inaccurate interpretation of the facts. For there is, we conceive, tv "naturalism" which lakes due account of the purpose and design that Mr. Balfour is con- cerned to establish, but which is certainly not "theistic" in the ordinary religious sense. But whether it meet with opposition or not, the book was well worth the writing, for it emphasises claims which must be met by any world-theory -which is to replace the imperfect theories of the past.. Mr. Balfour is not. a philosopher in the sense that he has a cut- and-dried system to offer for acceptance; rather is be a critic who shows up the focuses and shortcomings of 01l philosophies. Be is not antipathetic to philosophical speculation, but he is the candid friend who, though he some- times, praises, mercilessly exposes the faults and failings he desires to see amended. . The present work has all the subtle' charm which always distinguishes Mr. Balfour's utterances, and the book is a delight to read. We warmly recommend it it) all who would seek to know the real problems which philosophy and religion have to solve.</p>
        <p>A. W. PERRIS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0250" n="art0250">
        <head>MR. BALFOUR'S GIFFORD LECTURES.</head>
        <p>MR. BALFOUR'S GIFFORD LECTURES.</p>
        <p>Theism and Humanism. By. the Rt. Hon. A. J.' Balfour.</p>
        <p>London: Hodder &amp; Stoughton. Price 10/6,</p>
        <p>This volume is the outcome of the series of lectures delivered by Mr. Balfour under the Gifford Trust at the University of Glasgow in January, and February, 1914. It -contains the substance of the lectures, but is itself, as Mr. Balfour explains in his Preface, practically rewritten. We may feel grateful for Mr. Bal- four's election to the office of Gifford Lecturer because of the opportunity it presented for a restatement of his views on the Naturalistic controversy. His "point of view "—as he calls it in the present work--is well known, having been partially elaborated in his previous works.</p>
        <p>"Theism and Humanism" is an attempt, by a searching criticism exposing the insuffi- ciency of the naturalistic theory of life to account for the origin of testheties, ethics and the rational faculty, and the values We attach thereto, to show the need for a theistic inter- pretation, using the word "theistic" in its ordinary religious sense. Mr. Balfour ap- proaches. his subject not in the traditional philosophic manner, but as he himself says "from the plain man's point of view "; that is, he accepts the plain man's view of life and the world, the common-sense view, which is held, in practical affairs, by,expert and plain man alike; and front this basis he proceeds to a consideration of the rival claims of Natural- ism and Theism to explain the origin and strength of the inevitable beliefs which the common-sense view of life force's on men.</p>
        <p>Whether the " plain limn," if be reads this book—and we should like to urge any who claim to be "plain men" to do so—will be quite able to follow Mr. Balfour, it is not easy to decide; for, though the book is commend- ably free from the more technical terms and discussions, the arguments adduced in support of his views are by no means simple, and demand a capacity for sustained reasoning usually associated only with the expert, and to which the " plain man" would be the last to lay claim. Emotions and Beliefs.</p>
        <p>The problem which Mr. Balfour has set himself in this book is this: Man, in the course of his development, has come to hold certain beliefs—beliefs which, "whether true or false, proved or unproved," he feels to be "inevitable." These nmy be divided into three classes, beliefs in regard to Beauty, Goodness and Truth. With these beliefs cer- tain definite emotions have come to be asso- ciated, with the result that man attaches higher values to these beliefs and emotions than to any other of his possessions. The emotions, however, depend .for their value on the beliefs with which they are associated, and would vanish if the latter weie discredited. "without the beliefs, the emotions would dwindle; without • the emotions, the beliefs would, lose their worth." What, then, is the relation which origin bears to value in regard to. these beliefs? If the beliefs are shown to .have had a "naturalistic" origin, will the emotions preserve the values which man has in the past attached to them in the belief that they were due to a creative purpose? Mr. Balfour proceeds to a searching analysis of the naturalistic theory of the origin of these " inevitable " beliefs, and the argument becomes:, to use Mr. Balfour's phrase, "an .argument not FROM design, but TO design," theism being, in Mr. Balfour's opinion, the only theory which gives a rational and coherent account of their origin, in view of the position they /hold in the estimation of man. On the mechanistic hypothesis the " living" has evolved from the "not living," and •in the course of organic evolution the beliefs in question have emerged as a result of the operation of the single factor of natural selection. But these beliefs, and the resultant emotions, have not the slightest survival value, for they aid neither the race nor the individual in the struggle for existence. It is in the sphere of intellectual values, however, that, according to Mr. Balfour, the mechan- istic theory fails most completely; for the appeal here is to reason, and yet when the beliefs on which these values are based are traced back to their source, it is found in the non-rational and the material; and such an origin is not congruous with man's developed powers of thought.</p>
        <p>The Failure to Explain. Life presents unmistakable evidences of creative purpose and design in the very beliefs themselves, and the only agent which the mechanistic theory can suggest as their cause, namely, selection, fails utterly to explain them:—" The best imitation of creative pur- pose, therefore, which Naturalism can provide breaks down where,it is most required, namely, at the highest levels of value. I have just shown this in connection with our powers of thought, and the beliefs to which they lead. But the failure is not confined to them. It is as wide as Humanism itself. Wherever we find great intrinsic worth, there we are in a region where the direct effect of selection is negligible. The noblest things in speculation, in art, in morals, possess small survival value; and though the geniuses to whom we owe them have added greatly to the glory of their race, they have added but little to its animal suc- cesses. In the language of these lectures, they. are 'accidental '—due -neither to purpose, nor to any arrangement of causes by which pur- pose is suceessfully copied." Consideration of testhetic, ethic and intel- lectual values, therefore, leads Mr. Balfour to find their ground and origin in a theistic view of the universe; theistic; that is, in the sense in which the " plain man" understands that term. "We must hold that reason and the works Of reason have their source in God, that from Him they draw their inspiration; and that if they repudiate their origin, by this very act they proclaim their own insufficiency." Mr. Balfour modestly disclaims all intention of offering a new system of philosophy to the world. He merely puts forward a " point of view." He does not suggest that it will solve all the outstanding scientific and philosophic problems, but he does claim that it points to the direction in which such solution—if it is to be found—must be sought..</p>
        <p>Expected Opposition, Mr. Balfour anticipates opposition to the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0251" n="art0251">
        <head>LAND OF TRAGEDY.</head>
        <p>THE LAND OF TRAGEDY.</p>
        <p>Poland. By W. Alison Phillips, M.A. With Maps. (Home University Library. 256 pp. is. net.) London: Williams and Norgate.</p>
        <p>The terrible 'vicissitudes endured by Poland and the suffering part that .this unhappy country is now playing in the contemporary Armageddon of Europe renders the publication of this volume singularly appropriate. The appeal of such a subject to the widest range of intelligent readers is not greater than the poignant interest of the vivid • narrative so ably told in the pages of the latest addition to that wonderful series which has become part of the educational development of the English- speaking races. Moreover, the Lecky Professor of Modern History in Dublin University writes not only with thorough grasp of the subject, but with a practised pen which ably sets forth the salient facts and deductions that people want to know.</p>
        <p>The tide of the present war has swept back- wards and forwards over Poland. Why? The Polish Question is something more than the phrase of the Grand Duke Nicholat: "Will Poland at last be free in faith, in speech, and in self-government under the sceptre of the Russian Emperor?"—the alternative to that being its victimisation under the ruthlela 'pro- paganda of German. Kultur. For in this volume we see how Poland has reached- the apotheosis of such a struggle and such viciesi- tudes as have no parallel in the history of any other country. It is not even in. the sad story of the crushing of the Polish insurrection of 1864 that we find the key to the remarkable story of the nation. Deathless Tradition.</p>
        <p>Like Ireland, Poland his preserved a mass of tradition, of practical and patriotic senti- ment. The romance of the small countries furnishes a more exciting tale than that of the huge armaments protecting encroaching commercialism, which threatened to be the key-note of twentieth-century Europe. It may he that Poland and Belgium will be the joint stumbling-block over which will crash in ruins the clumsy gigantic fraud that was known aa German civilisation.</p>
        <p>The Congress Kingdom came from the Treaty of Tilsit, and the l'an-Slav idea, against which Germanism is now in arms, was itself the creation of German scholarship and German philosophy. From it came the fatal insurrec- tion of 1863, and out of the ruins of that Polish revolution rose the work of Bismarck and the Russification in the Empire of the Tsars. How Poland stood as the buffer victim betweeit Austria, Germany, and Russia right through the last hundred years until the capture of Warsaw a few months ago is vividly set forth in this volume, packed with knowledge lucidly imparted. For the Allies the liberation of Poland is as imperative as that of Belgium. If these two were to be absorbed into the German system there would be soon after no l'ower in Europe to withstand the Kaiser's will. It is the self-preservation of England, France and Russia which is involved in the liberties of Poland, and that is why this little book is so invaluable and topical. It shows what the doom would be and how that doom would fall on ourselves. Happily there is no fear so long as England to herself is true, and pages such as theAe are, bugle-calls to addi- tional effort. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0252" n="art0252">
        <head>GIIEEN'S HISTORY.</head>
        <p>GIIEEN'S HISTORY.</p>
        <p>Macmillan's New And Final Edition.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Macmillan have had in preparation for a considerable time, and are publishing, their new and final edition of the "Short History of the English People," by J. R. Green --a book which has been in their charge for forty years. The original edition, which .ap- peured in 1871, in which there had been in- accuracies of detail, was revised by Mrs. Green according to the author's special directions.- In this work Mrs. Green had the advantage of Mr. Green's own corrections, and also in difficult questions the advice of the leading historians; in their several departments, such as, for ex- ample, l'rofessor Gardiner. Mr. Lecky. Lord Bryce, Bishop Stubbs, Bishop Creighton, and others. For purposes of study or of reference the revised volume is essential, and the edition published by Messrs. Macmillan remains the only authorised or authoritative form of the " Short History." This final edition includes an Epilogue which continues the history to the present day.. Seventy page's of this epilogue are devoted to the social and industrial history of Englp.nd: and some eighty pages. to foreign and impirial policy, and to the development of the great dominions of the. Empire. In this work Mrs. Green has had the advice and assistance of scholars who have valued the "Short History of the English People."</p>
        <p>-._</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0253" n="art0253">
        <head>ANGLING.</head>
        <p>ANGLING.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON WORKING MEN'S</p>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>N.A. CENTRE,</p>
        <p>Very little angling has been (lane by our mem- bers since Christmas. Those who visit the Gowy during the winter months have been. very busy getting their baits from the different brooks around Warrington, and to judge by the num- ber on the job last week-end around Appleton way, the Gowy roach are in for a lively time. One or two paid a visit to the Gowy last week- end, but the result was nil. •The stream, looked at from the road bridge, was all that was de- sired, but fishing the -different holes was quite .'another thing, as the stream had not fined down after the fresh, with the result that the holes were muddy and all in a boil, which did not give the angler any chance only in the back eddies.</p>
      </div>
