      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0258" n="art0258">
        <head>RUGBY.</head>
        <p>RUGBY.</p>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>Yorkshire Clubs Head The League.</p>
        <p>A couple of unexpected results arose out of last Saturday's fixtures—one being the defeat of Swinton by Broughton, and the other a first victory of the season by Brighouse Rangers. Leeds, Dewsbury, and Hull were successful in their games, and Swinton has dropped to the fourth position of the League. Though Rochdale lost to Halifax at Thrum Hall, Stanley Young, late of Warrington, gave another fine exhibition of play. The "Athletic News" says :—" Young and Prudence in the back • division may.be fairly described as the cleverest players on the field. Curiously enough, they did their best work against the wind. Stanley Young cleverly burst through on several occasions by selling his immediate opponents the dummy, and his combination with Prudence deserved at least one score." The results of last Saturday's .thatches were : Leeds, 19 points; Bradford, 12 points. Brighouse, 22; York, 7. pewsbury, 38; Bramley, 3. Featherstone, 5; Batley, 3. , Hull, 13; Hull Kingston R., 2. Broughton R., 3; Swinton, 0. • St. Helens Recs., 18; Salford, 6 Wigan, 10; Oldham, 5. Halifax, 15; Rochdale IT., 0.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0259" n="art0259">
        <head>NTERIVIEDIATE LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>NTERIVIEDIATE LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>More Favourable Conditions Last Week.</p>
        <p>Some good games were witnessed in the local Intermediate League on Saturday, the weather conditions not being unfavourable. Bolton were in good form at Wilderspool against St. James', and they triumphed by the goad margin ,of fifteen points to nil. Another team who were also " nilled on the same ground was Beamont, who were beaten by St. Thomas' by eight points. Perhaps the best' game of the afternoon was witnessed at the Gorsey-lane ground, the home team being opposed by Heathside. The game was very evenly contested, for, whereas in the first half Heathside had almost all the play, the home team had quite as much in the second moiety. Unfortunately, some of the players were in- clined to introduce rough tactics, but Mr. W. _</p>
        <p>Bibby kept them well in hand. The game re- sulted in a win for the home team•by five points to four.</p>
        <p>RESULTS UP TO AND INCLUDING SATURDAY, JANUARY 15th. Points P. W. L. D. F. A. P. Parochial Heathside Rylands's St. Thomas' Beamont Bglton Sacred Heart s. 12 4 8 0 52 81 8 St Mary's St. James'</p>
        <p>ST. JAMES' LOSE AGAIN.</p>
        <p>For the tenth time this season the St. James' earn had to acknowledge defeat: This time at</p>
        <p>Le hands of Bolton.</p>
        <p>Bolton started with only ten men, but their full complement was made up as the game eeeded. In the openipg stages St. James' made most of their numerital strength, and for about five minutes the Bolton lads had a rough time of it. Missing passes, however, ' was the attackers' weakness, and nothing in the way of Points resulted. Before the game was very old Bolton showed a marked superiority and took</p>
        <p>he game in hand. They began to press hard and, following a bit of fine play, Wilcock opened the score for them by dropping a beautiful goal. A few minutes later Cowper ran over the line, adding another three points. Jones took the goal-kick and made no mistake. At half-time</p>
        <p>the score was 7—nil in favour of Bolton. In the second half Bolton kept up a steady pressure. 'Following a few scrimmages on the St. James' line, Cliffe scored under the posts. Again Jones was successful with the goal-kick. Later, Wilcock. who throughout the game played well, scored a brilliant, but unconverted try. The game resulted.:—Bolton, 15 points; St. James', nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0260" n="art0260">
        <head>ST. THOMAS' IN A WINNING MOOD.</head>
        <p>ST. THOMAS' IN A WINNING MOOD.</p>
        <p>St: Thomas' team were in a winning mood again and added a couple of points, which will be of value in the League table. Their opponents were Beamont. St. Thomas' opened wall and looked like scoring in the early part of the game, but the play was soon transferred to their own quarters. They were penalised on a couple of occasions and Beamont made attempts at goal, but success was not for them. Throughout the first half Beamont were somewhat superior, but. were unable td.score.</p>
        <p>After the interval, however. St. Thomas' kept the play in their opponents' half, and before many minutes Gill scored a try for them. The extra points were added by H. Hughes_ Later St Gleave put another three points on for St. Thomas', and the game ended eight points in favour of the Stockton Heath lads.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0261" n="art0261">
        <head>RYLANDS'S ONE POINT VICTORY.</head>
        <p>RYLANDS'S ONE POINT VICTORY.</p>
        <p>Rylands's and Heathside met on the ground of the former team, before a thin line of spec- tators. The playing pitch was in a very soft condition, and shortly after the commencement of tha game the ground began to cut up very badly, much to the discomfiture of the players. Heatuside won the toss and elected to play with the wind at their backs. Ryli3nds's started with eleven players, and the ball being returned, play was, for a time, waged in midfield. After about five minutes' even play Heathside broke away, and, when in the vicinity of their opponents' line, were a war.led a free-kick. With a fine hefty effort, Harter gave his side a 'two points lead. Hardly had the game been recommenced when Heathside made another fine rush down the field, and Rylands's being penalised, Harter again (lid the needful. The two players who were niissing from Rylands's side now put in an appearance, and the home team were im- mediately on the attack. After a desperate struggle on the Heathside line, the ball was made dead. From the restart Heathside at- tacked, and, one of their players being obstructed. were awarded another free-kick, but this time Harter failed.</p>
        <p>After play had been. waged in midfield for a short period, the :Heathside left-wing got off- side, and. by means of the free-kick awarded to them. Rylands's worked their way towards their Opponents' line. Heathside, however, de- fended- well, and, after a while, broke away. One of their players was again obstructed, but Harter missed the goal-kick. Shortly before half-time, too-much feeling crept into the game, and the referee had occasion to caution several players. With a few minutes of the first half left for play, the home team •broke away, and, after several scrimmages had been formed, the ball came out to Hill, who, with a fine dash, ran over and scored. Dumbell kicked a good goal, and gave his side a one-point lead. This was the position of affairs .at the interval. In the second half Heathside were set to face the wind, and it was fully realised that they would have to play hard to win, for the power of the home Men was gradually improving, Fr:in the kick-off Rylands's attacked, and thi, visiting full-back was forced to make "dead." Heatliside now commenced a fierce onslaught, and, for several minutes were hammering at the Rylauds's line.- From a scrimmage, however, the home team forwards got command of the Mill, and. with a good dribble, removed all danger. The game was being contested in mid- field when the whistle blew, leaving Rylands's winners of good .game by five points to four.</p>
        <p>LAST .SATURDAY'S RESULTS.</p>
        <p>Rylands's, 5 points; Ileathside, 4 points. Bolton, 15; St. James' 0. St Thomas', 8; Beamont, 0.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0262" n="art0262">
        <head>MATCHES FOR TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>MATCHES FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>At Wilderspool. St. James' v. Parochial. Referee, Kick-off 2.15. Ifeathside . Beamont. Referee, R. Williams. Kick-off 3.15.</p>
        <p>Barber.</p>
        <p>On Victoria Park. Sacred•lTeart v. Bolton. Referee, J. Flynn. Kick-off 2.30.</p>
        <p>At Gorsey-lane. Bylands's v. St. Mary',. Referee, G. Cooper. Kick-off 2.45.</p>
        <p>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>RYLANDS'S REC. v. ST. MARY'S.</p>
        <p>Rylands's: T. Archer; Thomas, Littlewood, Britton and Hill; Savoury and Sloane; Dumb- bell, Barker, Sutton, Mulvey, Latimer and Butler.</p>
        <p>Reserves: Afolyneux, Slane and Bibby.. BEAM ONT JUNIORS v. ST. JAMES' JUNIORS At Victoria Park.</p>
        <p>Beamont: J. Anderton; W. Brookes, W. Downes, A. Mason and J. Higgins; D. Sinclair and J. Coleman; W. Smith (captain), A. Hackett, G. Armitage, G. Taker, A. Acourt and J. Darbyshire. Reserves.—H. Brown and H. Hardman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0263" n="art0263">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>NEW ASSOCIATION CLUE.</p>
        <p>League Programme Opens With</p>
        <p>Good Gamie.</p>
        <p>HONOURS EQUAL,</p>
        <p>• The first attempt for many years to provide Association football above the junior class in Warrington has beep made and now Warring- ton, judging by a displai on Saturday, may boast of a team worthy of competing with tho..e in the Lancashire Combination League. D 1-- ing the pregent season the Sankey Club ha re been engaged in playing friendly matches ith teams j.n the above League, and the results Isa,re been satisfactory. With the withdrawal t Denton from the League the officials and play MS of Sankey decided to apply for the vacancy a.,:t to fulfil the fixtures of the defunct club, and to rename the club, Warrington.</p>
        <p>Their debut in League football was made a fortnight ago, when they played Tranmere at Tranmere, when they lost by four goals to nil. Last Saturday was their first home match, a tot they had an opportunity of turning the tablas, but only succeeded in drawing Their ventara seems to havb caught on, for a crowl ef :du at a thousand assembled at Hood-Lane and invi pleasure of witnessing' a keen and interesting struggle. Warrington certainly deserved more than a draw, for, generally speaking, with the exception of the first few minutes, they did most of the attacking. Faulty marksmanship, how- ever, denied them a victory.</p>
        <p>The club have been promised the assistance of several well-known players, including Berry, of Evertori; Bullen, of Bury; and .Heywood, of Manchester United, and in their ranks are also a number who have figured in teams of the class they are now opposing. Bnllen was the only "star" artist .on view last Saturday. Berry, the goalkeeper, was indisposed. and Heywood answered the call of Manchester "United. The club have got together a very serviceable side, and they should give a good account of them- selves in the League. The appearance of Bullen at left-half materially strengthened the defence and his forceful methods in leading an attack gave the home forwards many opportunities, which were not always turned to the best advantage. It was noticeable that both sides played better football when facing the strong breeze.</p>
        <p>The first goal, to Tranmere, gave the War- rington men a startler, but it was quite their own fault, for very loose defensive work pre- sented Campbell with an opportunity which he was not slow to seize, Kite having no chance with the shot, which entered the top corner of the net. This reverse acted as a tonic to the Warrington players, and several spirited raids were made on the Tranmere goal, but the shoot- ing of the forwards left something to be desired. tr. Whitfield seemed to have a splendid chance of equalising, but his shot was wide of the mark. The equali.er came quite unexpectedly for the " Rovers' " goalkeeper misfielded a simple shot, with the result that Aspinall, who was unmarked, shot into the untenanted It was quite a gift goal, but, considering the pressure the home team had applied, it was only their deserts. This proved to, be all the scoring up to the interval.</p>
        <p>With the wind in their favonr in the seceo.l half, the visitors were exnected to make .11-A4-er; warm for Warrington. For a time Wa-r;r:!t,n were kept busily on the defensive. L.lt. Tran- mere failed to break through The cterlig play of the youthful full-back, 1, w;.s the chief feature of the afternbon. His cool- ness under pressure, combined, with accurate judement called for much admiration., It was unfortunate for Warrington that Tranmeres second goal should have been scored by one of their own nlayers. Ditchfleld, instead of allow- ing the ball to roll harmlessly outside the posts. unfortunately, in attempting to clear, deflected the ball through the goal, with Kite helpless to save. Prior to this, Kite had effected several smart clearances.</p>
        <p>Undismayed, Warrington made valiant efforts to draw level, and the "Hovers' goal had Several narrow escapes. Their efforts were re- warded when Forster scored cleverly, after a prolonged attack. Right up to the close the game was fought out at a rare pace, but neither side could add to their score.</p>
        <p>. . Of the Warrington players Bullen, Millward, and Aspinall were the pick, whilst Ditchfield, but for his one mistake, which cost his side a goal, played a very useful game. Kite in goal saved several good shots, especially in the second half. Tranmere, who had the assistance of N.uttall and Roberts (both Evertow players) were best represented by Cookson at back, Holden at half, and Roberts left-outside.</p>
        <p>The Rovers included several players whn, in ordinary 'times, are members of different English League. Clubs, notably. • Cookson. Manchester united: Nuttall and Roberts, Everton; and either Birk s or Holden. Clapton Orient; while Millen (a member of the :Bury League team) made his first appearance with the Hood-laners. All the other members are Warringtonians. and , Kite (who also made his first appearance). G. Whitfield, Leah and Aspinall, have had previous experience in Combination football.</p>
        <p>The keennes that was shown by the spectators in following the play, goes to prove that if the men will give of ,their best. in co-operation with the committee, there.wili be plenty of support forthcoming to maintain a club of Combination standard in Warrington.</p>
        <p>To-day IVorrington journer to South Lir- popl in sParch of points, and the return match is at Hood-lane next Saturday.</p>
        <p>The Warrinzton Central were again dis- apnointed in their original fixtures last week- end. A hurried arrangement was made with the Hollins Green Club to play in the Park. Owing to the late arrival of the visitors and the official closing time of the Ptsit, they were not able to play the minimum time of sixty</p>
        <p>minutes according to League rules, and the game was played as a friendly fixture, ending in a victory for the Central.</p>
        <p>By previous arrangements, the Central players posed before the camera before playing the match. Had this any effect on their play? In the excitement subsequently they missed scoring from two penalty-kicks. Muskett, after scoring one of his characteristic goals, was well looked after by; Gilbert and Sankey. Cleworth proved himself a capable custodian by the satisfactory manner in which he cleared some of the shots sent in, and at the opposite end Knight kept up his reputation by his good work 'between the sticks.</p>
        <p>The Elmwood Congregationalists, owing to the Derby recruiting scheme and munition work, have of late been unable to raise a team to fulfil their fixtures. On Saturday they should have travelled across the town to play Sankey Athletic, but only four players put in an appearance at the meeting rooms, and they had to cancel the fixture. .</p>
        <p>To-day they have a fixture with Hollins Green at the King and Queen grounds, and a special effort is to be made to get a team together for the occasion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0264" n="art0264">
        <head>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION. Southern Section.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION. Southern Section.</p>
        <p>TABLE TO SATURDAY.</p>
        <p>Goals—</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. For. Agt. Ps. Eccles Borough 2O 13 5 , 2 51 26 28 South Liverpool 2O 12 6 2 50 27 26 Atherton Hyde Altrincham Stalybridge Ce!t lc 2O 7 5 8 40 26 22 Tranmere foyers l9 6 8 5 39 39 17 Walkden Central 2O 6 13 1 30 67 13 Glossop Warrington</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0265" n="art0265">
        <head>WARRINGTON ASSOCIATION v. TRAI:JMERE ROVERS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON ASSOCIATION v. TRAI:JMERE ROVERS.</p>
        <p>The return fixture between the above clubs was played on Saturday last on the Hood-lane ground, before a good crowd of spectators. Both clubs were strongly represented, and the following players turned out under the control of Referee M. Parr, of Hindley:— Warrington: P. Kite; Millward and Leah; Bullen, G. Whitfield and Ditchfield; J. Banner, Foster, Aspinall, T. Whitfield and J. Banner. Tratimere Rovers: Devaney; Bull and Cook- son; Birks, Holden and Povey; Williamson, Nuttall, Lomas, Campbell and Roberts. The home team won the toss and played with the wind at their backs. Lomas set the ball rolling and very soon Foster sent out to Ban- ner on the wing, but this effort was easily repulsed, and the visitors transferred play to the home end. A free-kick for hands against</p>
        <p>Bullen placed the home goal in danger: and , the ball being well placed, the defence was at fault with poor tackling, and Campbell beat Kite with a rising shot after five .minutes' play.. From the centre-kick a brief visit was paid to the visitors' end, Bull clearing. Roberts raced along the touch-/ine and centring accurately, caused Kite to handle a dangerous ball, and a minute later T. Whitfield tested Devaney with a good shot. During a strong attack by the home forwards the visitors' goal was nearly cap- tured, slnSts going just wide of each upright: From the goal-kick good play by the visitors was spoiled by Williams getting into an offside position. Another corner to the home team was well placed by lllidge, and headed just over the crossbar. The visitors now made a strong at- tack on the home goal, and Millward was seen to great advantage by his good clearances when hard pressed. After a spell of even play the home team took up the aggressive, and from a centre by Illidge, Devaney left his goal and missed the bail. Aspinall securing, succeeded in equalising the score. From the centre-kick the home team had the best of exchanges, and Foster, when in a good position, was too eager to score, and sent the ball high over the bar. This brought relief to the . visitors' defenders; and from the goal-kick play was taken into the home half; and when Williams was nearly‘ past the backs he was brought down just outside the Penalty area. A few minutes later the interval was called with the. scores one goal each. On resuming, the visitors, with the wind in</p>
        <p>their favour,- were soon 'attacking the home goal, and owing to a poor clearance by Bullen, the home goal; had a narrow escape. Shortly after Kite had to-save.a couple of shots in quick Succession. Then by strong play the ball was taken to the visitors' end, but shots by Foster, Banner and Whitfield. were futile. The wind at this point seemed to have abated someirhat, and the game was more even. Williams, on the visitors' right, was prominent with good centres, one of which unfortunately for the home team, was diverted into the net by Ditchfield in at- tempting to clear when in an awkward position, and this gave the visitors the lead.' After this reverse the home team adopted .more open methods. They were much keener in their play and the visitors were tested to their fullest ex- tent. From a centre by Illidge, Foster gained the equaliser after'Devaney had partially saved. The game was now very keen, and Nuttall tested Kite with a hard shot in the top corner of the goal. A corner was conceded, but there was a good clearance. Play was in midfield at</p>
        <p>the call ortime. Result :—Warrington, 2 goals; Tranmere Rovers, 2' goals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0266" n="art0266">
        <head>AVARRINGTON AND DISTRICT JUNIOR LEAGUE. TABLE TO DATE. '</head>
        <p>AVARRINGTON AND DISTRICT JUNIOR LEAGUE. TABLE TO DATE. '</p>
        <p>Goals P. W. L. D. F. A. P. St. Elphin's Fairfield United Res. 7 4 2 1 23 9 9 Stockton Heath Scouts 9 4 4 1 19 17 •9. Howley United Bewsey A.F.C. Orford Juniors t-Penketh Juniors -Mersey Juniors * Penketh Juniors have had two points de- ducted for playing ineligible players</p>
        <p>On Saturday, St. Elphi9;s, in their game with the Penketh Juniors, got their biggest fright of the season. The Juniors opened in very strong fashion, and for a time quite upset the " Saints' " defence and obtained a lead of three goals before the " Saints " made any response. The " Saints " then adopted different methods and seored their first point before the interval. In the second portion the "Saints " played with a determination to save their record, and 'a few minutes before the call of time they succeeded in gaining the lead and snatching a victory by the odd goal in seven.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath Scouts of late appear to be on the down grade and, following on their defeat of the previous week, they were to some degree lucky to share the points in their match with the Mersey Juniors last week. Had they played up to their usual form and gained both points they would have gone up to second place again in the League Table. At the same time, the Merseyites must be congratulated upon their display, and the gaining of a point Should urge them on to make stronger efforts to get away from the foot of the chart.</p>
        <p>The Fairfield United *Reserves were handi- capped in their return fixture with the Bewsey A.F.C. by having to play with ten players dur- ing the whole of the game. They held the lead at the intrval. but in the second portion the visitors showed themselves worthy opponents, and that their improved displays recently was no flash in the pan. At the call of time they shared the points. '•</p>
        <p>Howley United retained their position in the table at the expense of ,the Orford Juniors, wiliona they defeated last week by two clear goals. There was a good crowd. Some keen play was witnessed, as of late the Howleyites have greatly strengthened their team. They are going to make great efforts to be the first team to lower the colours of the leaders</p>
        <p>While writing of the Howley Club. I have been informed by players in several of the clubs that the tactics of some of their players are not worthy of the sport. The committee woulil do well o caution the offenders, and the 'referees also should act up to the powers which the rules give them, and put a stop to dirty play.</p>
        <p>HOV/LEY UNITED v. ORFORD JUNIORS.</p>
        <p>The above teams opposed each other on the Arpley ground, before a good attendance of</p>
        <p>spectators. The following players lined out : Howley : Walters; Ashcroft and 13radburn; Glassy, Miller and Richardson; Hibbert, Hunter, Hough, Davis and Potter. Orford: McDougall; Gibson and Doughty; Cousins, Witton, Austin; Barnett, Gleave, Hirst, Clam and Povey. The Juniors won the toss and played with a strong wind at their backs. Each goal was visited and the respective defenders proved to be on the alert. A spell of midfield play fol- lowed. The Juniors controlled the ball better and set up an attack on the Howley goal, Hirst sending in a shot which Walters managed to stop, but did not properly clear. Clare, rushing up, failed at an open goal. This gave relief to the Howley defence, and from the goal-kick their forwards made strong efforts to get through the opposing defence, but their efforts were re- pulsed. Half-time, no score. The Howleyites, with the wind ip their favour, began with a rush on the Juniors' goal, but Gibson and Doughty defended stoutly. Play continued chiefly in the Juniors' quarters, and was frequently in touch. After fifteen minutes' play Hough scored the first goal for Howley. The game was contested in keener fashion, but the Howleyites continued to have the best of the exchanges and Davis increased the lead for them. From now to the call of time the defence proved superior to the attack and no further goals were scored. Gibson, Doughty, Austin and Clare played well for the losers, and Walters, Bradburn. Hough, Hunter and Davis played well for the winners.—Result :—Howley United, 2 goals; Orford Juniors, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0267" n="art0267">
        <head>MERSEY JUNIORS v. 37OCKTON HEATH</head>
        <p>MERSEY JUNIORS v. 37OCKTON HEATH</p>
        <p>SCOUTS.</p>
        <p>The return fixture between the above clubs was played last week on the Arpley ground. The Scouts won the toss and had the advantage of a strong wind at their backs. From the centre- kick the Juniors were soon put on the defensive. After ten minutes' play Kenwright centred well from the touch-line and Farnell seized the opportunity and opened the score for the Scouts. After this reverse the Juniors made several attempts to break through the Scouts' defence, but with the aid of the wind they proved too good for the attack and had to return to their own half, where Williams made a neat pass to Kenwright, and the latter obtained the second goal. Half-time: Scouts, 2 goals; Mersey Juniors, 0. _</p>
        <p>_ - - The Juniors now with the benefit of the wind, pressed very keenly, Grounds making several good saves. The home team gained several corner-kicks, and from one of these Johnson, of the Scouts, leaded through his own goal. It was only on rare oceasions that the Scouts paid a visit to the Juniors' goal, and a lot of touch- line play was indulged in. Towards the close of play the Juniors made a strong effort to get on level terms, and it was from another corner- kick that they equalised. Time was called with the result :—Mersey Juniors, 2 goals; Stockton Heath Scouts, 2 goals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0268" n="art0268">
        <head>PENKETH JUNIORS v. ST. ELPHIN'S.</head>
        <p>PENKETH JUNIORS v. ST. ELPHIN'S.</p>
        <p>The above teams opposed each other for the first time on Saturday, on the ground of the former. The Juniors won the toss and played down the slope with the wind behind them. From the centre-kick the Juniors played very keenly and quickly bombarded the "Saints' ", goal. After a few minutes' play they opened their score. Encouraged by this early success they were all over the "Saints " defence, and soon increased their lead. After this they con- tinued to have thp best of the game and obtained a penalty-kick, from which they scored again. This state of affairs, was staggering to the "Saints," but they played in a determined style, and after the centre-kick they showed great improvement in their tactics, and paid several visits to the home goal. On one occasion Boyle was well served by his speed, and out- running his opponents, he opened the " Sainis' " I account with a splendid shot. Half-time : Penketh, 3 goals; St. Elphin's, 1 goal. The "Saints'," with both slope and wind be- hind them, give the home defence a taste of their abilities, and rushing towards the home goal, Burns scored their. second point. The " Saints " now saw a chance of saving their un- defeated record, and played more keenly. The home defence was hard pressed. From a well- placed corner-kick taken by Boyle, Rothwell equalised the scores with a shot that gave Hatton no chance to save. Both teams now</p>
        <p>played very strenuously, but the home team could not penetrate the visitors defence again. A few minutes before time the " Saints " ob- tained another corner, from which Tither scored and gave the "Saints " the lead. Result :—St. Elphin's, 4 goals; Penketh Juniors, 3 goals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0269" n="art0269">
        <head>FAIRFIELD UNITED RESERVES v. BEWSEY.</head>
        <p>FAIRFIELD UNITED RESERVES v. BEWSEY.</p>
        <p>The return fixture between the above clubs was played on the ground of the former. Bewsey won the toss and defended the goal nearest the railway, and the home team, with only ten men, set the ball in -motion. After a few minutes' even play the visitors made an attack on the home goal, where Bolton an Cooper defended smartly, and eventually cleared their lines. As the interval was drawing near the home team adopted the _rushing methods, _George passed _ _</p>
        <p>forward to Holden and the latter raced past the backs and scored for the United. Half-time: United Reserves, 1 goal; Bewsey, nil. On resuming the United were not seen to the best advantage, and the visitors were the first to attack. Hassell made the scores equal with a good drive. The visitors were now play- ing the better game and Hassell was again prominent with several good shots, which gave trouble to the United defenders. From a goal- _ . _</p>
        <p>kick the United transferred play to the opposite end, and during an attaCk on their goal the referee awarded the home team a penalty-kick, from which Lygo gained the lead for them. Even</p>
        <p>play followed,- in which the home forwards showed good combination, but they failed at the critical moment. Hassall received the ball in midfield, raced along the touch-line and centred beautifully to Noel, who had not much difficulty in making the scores equal again. After this the United Reserves missed several good openings to take the lead, and. at the call of time the result was :—Fairfield Reserves, goals; Bewsey, 2 goals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0270" n="art0270">
        <head>FRIENDLY MATCHES.</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCHES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON CENTRAL v. HOLLINS GREEN.</p>
        <p>This fixture between the above clubs was ar- ranged at very short notice, and was played laSt Saturday in Victoria Park. The visitors had not a full team, and Fearnley, of Longford United', played as sub. in the back division. The visi- tors won the toss and decided to play up the hill. Emory set the ball rolling and the open- ing exchanges were evenly contested, after which the visitors, aided by the wind, gradually forced the home team on the defensive. Muskett received a centre from the right and made one of his characteristic individual efforts, opening the score with a shot which beat Knight all the way. After this reverse the home team made a strong attack on the visitors' goal, where the ball was handled in front of goal by one of the defenders. The penalty was entrusted to Stevens, Who shot on the wrong side of the up- right. Even play followed to the interval, when the score was :—Hollins Green, 1 goal; Central, nil.</p>
        <p>In the second portion the • Central .at once rushed to the visitors' end, and after several un- successful attempts to lower the visitors' colours, Emery tested the visitors' goalkeeper with a .splendid shot, which was only partially cleared. O'Neil following up, gained possession and sent the leather between the uprights. making the scores equal. Encouraged by this success, the home team played more keenly and kept up the attack, and were awarded a second penalty-kick, upon which Sankey failed to im- prove. This gave relief to the visitors' defence, and the play was. transferred to the home end. A good clearance by Glover enabled Williams to race away along the wing. From Williams Catlow received a centre from a fine angle and headed a good goal. A few minutes later Stevens increased the lead with a splendid shot from 30 yards' range. Following this the visitors made strong efforts to score and Knight saved a. hard shot from Muskett at the expense of a corner. This was not improved upon. the final score being :—Central, 3 goals; ollins Green, 1 goal.</p>
        <p>EMMANUEL INSTITUTE v. CAIRO-STREET</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0271" n="art0271">
        <head>UNITARIANS.</head>
        <p>UNITARIANS.</p>
        <p>These teams met at Victoria Park on Satur- ay. Emmanuel won the toss. Cairo-street set the ball rolling, and the first few minutes saw their defence hotly attacked. Stubbs made a good attempt to open the scoring with a fine shot, but without avail. Plenty of vigour wps put into the game by the Institute forwards, and after a neat piece of combination T.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0272" n="art0272">
        <head>DA 9 7 ti'S</head>
        <p>DA 9 7 ti'S</p>
        <p>Hughes received a long pass from Hardy and opened the Institute scoring. The Unitarians were penned in their own half and were very seldom dangerous. The Institute continued to be*dangeraiiSly aggressive, and Hill had to deal with shots from Eaton and Oakes. The Uni- tarians relieved the pressure and good work by Harrison and Bailey took the ball into the Institute quarters, Where Knight had his first Shot to deal with, which he did in easy style. Hardy put the ball well out to the left, where Eaton received and transferred to Stubbs, who outwitted Hallam in comfortable fashion and scored with a fine shot. Shortly afterwards Emmanuel forced a corner, and this was nicely placed by William Eaton, but Hallam handled in the penalty area, and. Stubbs again did the needful. • Half-time arrived with Emmanuel leading by .three goals to nil. On resuming, it looked evident that Cairo- street would soon wipe off the arrears, as the wind had slightly increased. They opened with great dash, but Crozier and S. Hughes drove them back. The Institute backs were playing well and kept the opposing forwards !well in hand. Play became fast and more spirited than in the previous half. Eaton got 'in a well-judged centre, but Hill cleared luckily. The Institute forwards were playing well on the ;backs. Hallam and Hankinson being hard pressed. Harrison made one. din- gerous effort, which -ended in a corner and which was cleared. Knight, in the Institute goal, was having an easy time, whereas at the other end there was plenty to do. A corner fell to the Institute, this being well placed. T. Hughes had no difficulty in putting the ball through. Cairo-street warmed to their work, but, try as they would, they could make no impression" on the Institute *backs, who were proving far too good for them. Emmanuel set up a spirited attack and Stubbs neatly- pushed the ball past Hallam, and after beat- ify, him for possession again scored, leaving the goalkeeper helpless with fast low shot. The leaders were still having the best of the game. Cairo-street were Awarded a free-kick close in and Pilling was entrusted with it, but a fruitless corner was the result. Bailey 'put in a stinging shot, but it was cleared. When the whistle blew Emmanuel ran out easy winners by, five goals to nil, thus defeating the Unitar- ians for the second time this season. _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0273" n="art0273">
