      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0285" n="art0285">
        <head>PAROCHIAL TEA.</head>
        <p>PAROCHIAL TEA.</p>
        <p>Anglicans Spend An Enjoyable Evening.</p>
        <p>Every year the congregation of the War- rington Parish Church meets for the purpose of spending a few hours together socially on the occasion of the Parochial Tea. The event, however, includes much more than a tea, and on Thursday evening, when it was held at the Fairfield Schools, a ,whist drive, concert and dance followed.</p>
        <p>Its popularity is increasing year by year, and the accommodation at the Schools on Thursday was taxed to its uttermost. A unique and pleasing feature of Thursday night's gathering was that the Rev. Canog Willis the Rector) was accompanied by Mrs. Willis and his three sons—the Rev. Earle D. Willis (the Vicar of St. Stephen's, Burnley), Mr. Evelyn D. Willis, and Mr. Arthur 1). Willis.</p>
        <p>A hundred and eighty-seven guests sat down to tea. On its conclusion the Rector made a short speech, in which he appealed to the congregation for further support for the Curacy Fund, saying that about ..tl5O a year was necessary. He also referred to the altera- tion of the morning services, and hoped that the congregation would do its best to attend. In the evening the visitors had the choice of either playing at whist or listening to a con- cert, where considerable talent was displayed. Each drew a good number of supporters. Music, Song and Elocution.</p>
        <p>The musical programme was made up of a variety of good items. The Misses Fairclough opened with a pianoforte duet, which won much approval, and this was followed by the song "There's a Land," by Mrs. Sepliton. Master Neville Davies. of the Parish Church Choir, rendered the song entitled "The Pro- mise of Life," and Miss Hipkiss sang effec- tively " Carmena" and "Sincerity." Mr. and Mrs. Sephton were heard at their best in the duet "In Spring-time." Miss Reynolds gave two excellent solos—the " Bouree " (Arthur Somervell) and " Serenata " (Moszkowski)— on the violin, showing a skilful and thorough understanding of her instrument. The Brookln Quartet contributed a good item entitled "Come where my love lies sleeping," and Mr. Dutton's song, "Sentinel am I," was very popular. Mr. W. J. Hopkins was a great favourite with his humorous songs. "I followed her here" arid "I kept on walk- ing." As an encore for this last number Mr. Hopkins recited an essay on "Cats," supposed to have been written by a child. The programme concluded with a dialogue, in which the four parts were cleverly taken by members of the Students' Training College. It was entitled "A Terrible Journey," and its humorous points were finely brought out by the participants..</p>
        <p>At- the cOnelusion of the concert the Rector took the opportunity of presenting ambulance certificates to seven members of the Church Lads' Brigade. The recipients were :—Sergt.- Major S. C. Essenhigh (Ist class), Lance- Coiporal Starkey (3rd class), Private H. Bibby (3rd class), Private D. Green (3rd class), Private A. Bailey (3rd class)' Private H. San- son (3rd class). Private S. Holbrook (3rd class). Meanwhile the whist drive had been in pro- gress and was soon at an end. The prizes were won by the following :—Ladies: 1, Mrs. Wor- rall; 2, Mrs. George; gentlemen: 1, Mr. Robert Lee; 2. Mr. E. H. Coates. After the whist drive and concert, dancing was the pastime until midnight. Mr. Broad- hurst presided at the piano.</p>
        <p>For the function Mr. R. W. Antwiss was the secretary and Mr. R. T. Hardy the treasurer. Mr. G. W. Knott acted as M.C. for the whist and Mr. R. W. Antwiss for the dancing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0286" n="art0286">
        <head>LIGHTING ORDER PUZZLES A GOOD CITIZEN.</head>
        <p>LIGHTING ORDER PUZZLES A GOOD CITIZEN.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of " The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—When the lighting restrictions first came into operation 1. inquired, like Artemus Ward, " Why this thusness ?" The explanation was that the town was in darkness by order of the military authorities. We were at war and Zeppelins were expected, so, like a good citizen, I made the best of it, notwithstanding the fact that on looking towards town from a point on my way I observed there were plenty of lights still burning to guide any of these murderous aircraft to the good old town. The Electricity Works on the river side, for example, has always been, and still is, in my opinion, a splendid landmark. Then rumour said there was a shortage of coal and it was absolutely necessary from an economical standpoint to out the public thoroughfares in darkness. Again, like a duti- ful citizen, I made the best of it. Since the beginning of the year, however, the centre of the town has been illuminated at 1 a.m., and rumour again says this is due to the fact that experiments are being made at the Electricity Works. If this is so the times are not nearly so critical either from the standpoint of air raids or from the economical side of the que,- tion, as one is led to believe. This week the moon is at the full, and as some people have an idea that air raids are not made whilst the queen of the heavens sends forth her bea:ateous light, peradventure this may account for the strange innovation. On Tuesday, Wednesday and Thursday at 1 a.m. there were eight lights burning on Warrington Bridge alone, and two more in front of the "most interesting house in Warrington." If Aldermen Bennett and Smethurst, who have taken such a great interest in the bridge have not yet seen it illuminated, I should respect. fully advise them to do so. They will be amply repaid, for it certainly does look well. But seeing there is very little traffic at that time of the morning, if they could persuade the powers that be to turn some of them out and leave one of two more lights burning on the outskirts of the town they would earn the gratitude of</p>
        <p>A PUZZLED RATEPAYER.</p>
        <p>Selfishness mars the loveliest actions; it stains the fairest beauty; it dims the brightest lustre; it blotches the most magnificent charity.</p>
        <p>How He Came To Fail. "My poor man," said a kind woman, "how did you come to fail? You say you owned a condensed milk factory." "Ah, ma'am," responded the tramp, "it was the fault of my partner! He called the milk the 'Globe' brand." "And the name ruined it?" "Yes, ma'am. You see, the globe is two- thirds water !"</p>
        <p>Why He Knew. "Can you tell me," said a teacher to a lad who had seemed particularly ignorant, "whe- ther George Washington was a sailor or a soldier?" .</p>
        <p>The boy grinned. "He was a soldier," he said. "How do you know?" the teacher asked. "Because I saw a picture of him crossing the Delaware. Any sailor would know enough not to stand up in the boat!"</p>
      </div>
