      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0294" n="art0294">
        <p>Miss Lena Duckworth Marries Oswestry Gentleman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0295" n="art0295">
        <head>BRIDE.</head>
        <p>THE BRIDE.</p>
        <p>HULNIE--11E1A ITT.</p>
        <p>. . . •:.</p>
        <p>Photograph by Birtles.</p>
        <p>The bride, who entered. the church accom- panied by her father, was tastefully attired in a travelling dress of tete-de-niire and crepe-de- chine, with a hat to match. Her bouquet was of daffodils and mimosa, in the carrying of which could be distinguished her gold • and peridot bracelet, the gift of the bridegroom.</p>
        <p>She was attended by only one maid—Miss Hilda Mary Lunt—who also looked charming in a dress of pale blue crepolene, with hat to match. She carried a bouquet of pink carna- tions and wore a gold signet ring, the gift of the bridegroom. Mr. F. Marsden acted as best Man, and the Rev. A. J. Morton conducted the ceremony.</p>
        <p>The reception was held at the home Of the bride's father, and later in the course of the afternoon the. newly-married couple left for! Llandudno, where the honeymOon is being spent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0296" n="art0296">
        <head>BRIDE AND BRIDEGROOM.</head>
        <p>THE BRIDE AND BRIDEGROOM.</p>
        <p>A pretty wedding was solemnised at Bold. street Wesleyan Church on Wednesday, when Miss Mary Hewitt, daughter of Mr. and Mrs. Thomas Hewitt, The Maples, Longdin-street, Latchford, was married to Mr.. Alfred Hulme, soh of Mr. and Mrs. S. C. Hulme, of Smugbro', Douglas, 1.0. M. The Rev. Percy meson officiated. The bride, who was given away by Mr. Thos. Hewitt, looked charming in a ducbesse satin dress, trimmed with ninon and lace. She carried a. sheaf of lilies, and wore the crest of the Liverpool Regiment, to which her bro- ther belongs. The bridesmaids were Miss Nellie Green</p>
        <p>OVigan) and Miss Olive Tarbuck (St. Annes- on-Sea). The former wore a blue silk taffeta dress and carried a bouquet of pink carnations. Miss Tarbuck was attired in a duchesse satin dress, and also .carried a basket of pink carna- tions. She ivore a gold bangle, the gift of the bridegroom. The duties of best man were carried out by Mr. T. Hewitt. In the church, where Mr. F. Hickman pre- sided at the organ, the hymns ." The Voice that breathed o'er Eden" and "0 Perfect Love" were sung. The reception was held at the home of the bride. The bride's travel- ling dress was of blue cloth, covered .by a fur coat. Her black satin hat was underlined with pink. She also wore a set of natural fox furs.</p>
        <p>The wedding was a pretty one, and the ser- vice was choral, Mr. James Muir being at the organ. The bride was charmingly attired in a mole cloth costume, and wore a gold bangle, the gift of the bridegroom. She was given away by her uncle, Mr. W. Pemberton, and her brides maid was Miss Mary Smith, the sister of the bridegroom. 'Miss Smith wore a navy blue cos- tume.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Irvine Lister officiated. After the cerethony a reception was held at A #l-inson's Cafe. Bridge-street.</p>
        <p>The newly-married pair have received many handsome gifts, including a case of cutlery from the St. Helens Cable Company, where the bride is employed as head typist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0297" n="art0297">
        <head>FLYING MAN'S WEDDING AT UNITARIAN CHURCH.</head>
        <p>FLYING MAN'S WEDDING AT UNITARIAN CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The first khaki wedding to be celebrated at the Cairo-street Unitarian Chapel was that between Miss Florence Dawson, the second, daughter of Mrs. Pemberton, 54. St. Mary's-street, Latch- ford, and Mr. John Dixon Smith, of the Royal Flying Corps. The bridegroom is the second son of Mr. and Mrs. J. T. Smith, of 68, Bewsey-street, War- rington. At the time of his wedding he' was on his last leave before going to the Front, and arrived in Warrington only two days before the event.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0298" n="art0298">
        <head>CO-OPERATIVE SOCIETY PROFITS.</head>
        <p>CO-OPERATIVE SOCIETY PROFITS.</p>
        <p>Private Traders' Letter To Local M.P.'s. Mr. George H. Rogers, secretary of the War- rington and District Fruiterers', Florists' and Fishmongers' A ssmiation, sou&amp; us a copy of a letter he has sent to Mr. Harold Smith, ALP., Lord Wolmer, M.P., and Colonel Allan Sykes, M.P.:— •</p>
        <p>"I am directed by my Executive to 'communi- cate with you respecting' Co-operative So3ieties and the .payment of income tax, and respectively. to urge that you will give your support to any legislation which may be brought before Parlia- ment with a view t 9 placing these Societies on the same level as private traders, and pay, their fair share of taxation. - "I would request your kind consideration to, this matter, and would esteem it a favour to re- ceive your reply, together with your views re- lating to this subject.</p>
        <p>"Another important matter which is com- manding the attention and interest of this and other Federated Trades Societies is the workers and the War Loan Committee.</p>
        <p>"As probably you are already aware, the Government have appointed a committee to con- sider the question of obtaining contributions from the working classes to the War Loan. Upon this committee there appears to be no repre- sentative of private traders, though yet it eludes in its number the secretary, 91 the Par- liamentary Committee of the Co-operative Con- gress. "Taking into consideration the fact that Co- operative Societies were urging their members</p>
        <p>to put their money into their stores, instead of pushing National War Loan Investments, it would appear obviously unfair to appoint a representative of the Co-operative movement and omit a representative of the private traders. "This is a point which I beg le—a-ve to suggest might reasonably be brought before the notice of the Chancellor of the Exchequer, and the un- questionable necessity for a representative Of private traders to be appointed forthwith."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0299" n="art0299">
        <head>MR. SMITH'S REPLY.</head>
        <p>MR. SMITH'S REPLY.</p>
        <p>House of Commons, Jan. 18th.</p>
        <p>My Dear Sir,—Y-ia-V-e to acknowledge with thanks your letter of the 15th. It is quite ob- vious that there is much weight in the point which you put forward on behalf of your Asso- ciation, and you may rely upon my keeping the matter in mind in the event of an opportunity presenting itself for discussion.—Yours Very truly, (signed) HAROLD SMITH.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0300" n="art0300">
        <head>xtract From Letter From An Unconscientious</head>
        <p>xtract From Letter From An Unconscientious</p>
        <p>Slacker:</p>
        <p>"DEAR LORD KITCRENER,^I am not a, good walker, which prevents my joining the Infantry. As I have no experieno of horses, the Cavalry is also out of the question. The Artillery I don't care for on account of the noise, and flying makes me giddy. The A.S.C. does not appeal to me, and the R.A.M.C. would entail some vefy unpleasant duties. "So you had abetter not worry about me. Perhaps when tEe fine weather comes I may think about the Navy. I am rather keen on boating. • • ."—" Punch."</p>
        <p>immmg</p>
        <p>mow</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0301" n="art0301">
        <head>BRIDEGROOM.</head>
        <p>THE BRIDEGROOM.</p>
        <p>In the presence of a large congregation in Padgate Wesleyan Church on Tuesday after- noon, Miss Lena Duckworth, who is highly es- teemed in local social and musical circles, was married to Mr. Ernest Powell, youngest son of Mrs. J.' Powell and the late Mr. J. Powell, of "Granville House," Oswestry. The bride is the only daughter of Mr. and Mrs. T. B. Duck- worth, of Westholm, Padgate.</p>
        <p>Owing to the war it had been designed that the marriage ceremony should be of the quietest character, and only relatives and the closest friends of the family were invited, but .many well-wishers assembled.</p>
        <p>Two hymns—" The Voice that Breathed o'er Eden" and "0 Perfect Lore "—were sung, and Mr. W. H. Ward discoursed pleasantly on the organ, his selections being "Salut D'Amour" (Elgar), "Hymne Celeste" (Grey), and Men- delssohn and Guilmant's Wedding Marches.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0302" n="art0302">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0303" n="art0303">
        <head>LOCAL AMUSEMENTS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL AMUSEMENTS.</p>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>"The Confessions Of A Wife."</p>
        <p>The play presented by Mr. Joseph Millane's company this week at the Royal Court Theatre is a well-constructed one and', to the majority of patrons, is a pleasing one, for its human touch appeals very strongly to the audience. The story is an old one, touching upon the dis- illusionment of a too-trusting wife and of a mis- taken marriage. • In the play there is a detective element, which goes a long way to make the piece a thoroughly interesting and enjoyable entertainment. Mr. F. C. Bailey, as the pro- fligate husband, is splendid, particularly in the fifth scene, where, in brutal fashion he thrashes his own child—an episode which brings storms of disapproval, especially from pit and gallery Miss Mabel Rose, as the unhappy wife, rende a difficult part in capital style, infusing plenty of emotion into her acting. Miss Dolly Maude, as her son, is very promising, and acquits her. self very creditably. Mr. John S. Millward, not unknown to Warrington audiefices, is. an ideal lover, and portrays his part in worthy style. Mr. William Calvert, as the sleuth-hound, is very good, as also is the remander of the Ccii, pany. The whole action of-Abe play takes piac.e in one building, viz., The Belvedere Meniions, London, the scenery being entirely new az n up-to-date. Rollicking "Panto" Next Week.</p>
        <p>"Goody Two Shoes," the production of Messrs. Harry Russell and Levaine, is due to occupy the boards at the Royal Court Theatre next week, and, from the bill, one expects to witness a good show. We believe it is many years since the pantomime entitled "Goody Two Shoes" visited the town, so the fare pro- vided should be refreshing and new. All the latest songs will be sung and many specialities will be introduced, including Talberto and Douglas, eccentric equilibrists; the Regina Troupe of Dancers; Rita 3foya, dainty soubrette and dancer; and Harry Levaine. in a new historical burlesque entitled "Josephine." Special scenery and effects, and a bevy of beautiful ladies, should make the entertain- ment worth visiting. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0304" n="art0304">
        <head>HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>Consideration of the fact that they are refugees is unnecessary in recommending the Belgian Sextet. One has known instances where it was essential that this sentimental circumstance should be kept in the forefront if the performance were to be appreciated. The musical abilities of these lady and gentlemen are of surpassing merit, however. Displaying full compliance with the rules of English pro- nunciation, the lady sings sweetly and effectively in the mezzo-soprano register; one 'gentleman renders a grand opera solo in mellow baritone in his native language, and. with the lady, then joins in a duet in which the English lines are repeated so often (and effectively, too) as to admit of no excuse should he forget them; three gentlemen play violins most excellently; and completing the company is A pianist, who looks as if he has spent most of his time in Paris. His little beard is pointed perfectly, and his pianistic touch is vivacious and refined. All the numbers they submit are of classic quality.</p>
        <p>Astonishingly different, but equally good in its way, is the Jewish comedy, "The Meanest Man on Earth." Staged in a tailor's shop (like that in "Potash and Perlmutter" a deal of comedy is extracted from the deprecating re- marks of a mean man against his dead partner, whom he considers "the meanest man on earth." Mr. Naylor Grimsoh, who has achieved distinction in his presentation of Jewish charic- ters, Lakes the main part. Abel and Welsh are eccentrics of extraordin- ary contortionist and acrobatic persuasion; one of the Soho Trio sings grandly and the.other two dance daringly and uniquely; and Frou-Frou is a •French dog who understands English as a party to the entente should. Cosmopolitan Fare Next Week.</p>
        <p>Judging from the attractions due to appear at the Hippodrome during the forthcoming week, the most fastidious of patrons should be amply satisfied, for the management have secured a very varied programme. We are to have a return visit of Gillin, a ventriloquist who has previously delighted the habitues of this variety house. Other capital attractions are The Diving Belles, in a wonderful water illusion; the Bell. Bros. in versatile gymnastic novelties; Frank Dickenson, the Yorkshire basso profundo; Royce, Babs and Royce, in novel singing and dancing act; Jim Kenny and Emmie Astin, the coloured comedian and won- derful pianist; and Patti Loftus, the dainty comedienne and dancer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0305" n="art0305">
        <head>STAR KINEMA.</head>
        <p>THE STAR KINEMA.</p>
        <p>Another good programme was presented at the popular Star Kinema House during the first half of this week. Sir Arthur Pinero's play " Sweet Lavender," was 'shown, Henry Ainley being the leading actor. The tenth episode of that magnificent picture, "The, Exploits of Elaine," was screened, as also was an instruc- tive picture depicting the Norwegian Air Ser- vice. " Pathes' War Gazette" was as interest- ing as ever, while "Dances of the Ages" was, perhaps, chiefly enjoyed by the ladies. "A Day on the Force," a comedy featuring Billy Reeves, was a rare "screamer." For the latter end of the week, premier position is held by "The Moth and the Flame." Fine Films Next Week.</p>
        <p>A couple of fine films are promised for next week. The first three , days, " Stoleit Goods" will be showing, as well as other good films. " Infelice " is due for the later part of the week, and the popularity of the book, which it has been dramatised from, bespeaks for it large and attentive audiences.</p>
        <p>Among future engagements for the Star are "The Rosary," adapted from the play, and featuring Kathlyn Williams, and Hall Caine's "The Eternal City," produced by the famous Players Company. These extra special attrac- tions will appear in succeeding weeks, "The Rosary," commencing January 31st, and "The Eternal City," commencing February 10th. By an unfortunate error in last week's "Examiner" we made it appear .that these films were a com- bined show.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0306" n="art0306">
        <head>GEM PICTURE lIOUSE.</head>
        <p>THE GEM PICTURE lIOUSE.</p>
        <p>The programme presented at the Gem Picture House, Winmarleigh-street, during the first three days of the week was a fine one. " Vaga, bond Soldier," a long drama, headed the pro- gramme, and those who watched the extra- ordinary career of this gentleman had an ex- citing time. f` Caught in the Act," a Keystone comedy, was much enjoyed, while another laugh- able picture in two parts, viz., "The Boob's Romance," brought a fine programme to an end. For the latter three days of the week the " star " picture is entitled "Abduction."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0307" n="art0307">
        <head>Follow the Gem.</head>
        <p>Follow the Gem.</p>
        <p>The principal picture during the first Part of the week at the Gem Picture House is to be "The Young Ironmaster," a drama which should go well in an industrial district; and during the second, "Phantom of the Violin," a classical production of quality.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0308" n="art0308">
        <head>CONFLICTING EVIDENCE.</head>
        <p>CONFLICTING EVIDENCE.</p>
        <p>Very conflicting evidence was heard at the Borough Police Court on Friday morning, when Mrs. Emily Swift, of 60, Haydock-street, applied for a separation order from her husband on the grounds that he was an habitual drunkard. Mrs. Swift said she married the defendant, who denied that he was a drunkard, fifteen months ago. She was a widow and he a widower, and each had two daughters. He was all right for the first four months, but since then he had been drunk every week-end. The Magistrates' Clerk (Mr. G. White) read a letter from the defendant's employers, stating that he had been in their employ for twenty years. He was steady and reliable, and his, general conduct was satisfactory. Alice Swift, a daughter of the defendant, stated that she had never seen her father the worse for drink.</p>
        <p>Another of the defendant's daughters main- tained that her .father was not a drunkard.</p>
        <p>The Mayor (who was presiding), said the Bench did not think that it had been proved that the defendant was an habitual drunkard, and, therefore, the case would be di,smissed. Mr. Steel intimated that he would apply for another summons on the ground of desertion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0309" n="art0309">
