      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0310" n="art0310">
        <p>El</p>
        <p>:OMMERCIAL TRAVELLERS' RECORD.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0311" n="art0311">
        <head>Clean Balance Sheet.</head>
        <p>A Clean Balance Sheet.</p>
        <p>The annual general meeting of the Warrington, and District Branch of the United Kingdom Commercial Travellers' Association was held last Saturday at the Lion Hotel, Mr. W. P. Ashton presiding over a large attendance of.' members, which included Mr. H. Walsh (vice- chairman), Mr. T. Percival (hon. sec.), and Mr. W. L. Walker (hon. assistant sec.) Mr. Percival, in presenting the annual report,. gave a brief resume of the work done during the year, and spoke of the excellent manner in which the meetings of, the executive had been attended. He also made a reference to the Bene- fit Society, and said he thought Many of the members were unaware of what a really good thing this was, or more would take it up. Mr. W. P. Ashton presented the balance sheet, and said that every member had paid his subscription for the year, which, he thought. was a record not only for Warrington, but for • every branch in the country. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>Both reports were, adopted, and the officials heartily thanked for their services.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. Peter Rylands, having signified his assent, was unanimously re-elected president, much to the satisfaction of all the members.</p>
        <p>The following Vice-presidents were re-elected: The Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock), Sir Arthur H. Crosfield. Bart., Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., Colonel J. Reid Walker and Messrs. H. R. Armitage. Arthur E. Boardman. P. Cald- well, John Dolan. F. A. Frost, J. Fulton-Smith, Henry Greenall, John Lee. James T. Locker, W. Milner, John A. Ransome, W. Sloane and W. Webster. •</p>
        <p>The following Hon. Viee-preqidents were re- elected :—Messrs. A. J. Allen, W. G. Hayes, J. T. Knowles. Harold Mercer, P. O'Brien, H. L. Taylor, W. L. Walker and j. E. Warren.</p>
        <p>A Three-office Official.</p>
        <p>Mr. H. Walsh. who was unanimously elected chairman, on taking the chair presented to Mr. Ashton a gold medal as a memento of his year of office in a triple capacity. On one side of the medal was the monogram " and on the other side the inscription:—" Pre- sented to Mr. W. P. Ashton, chairman, treasurer and chairman Benefit Society, War- rington Branch, 19M." He also proposed that his name be added to the list of lion. vice-presi- dents. This was carried with applause.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. H. Owen was electecl.iice-chairman, Mr. W. P. Ashton was re-elected bon. treasurer, and Mr. T. Percival hon. sec.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. L. Walker, wishing to relinquish the office of hon. assistant sec., which he has held' for a number of years, Mr. A. 0. Ililditch was elected in his nlaee. •</p>
        <p>Mr. T. S. Steel was unanimously se-elected' hon. solicitor.</p>
        <p>The following were elected to form the' executive for the year:—Messrs. A. J. Allen, G. Box. W. Dakin. W. G. Haves, C. Hemblett, A.. O. Hilditch, R. Lunt, N. Maguire. H. Mercer, P. O'Brien. D. J. Price, N. L. Taylor. J. W. C: Taylor, W. L. Walker and J. A. Warburton. The Warrington Cot.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. G. Hayes. who is secretary for th" 6 fund to. provide a "Warrington Cot" in the new dormitories at Pinner Schools, gave a re- port on the progress made. He said he had just received a cheque from Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Ltd., for 10 guineas— (applause)—which brought the total amount subscribed to date to about £4O towards the 100 guineas required. The object was a most worthy one, and he appealed to' all members who intended to subscribe to do so as early as pos- sible. It was decided to hold a whist drive early next month in aid of this fund, and a ladies' committee was formed to help in the matter.</p>
        <p>It was decided to hold the meetings in'future on the second Saturday in the month instead, of the third. `</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0312" n="art0312">
        <head>BILLIARDS.</head>
        <p>BILLIARDS.</p>
        <p>ADULT SCHOOLS' BILLIARD</p>
        <p>LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>P. W. L. F. A. Pts. Stockton Heath A.S. 4 2 2 1199 1454 2`• Sankey A.S. Longford A. 5.4 2 2 1659 1730 4 Bewsey A. 5.3 1 2 1277 1286 2 Two points for a win.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-22_WidnesExaminer_art0313" n="art0313">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH v. BEWSEY.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH v. BEWSEY.</p>
        <p>An interesting match was played at Stockton, Heath Adult School on Thursday evening with the Bewsey Adult School members. Result:— Bewsey : H. Perks 57, R.,Hehley 75, J. Mather 75, G. Boyle 70, G. Stockton 74, C. Plinston 75; total 426.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath: J. Greenwood 75„1. Norbury 60, W. Hankey 74, W. Mann 75, A. Albinson 75, F. Lynch 56; total 415. Majority of 11 for Bewsey. Referee, R. Williams, Sankey.</p>
        <p>Printed and published by John Walker and Co. (Warrington), Limited, at the. EXAMINES General Printing Works, Bewsey-street, War- rington ;• also published by them at the EXAMINER Offices, 2, Victoria-road, Widnes; 32, George-street, St. Helens; 3, Market-street. Earlestown, all in the County of Lancaster; and at Church-street, Runcorn, in the County of Chester.</p>
      </div>
