        <p>Another Record Broken. OUR WINTER SALE IS A BIGGER SUCCESS THAN EVER. Although hundreds of our Customers are on Active Service, thousands still at home are availing themselves of our exceptional offer. A FIRST-CLASS SUIT or OVERCOAT FOR 16/11 No one who appreciates value should miss this chance. Only a little while longer. ORDER NOV. Absolute Satisfaction Guaranteed. PATTERNS WITH PLEASURE. SEE WINDOWS. KELLETT' S Successors Ltd., 29, BRIDGE STREET, WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER SATURDAY, JANUARY 22, 1916.</p>
        <p>Notices of births, marriages, and deaths; in memoriam notices, and acknowledgments of sympathy are inserted in "The Examiner" at the following rates :—Not exceeding 20 words, 1/6 each; every additional .seven words, 6d.</p>
        <p>Marriages. PbWELL—DUCKWORTH.—On the 18th inst., at Padgate Wesleyan Chapel, by the Rev. A. J. Morton, Ernest Powell, son of Mrs. J. Powell and the late Mr. 3. Powell, of Oswestry, to Lena. daughter of Mr. and Mrs. T. B. Duckworth, of Westholm, Padgate. SMITH—DAWSON.—On the 13th inst., at Cairo- street Unitarian Chapel, by the Rev. Irvine Lister, John Dixon Smith, second son of Mr. and Mrs. J. T. Smith, of 68, Bewsey-street, Warrington, to Florence Dawson, second daughter of Mrs. Pem- berton, of 64, St. Mary's'-street, Latchford.</p>
        <p>Deaths. BARER.--January 16th, at 43, , Walker-street, Minnie Baker, aged 17 years. BURNS.—January 13th, at 12, Cambria-place, Char- lotte Burns, aged 28 years. CONNOR.—January 15th, at 21, Portland-street, Edward Connor, aged 50 years. CORCORAN.—January 14th, at 16, Naylor-street, Sarah Corcoran, aged 79 years.</p>
        <p>In Memoriam. TAYLOR.—In loving memory of Rose Hannah Taylor, died January 18th, 1915. "We miss her most who loved her best." SONS and DAUGHTERS. INIUNERALS BY J. MOUNFIELD &amp; SONS. 1. The Best in all Grades. Office: 15, Church- street. Tel 99.</p>
        <p>FOR WHOM DO THE TRADES COUNCIL SPEAK?</p>
        <p>SIR ARTHUR CROSFIELD'SI PROMOTIONS AND DISTINCTIONS. MADE LIEUTENANT-COLONEL. Promotion For Captain A. B. Layton. Captain A. B. Layton has been appointed a temporary Lieutenant-Colonel, and is to have command of the 314th South Lancs. Regiment. Although not a native of Warrington, Lieut.- Colonel Layton has been connected with the local Territorials for nearly twelve years, having joined them on the 17th September, 1904. He is a popular officer and went to the Front with the battalion in February of last year, and there proved himself to be highly efficient in his work. In the famous charge at Hooge on June 16th he was 'wounded and, on earning to England, spent a considerable time in the Royal Military Hospital at Woolwich. He is a bachelor and his home address is 32, Whitefield- road, Stockton Heath. Before war broke out Lieut.-Colonel Layton took an active interest in the commercial life of the town. He came to Messrs. Joseph Cros- field and Sans Ltd. twelve years ago as electrical engineer and departmental manager, previous to which he was employed by Messrs. Armstrong, Whitworth and Co.,,Ltd. In Lieut.-Colonel Layton the 3/4th South</p>
