        <p>SERBIA'S NEED. Help Required For A Distressed Population: To avoid any possibility of confusion in the administration of contributions intended for the benefit of the distressed Serbian population, the Serbian Legation request that subscriptions intended for the Serbian ReBO Fund, of which H.•. 31. the ,Queen is patroness, should be sent to the Earl of Desart, K.C.8., at the offices of the fund, 5, Cromwell-road, South Kensington, S.W. The Serbian Legation will continue to receive subscriptions for the following Serbian funds:- I.—The Archbishop of Belgrade's fund for the families of the killed and wounded soldiers. 2.—The Society of the Serbian Red Cross. 3.—The Parliamentary Commission for the Refugees. 4.—The Society of St. Helena for the orphans whose parents have been killed in the war. All contributions addressed to the Serbian Legation, 195, Queen's Gate, S.W., London, for these funds, will be gratefully received and acknowledged.</p>
        <p>The above, photograph, taken on .Christmas Eve on board H.M.S. , shows the extent to which Warrington and its'neighbour- h'ood is doing its duty on sea. So many local "boys," are in the "E" stokehold of this single vessel that they have been christened the "South Lancashire Regiment." We regret that our cheery correspondent has omitted to state the names of the men in their order; but it is a way they have in the Navy to look upon' themselves as one combined whole. The words on the board in front of this united.team are:— " Stilr waiting for 'Bill.'" " E ' stokehold; Ist steaming watch; H.M.S. "Christmas Eve, 1915. Are we downhearted? No." A ROUGH NEW YEAR'S DAY. The letter containing the photograph came to hand early this week 'and was as follows Stoker J. E. O'Rourke, 14 Mess, H.M.S. , c/o G.P.O, London. january 4th, 1916. Dear Editor,—l suppose you will be surprised • -</p>
