        <p>VICTORY THIS YEAR. Premier's Message To Russia. The " Russkoe Slovo" last Friday gave pro- minence to messages from leading personages in the Allied States in response to the newspaper's expression of -a hope that peace would be ,de- clared only on the complete triumph of -the 'Allied-cringe. 'All' the replies. pay a' tribiate- to the heroic efforts and sacrifices of the Russian Army and people. Mr. Asquith avows confidence that "the year 1916 will witness the consummation of the hopes of the Allies in the final overthrow of the common enemy." Sir Douglas Haig _replies on behalf of all ranks of the British Army. Mr. Rudyard Kipling writes:—" Like you, I look for peace, but not ligfore our common work .is done and a Power built up on afro- gance and disciplined in evil has been taught that there is a God other than its own lugts. We who have borne our part as your Allies, and will bear it to the end, will never forget with. what 'inflexible fortitude and sacrifice Russia has laid the foundations of that day of full reckoning." LORD KITCH,ENER'S CONFIDENCE. Among many congratulatory telegrams re- ceived by General Polivanoff, Minister of War, on the occasion of the Russian New Year, is one from Lord Kitchener, who says : "Please accept my sincerest good wishes for the coming year, which will, I pray God, result in complete victory and lasting peace." • To this th - Minister of War replied "Deeply touched by your kind words. I wish to assert my strong faith that in the New Year God will bless the Allied armies, and send us complete victory and glorious peace."</p>
